window.SAFETY_DATA = {"generated": "2026-10-24", "divisions": [{"code": "electric", "name": "Electric Services"}, {"code": "environmental", "name": "Environmental Services"}, {"code": "public", "name": "Public Services"}], "courses": [{"slug": "fall-protection-general", "title": "Fall Protection: General Requirements", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "standard_title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 1, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Falls are the leading cause of death in construction, and fall protection is the most frequently cited OSHA standard year after year. This course explains when fall protection is required on public works jobs, what hazards trigger it, how guardrails, safety nets and personal fall arrest systems work, and what has to be in place before anyone ties off. It applies to every division because every division has crews working on edges, roofs, tanks, structures and equipment.", "objectives": ["Identify the height triggers for fall protection in construction work (6 feet) and general industry work (4 feet) and tell which one applies to a task.", "Recognize the fall hazards named in 1926.501 that show up on public works jobs, including open edges, leading edges, holes, excavations, roofs, tanks and structures.", "Describe the three main fall protection systems and explain why guardrails are the first choice.", "List the components of a personal fall arrest system and state the limits on anchorage strength, free fall distance and arresting force.", "Inspect fall arrest equipment before each use and know when a component must be removed from service.", "Explain swing fall and why a rescue plan must be in place before anyone ties off."], "sections": [{"heading": "When fall protection is required", "html": "<p>OSHA's construction standard, <strong>29 CFR 1926.501</strong>, requires fall protection whenever an employee works on a surface with an unprotected side or edge <strong>6 feet or more</strong> above a lower level. The employer must first confirm the surface can hold the workers, then provide a guardrail system, a safety net system or a personal fall arrest system (PFAS).</p>\n<p>Public works crews also do <strong>general industry</strong> work covered by <strong>29 CFR 1910.28</strong> (walking-working surfaces), which uses a <strong>4 foot</strong> trigger. The task decides which rule applies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Building, altering, repairing or demolishing a structure, such as replacing a culvert headwall or re-roofing a pump house, is construction. The 6 foot trigger applies.</li>\n<li>Routine maintenance and operations, such as inspecting a clarifier walkway or changing a light on the shop mezzanine, is general industry. The 4 foot trigger applies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When in doubt, use the stricter 4 foot trigger. Above <strong>dangerous equipment</strong>, such as an open tank or the auger in a salt spreader, guards are required at any height.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Fall protection has held the top spot on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards for more than a decade. Inspectors cite 1926.501 so often because the hazard is easy to see and easy to prove. A worker on an edge with no guardrail and no harness is a citation on sight.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(b)(13) Residential construction.</strong> Workers on roofs and floor decks with no protection at all.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(1) Unprotected sides and edges.</strong> Open edges of floors, decks, platforms and loading areas.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(10) and (b)(11) Roofing work.</strong> Low-slope and steep roofs with no system in place.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(4) Holes.</strong> Uncovered floor holes and unguarded skylights.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common thread is not missing equipment. Most crews own harnesses and guardrail parts. The gear stayed in the truck because the job was 'only going to take a minute.' Short tasks are where most of our exposure is, and where the citations come from.</p>"}, {"heading": "Fall hazards you will meet in public works", "html": "<p>These are the situations in the standard that show up on municipal jobs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unprotected sides and edges.</strong> Any open edge 6 feet or more above the next level: a bridge deck under repair, a retaining wall top or a lift station roof.</li>\n<li><strong>Leading edges.</strong> An edge that moves as work advances, such as deck forms on a culvert or bridge. If guardrails, nets and PFAS are truly infeasible, a written fall protection plan under 1926.502(k) is required. Inconvenient does not mean infeasible.</li>\n<li><strong>Holes and skylights.</strong> Any hole more than 6 feet above a lower level needs a <strong>cover</strong>, a guardrail or PFAS. Covers must hold twice the expected load, be secured so they cannot slide, and be marked HOLE or COVER. A skylight, an open tank or vault hatch, and the gap left when a grate is pulled are all holes.</li>\n<li><strong>Excavations.</strong> Edges 6 feet or more deep need guardrails, fences or barricades when the edge is hidden from view. Wells, pits and shafts that deep need guardrails, barricades or covers.</li>\n<li><strong>Roofs.</strong> Low-slope roofs allow guardrails, nets, PFAS, or a warning line combined with one of those. Steep roofs require guardrails with toeboards, nets or PFAS.</li>\n<li><strong>Bridges, structures and tanks.</strong> Water tower catwalks, clarifier bridges and digester tops have fixed railings for a reason. When a railing comes off for a repair, replacement protection goes in first.</li>\n<li><strong>Poles and bucket trucks.</strong> Pole work and aerial lift work have their own rules under 29 CFR 1910.269, 1926 Subpart V and 1926.453. Tie off in the bucket every time, and use fall protection whenever you climb or change position on a pole.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "The three main systems", "html": "<p>The standard names three conventional systems.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Guardrail systems</strong> are the first choice because they protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker. The top rail sits <strong>42 inches, plus or minus 3 inches</strong>, above the walking surface and must withstand a 200 pound force. A midrail goes about halfway up. Add toeboards when people work below.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety net systems</strong> catch a worker after a fall. Nets go as close as practical under the work surface and never more than 30 feet below it. They are rare in public works.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal fall arrest systems</strong> stop a fall that has already started. They depend on correct equipment, a sound anchor and a trained worker, and fit short tasks where a guardrail cannot be built.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>warning line</strong> keeps workers back from the edge on low-slope roofs, and a <strong>controlled access zone</strong> limits who can enter an area where conventional protection is not in use. Both have strict conditions and are used only under a competent person.</p>\n<p>Remember the order of preference. First, remove the exposure by working from the ground or a lift platform with rails. Second, guard the edge. Third, rely on personal equipment.</p>"}, {"heading": "Personal fall arrest systems: components and limits", "html": "<p>A PFAS has three parts, often called the ABCs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A, Anchorage.</strong> The anchor must hold at least <strong>5,000 pounds per worker</strong> attached, or be designed, installed and used under the supervision of a <strong>qualified person</strong> as part of a complete system with a safety factor of at least two. A guardrail, a conduit run or a vent pipe is not an anchor.</li>\n<li><strong>B, Body support.</strong> Only a <strong>full body harness</strong> is allowed. Body belts are not accepted for fall arrest.</li>\n<li><strong>C, Connectors.</strong> A <strong>lanyard</strong> or self-retracting lifeline connects the harness to the anchor. Lanyards need a 5,000 pound breaking strength, and snaphooks must be self-locking. A <strong>deceleration device</strong>, such as a shock-absorbing lanyard or self-retracting lifeline, limits the force on your body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The system as a whole must meet these limits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Free fall of no more than 6 feet</strong>, and the worker must not contact a lower level.</li>\n<li>Maximum arresting force of <strong>1,800 pounds</strong> with a full body harness.</li>\n<li>Deceleration distance of <strong>no more than 3.5 feet</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Add it up before you tie off. A 6 foot lanyard, 3.5 feet of deceleration, the worker's height below the D-ring and a safety margin can mean more than 17 feet of clearance below the anchor. If the next level is closer, use a self-retracting lifeline anchored overhead or a different system.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection, swing fall and rescue", "html": "<p><strong>Inspect before each use.</strong> Check webbing for cuts, burns, frays, chemical damage and sun fading. Check stitching, D-rings, buckles and snaphook gates. Check the shock pack on a lanyard for a deployed indicator and a self-retracting lifeline for smooth retraction and a working brake. Any system that has caught a fall comes out of service until a competent person inspects it and clears it as undamaged. Anything defective gets tagged and removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Swing fall.</strong> If you anchor off to the side and fall, you swing like a pendulum back under the anchor. You can hit a wall or structure with full force, and the swing adds fall distance. Anchor as close to directly overhead as you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Rescue.</strong> The employer must provide for <strong>prompt rescue</strong> or make sure workers can rescue themselves. Hanging in a harness restricts blood flow in the legs, and a person can pass out in a short time. Before anyone ties off, the crew must know how a fallen worker will get down: a bucket truck on site, a rescue kit with a descent device, or a ladder that reaches the worker. Calling 911 and waiting is not a rescue plan. Never work tied off alone.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Substation steel, transformer tops and capacitor racks put technicians above 4 feet during maintenance and above 6 feet on construction projects. In a bucket, attach the lanyard to the anchor on the boom or basket, never to the bucket lip. Meter technicians on commercial rooftops face unguarded edges and skylights; treat them like any other roof.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants are full of open tanks, hatches, clarifier bridges and digester roofs, and a fall into a tank is also a drowning hazard. Pulling a grate or railing to lift a pump creates a hole; cover it or guard it right away. Solid waste crews face fall hazards at the transfer station tipping floor and on top of roll-off containers.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Bridge deck repairs, culvert headwalls and retaining walls are construction work with the 6 foot trigger. Signal technicians tie off in the bucket every time. Facilities staff on flat roofs need a warning line, guardrail or PFAS. Forestry crews in aerial lifts tie off, and climbing arborists follow ANSI Z133. Fleet mechanics on top of plow trucks and tanker bodies are above 4 feet; use the shop's fall arrest rail or a platform with rails.</p>\n<p>In every division, ask the same question: what will stop me if I slip right now? If the answer is nothing, stop and set up protection.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Construction work needs fall protection at 6 feet, general industry work needs it at 4 feet, and public works crews do both kinds of work.", "Guardrails are the first choice because they protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker.", "A hole is anything you can fall through, including skylights, open hatches and the gap left when a grate is pulled, and it needs a cover, a guardrail or PFAS.", "Anchor points must hold 5,000 pounds per worker or be engineered by a qualified person with a safety factor of two.", "Only a full body harness is allowed in a fall arrest system, free fall is limited to 6 feet, and the worker must never contact a lower level.", "Inspect every component before each use and remove anything that has caught a fall or shows damage.", "Anchor overhead to limit swing fall, and have a rescue plan in place before anyone ties off."], "questions": [{"text": "Under the construction standard 1926.501, at what height above a lower level is fall protection required at an unprotected edge?", "options": ["4 feet", "10 feet", "6 feet", "8 feet"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The construction trigger in 1926.501 is 6 feet. The 4 foot figure is the general industry trigger under 1910.28."}, {"text": "A facilities worker is doing routine maintenance on a shop mezzanine with an open edge. Which height trigger applies?", "options": ["4 feet, because routine maintenance is general industry work under 1910.28", "6 feet, because all public works field work is construction", "10 feet, because mezzanines are storage areas", "No trigger applies to indoor work"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Routine maintenance and operations are general industry work, so the 4 foot trigger in 1910.28 applies. The 6 foot trigger is for construction tasks such as building, altering or repairing a structure."}, {"text": "According to the course, what is the most common reason crews get cited under 1926.501?", "options": ["They do not own guardrail components", "Harnesses are too expensive for small departments", "Inspectors cannot tell construction from maintenance", "Equipment stays in the truck for tasks that will 'only take a minute'"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Most crews own the equipment. Citations happen when it is not used, usually on short tasks that the crew expected to finish quickly."}, {"text": "Which statement about hole covers is correct?", "options": ["Covers only need to hold the weight of one worker", "Covers must hold twice the expected load, be secured against sliding, and be marked HOLE or COVER", "A piece of plywood laid over the opening is enough if everyone on the crew knows it is there", "Skylights do not count as holes because they have glass or plastic panels"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Covers must support at least twice the load that could be placed on them, be secured so they cannot be displaced, and be marked so people know what they are. A skylight is treated as a hole."}, {"text": "Why are guardrail systems the preferred fall protection system?", "options": ["They protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker", "They are the cheapest system to buy", "They are the only system allowed on roofs", "They do not require a competent person"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A guardrail is a passive system. It works for every person who walks near the edge, whether or not they remembered to clip in."}, {"text": "What strength must a fall arrest anchorage have if it has not been engineered by a qualified person?", "options": ["1,800 pounds per worker", "3,000 pounds per worker", "5,000 pounds per worker", "10,000 pounds total"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An anchorage must support at least 5,000 pounds per worker attached, unless it is designed and used under a qualified person as part of a system with a safety factor of at least two. The 1,800 pound figure is the limit on arresting force."}, {"text": "What is the maximum free fall distance allowed for a personal fall arrest system?", "options": ["3.5 feet", "6 feet", "10 feet", "12 feet"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A PFAS must be rigged so the worker cannot free fall more than 6 feet or contact a lower level. The 3.5 foot figure is the maximum deceleration distance, not the free fall limit."}, {"text": "What should happen to a lanyard that has arrested a fall?", "options": ["Return it to service if the webbing looks fine", "Keep using it until the shock pack is fully deployed", "Use it only for positioning from now on", "Remove it from service until a competent person inspects it and determines it is undamaged"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Any PFAS component subjected to impact loading must be removed from service immediately and may not be used again unless a competent person inspects it and finds it undamaged."}, {"text": "What is the best way to reduce the swing fall hazard?", "options": ["Use a longer lanyard so the swing is slower", "Tie off to a guardrail near the edge", "Anchor as close to directly overhead as possible", "Wear a body belt instead of a harness"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Swing fall happens when the anchor is off to the side. Anchoring directly overhead keeps the worker from swinging into walls or structure and keeps the fall distance shorter."}, {"text": "Which of these meets the standard's requirement to provide for prompt rescue?", "options": ["Calling 911 after a fall and waiting for help", "Having a bucket truck, rescue kit or ladder on site with a crew that knows how to use it before anyone ties off", "Instructing the fallen worker to unclip and climb down", "Working alone so no one else is exposed"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The employer must provide for prompt rescue or make sure workers can rescue themselves. That means the method and equipment are in place and the crew knows the plan before the work starts."}]}, {"slug": "hazard-communication", "title": "Hazard Communication (HazCom)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "standard_title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 2, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Hazard Communication, 29 CFR 1910.1200, gives every employee the right to know the hazards of the chemicals they work with and how to protect themselves. This course covers the written program, chemical inventory, Safety Data Sheets, GHS labels and pictograms, secondary container labeling and training requirements, with examples drawn from water and wastewater plants, fleet shops, streets, forestry and electric operations. It also explains the gaps that most often lead to HazCom citations and how crews can close them.", "objectives": ["Explain the employee right to know and the five working parts of a hazard communication program.", "Locate and read the key sections of a Safety Data Sheet for hazard, first aid, handling and PPE information.", "Identify the six elements of a GHS label and the meaning of each of the nine pictograms.", "Apply workplace labeling rules to secondary and portable containers.", "Recognize the hazardous chemicals common to your division and where to find their SDSs.", "Describe when HazCom training is required and the most common reasons for citations."], "sections": [{"heading": "Your right to know", "html": "<p>The Hazard Communication standard, often called <strong>HazCom</strong> or the <strong>Right to Know</strong> rule, is built on a simple idea: you have the right to know what chemicals you work with, what harm they can cause, and how to protect yourself. Manufacturers and importers must classify the hazards of their products and pass that information along through <strong>labels</strong> and <strong>Safety Data Sheets (SDS)</strong>. Your employer must then pass it on to you. HazCom covers any hazardous chemical you could be exposed to at work or in a foreseeable emergency.</p>\n<p>The standard has five working parts:<br />\n- A written hazard communication program<br />\n- A chemical inventory<br />\n- Labels and other warnings<br />\n- Safety Data Sheets<br />\n- Employee training</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>HazCom sits near the top of OSHA's most frequently cited standards list year after year. The rule is simple. The paperwork and daily habits it requires slip when crews are busy.</p>\n<p>The citations inspectors write most often fall into four groups:<br />\n- <strong>No written program.</strong> The department cannot produce one, or it has a generic template that never says who is responsible locally.<br />\n- <strong>No SDS available.</strong> A chemical is in use but its SDS cannot be found, or it is locked in an office the night shift cannot reach.<br />\n- <strong>No training, or no record of it.</strong> Employees cannot say what a pictogram means or where the SDS file is.<br />\n- <strong>Unlabeled containers.</strong> Spray bottles, parts washer tanks, gas cans and brine totes with no label, a faded label or one that just says cleaner.</p>\n<p>Every one of these is preventable with a working program and regular attention.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written program, chemical inventory and training", "html": "<p>Employers with hazardous chemicals in the workplace must have a <strong>written hazard communication program</strong>. It describes how the department handles labels, SDSs and training. It must include a <strong>list of the hazardous chemicals</strong> known to be present, using the same product identifier that appears on the label and the SDS so all three match. It must also explain how employees learn about the hazards of <strong>non-routine tasks</strong>, such as cleaning a clarifier, and about chemicals in <strong>unlabeled pipes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Keep the inventory current. A new product goes on the list, with its SDS on file, before it is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Training</strong> is required:<br />\n- At the time of <strong>initial assignment</strong> to a job where hazardous chemicals are present.<br />\n- Whenever a <strong>new chemical hazard</strong> the employee has not been trained on is introduced into the work area.</p>\n<p>Training covers where chemicals are used, where the program, chemical list and SDSs are kept, how to detect a release, the hazards, the protective measures, and how to read labels and SDSs. Keep training records.</p>"}, {"heading": "Safety Data Sheets", "html": "<p>A <strong>Safety Data Sheet (SDS)</strong> is the detailed reference for a chemical product. Every SDS follows the same <strong>16-section format</strong>, so once you learn where to look you can find what you need on any sheet:<br />\n- Section 1: Identification<br />\n- Section 2: Hazard(s) identification<br />\n- Section 3: Composition and ingredients<br />\n- Section 4: First-aid measures<br />\n- Section 5: Fire-fighting measures<br />\n- Section 6: Accidental release measures<br />\n- Section 7: Handling and storage<br />\n- Section 8: Exposure controls and personal protection<br />\n- Section 9: Physical and chemical properties<br />\n- Section 10: Stability and reactivity<br />\n- Section 11: Toxicological information<br />\n- Section 12: Ecological information<br />\n- Section 13: Disposal considerations<br />\n- Section 14: Transport information<br />\n- Section 15: Regulatory information<br />\n- Section 16: Other information</p>\n<p>For daily work, four sections matter most. <strong>Section 2</strong> gives the hazard classification, signal word, pictograms and hazard statements. <strong>Section 4</strong> tells you what to do right away if someone is splashed, breathes vapors or swallows the product. <strong>Section 7</strong> covers handling and storage. <strong>Section 8</strong> lists exposure limits and the gloves, eye protection and respirator you need. Section 10 lists incompatible materials.</p>\n<p>SDSs must be <strong>readily accessible during each work shift</strong> to employees in their work areas. A shop binder, a control room computer or a phone app all work, as long as everyone can reach the sheet without delay or permission. Electronic systems need a backup for outages. If you cannot find an SDS, tell your supervisor.</p>"}, {"heading": "Reading GHS labels", "html": "<p>HazCom uses the <strong>Globally Harmonized System (GHS)</strong> so labels look the same no matter who made the product. Every shipped container must carry a label with six elements:<br />\n- <strong>Product identifier.</strong> The name or code that matches the SDS and the inventory.<br />\n- <strong>Signal word.</strong> <strong>Danger</strong> for the more severe hazards, <strong>Warning</strong> for the less severe. A label carries only one.<br />\n- <strong>Hazard statements.</strong> Standard phrases that describe the hazard, such as \"Causes severe skin burns and eye damage.\"<br />\n- <strong>Precautionary statements.</strong> Standard phrases on prevention, response, storage and disposal.<br />\n- <strong>Pictograms.</strong> Black symbols on a white background inside a red diamond.<br />\n- <strong>Supplier information.</strong> Name, address and phone number of the manufacturer or importer.</p>\n<p>Read the label before you open a container for the first time, and again if you have not used the product in a while. Never remove or deface a supplier label.</p>\n<p>One exception: pesticides and herbicides are labeled under EPA rules, so their labels look different. They are still covered by HazCom for SDSs, the inventory and training.</p>"}, {"heading": "The nine pictograms", "html": "<p>Pictograms give you the hazard at a glance. Learn all nine:<br />\n- <strong>Health Hazard</strong> (silhouette with a starburst on the chest): carcinogens, reproductive toxins, respiratory sensitizers, target organ toxicity.<br />\n- <strong>Flame</strong>: flammable liquids, gases and solids. Gasoline, acetylene, many aerosols.<br />\n- <strong>Exclamation Mark</strong>: irritants, skin sensitizers, products harmful if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed. Many cleaners and deicers.<br />\n- <strong>Gas Cylinder</strong>: gases under pressure. Chlorine, oxygen, SF6, welding gases.<br />\n- <strong>Corrosion</strong>: skin burns and eye damage, or corrosive to metals. Sodium hypochlorite, battery acid, caustic soda.<br />\n- <strong>Exploding Bomb</strong>: explosives, self-reactives and organic peroxides.<br />\n- <strong>Flame Over Circle</strong>: oxidizers, which can cause or intensify fire. Calcium hypochlorite, concentrated hydrogen peroxide.<br />\n- <strong>Environment</strong> (dead tree and fish): toxic to aquatic life. OSHA does not require this one, but it matters near streams and storm drains.<br />\n- <strong>Skull and Crossbones</strong>: acute toxicity. Fatal or toxic in small amounts by swallowing, skin contact or inhalation. Chlorine gas, some pesticides.</p>\n<p>A product can carry more than one pictogram.</p>"}, {"heading": "Secondary container labeling", "html": "<p>Most unlabeled containers OSHA finds are not supplier drums. They are the spray bottles, buckets and small tanks that crews fill themselves. These <strong>secondary containers</strong> need labels too.</p>\n<p>A workplace label must carry at least the <strong>product identifier</strong> plus words, pictures or symbols that give general information about the hazards. The simplest approach is to copy the supplier label.</p>\n<p>There is one narrow exception. A <strong>portable container</strong> you fill from a labeled container does not need a label if the contents are for your <strong>immediate use</strong> and stay in your control. Once it is set down for someone else or stored, it needs a label.</p>\n<p>Good habits:<br />\n- Never put a chemical in a food or drink container.<br />\n- Label the container before you fill it.<br />\n- Replace labels that are faded, torn or soaked through.<br />\n- If no one knows what is in a container, do not sniff it or guess. Tag it and report it.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants handle some of the most hazardous chemicals in the city. <strong>Chlorine gas</strong> in cylinders is acutely toxic and under pressure. <strong>Sodium hypochlorite</strong> (bleach, usually 10 to 15 percent) is corrosive and releases chlorine gas if it contacts acid. Know where the SDSs and eyewashes are, and never mix chemicals. Solid waste and stormwater crews find unknown containers dumped at curbs and outfalls. Treat them as hazardous until identified.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Fleet shops stock fuels, brake cleaner, parts washer solvent, degreasers, antifreeze, paint and welding gases. Update the SDS file when a vendor changes products. Streets crews use asphalt products, cold patch and crack sealer, which carry burn and vapor hazards. Forestry crews apply herbicides, where the EPA label and the SDS both matter. Snow and ice crews handle rock salt, calcium chloride and brine, which irritate skin and eyes.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Substations hold lead-acid batteries with corrosive sulfuric acid, so an eyewash and acid resistant PPE must be close by. Transformer oil is combustible, and older units may contain PCBs. SF6 breakers contain gas under pressure. Line trucks carry solvents and sealants, and those small cans need labels and SDSs just like shop drums.</p>\n<p>For every division the questions are the same: Do you know what the product is? Where is its SDS right now? Is the container labeled? If any answer is no, fix it first.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["You have the right to know the hazards of every chemical you work with, and the department must back that up with a written program, a chemical list, labels, SDSs and training.", "Every SDS has the same 16 sections, and Sections 2, 4, 7 and 8 answer most field questions about hazards, first aid, handling and PPE.", "SDSs must be readily accessible to you in your work area during every shift, not locked in an office.", "A GHS label has six elements: product identifier, signal word, hazard statements, precautionary statements, pictograms and supplier information.", "Danger marks the more severe hazards and Warning the less severe, and the nine pictograms show the type of hazard at a glance.", "Secondary containers need a label with the product identifier and hazard information unless the container is for your immediate use and stays in your control.", "Training is required at initial assignment and whenever a new chemical hazard enters your work area.", "The most common HazCom citations, no written program, no SDS, no training and unlabeled containers, are all preventable."], "questions": [{"text": "How many sections are on a Safety Data Sheet in the format required by the HazCom standard?", "options": ["8", "12", "16", "20"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "HazCom requires a 16-section SDS in a fixed order, which makes it easy to find the same information on any sheet."}, {"text": "Which signal word on a GHS label indicates the more severe hazard?", "options": ["Caution", "Danger", "Warning", "Hazardous"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Danger is reserved for the more severe hazards and Warning for the less severe. A label carries only one signal word."}, {"text": "A mechanic pours brake cleaner from a labeled drum into a small unlabeled can. When does that can need a label?", "options": ["Never, portable containers are always exempt from labeling", "Only if the can holds more than one gallon", "Only if the brake cleaner is flammable", "If it is set down for another worker, passed to another shift or stored instead of used right away"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The portable container exception applies only when the contents are for the immediate use of the person who filled it. Once the container leaves that person's control, it needs a workplace label."}, {"text": "Which item must be included in the written hazard communication program?", "options": ["A list of the hazardous chemicals known to be present in the workplace", "A copy of every employee's medical records", "Purchase receipts for every chemical bought that year", "The phone number of the nearest OSHA area office"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The written program must include a chemical inventory that uses the same product identifiers found on labels and SDSs, along with how the department handles labels, SDSs, training, non-routine tasks and unlabeled pipes."}, {"text": "What does the skull and crossbones pictogram tell you about a product?", "options": ["It is corrosive to metal", "It is an oxidizer that can intensify a fire", "It is a gas stored under pressure", "It can be fatal or toxic in small amounts if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed through the skin"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The skull and crossbones marks acute toxicity, meaning the product can cause death or serious harm from a small exposure. Chlorine gas and some pesticides carry it."}, {"text": "When must an employee receive HazCom training?", "options": ["Only once, at the time of hire", "At initial assignment and whenever a new chemical hazard is introduced into the work area", "Only after an exposure incident or spill", "Once every five years"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard requires training at initial assignment and again whenever a new hazard the employee has not been trained on enters the work area. Annual refreshers are good practice, but those two events are the triggers in the rule."}, {"text": "Which of these meets the requirement for Safety Data Sheet access?", "options": ["SDSs are kept in a locked office and provided on written request", "SDSs are stored at city hall and sent to the plant when needed", "SDSs are available to employees in their work area during every shift, with a backup if the electronic system fails", "SDSs are kept only by the chemical supplier, who will send one on request"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "SDSs must be readily accessible during each work shift to employees when they are in their work areas. Electronic access is fine as long as there are no barriers to immediate access and there is a backup."}, {"text": "What hazard does the flame over circle pictogram represent?", "options": ["Oxidizer", "Flammable liquid", "Explosive", "Gas under pressure"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The flame over circle marks oxidizers, which can cause or intensify a fire. The plain flame marks flammables, the exploding bomb marks explosives and the gas cylinder marks gases under pressure."}, {"text": "A worker at the water plant is splashed with a chemical. Which SDS section tells you what to do right away?", "options": ["Section 2, Hazard identification", "Section 4, First-aid measures", "Section 9, Physical and chemical properties", "Section 14, Transport information"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 4 of every SDS gives first-aid measures for skin, eye, inhalation and ingestion exposures. Section 2 describes the hazards but not the immediate response."}, {"text": "At a wastewater plant, what happens if sodium hypochlorite is accidentally mixed with an acid?", "options": ["The two neutralize into a harmless salt solution", "It forms a stronger disinfectant that is safe to use", "It releases toxic chlorine gas", "Nothing, because sodium hypochlorite is not reactive"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Sodium hypochlorite reacts with acids to release chlorine gas, which is acutely toxic. Section 10 of the SDS lists incompatible materials like this, which is why chemicals are never mixed or stored carelessly."}]}, {"slug": "lockout-tagout", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy (Lockout/Tagout)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "standard_title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 3, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Lockout/tagout protects workers from the unexpected startup of machines or the release of stored energy while they service or maintain equipment. This course explains the energy control program required by 29 CFR 1910.147, the roles of authorized and affected employees, and the step-by-step sequence for locking out, verifying zero energy and safely restoring equipment to service. Examples come from treatment plants, solid waste operations, fleet shops, traffic signal work and electric service work.", "objectives": ["Explain the purpose of lockout/tagout and name the types of hazardous energy found in public works equipment.", "Describe the roles of authorized, affected and other employees under an energy control program.", "Perform the lockout sequence in order, including releasing stored energy and verifying zero energy before work begins.", "Explain when tags alone may be used and why a tag gives less protection than a lock.", "Apply the rules for group lockout, shift changes and removing a lock when its owner is unavailable.", "Identify the training, retraining and annual inspection requirements that keep an energy control program current."], "sections": [{"heading": "What lockout/tagout is for", "html": "<p><strong>Lockout/tagout (LOTO)</strong> keeps a machine from starting, energizing or releasing stored energy while someone is servicing or maintaining it. 29 CFR 1910.147 covers servicing and maintenance in general industry where unexpected startup or the release of stored energy could injure an employee.</p>\n<p>The hazard is easy to picture. A pump restarts while a mechanic has a hand inside the volute. A baler ram cycles while someone clears a jam. A dump body drifts down on a technician underneath it. In each case the energy was still there, or came back, while a person was in the danger zone.</p>\n<p>The standard does not apply to cord-and-plug equipment when the plug is pulled and stays under the exclusive control of the person doing the work. Everything else that involves reaching into, under or behind a machine for servicing is covered.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Lockout/tagout appears on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards year after year. Inspectors usually find the same problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No machine-specific procedures.</strong> A general policy exists, but no written steps for each machine say which energy sources to isolate and how to verify zero energy.</li>\n<li><strong>No periodic inspection.</strong> Procedures were never inspected at least annually, or no certification of the inspection exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Training gaps.</strong> Authorized employees were never trained or retrained, or affected employees were never told what the locks and tags mean.</li>\n<li><strong>Procedures not used.</strong> Locks and tags exist, but crews clear jams with only the stop button pressed.</li>\n<li><strong>No program at all.</strong> Smaller shops and plants sometimes have nothing on paper.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works departments face every one of these risks because crews maintain so many different machines across plants, shops and field equipment.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of hazardous energy", "html": "<p>Energy that can hurt you is not limited to electricity. Before any servicing task, identify every source and how much of it there is.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> motors, control circuits, generators, and capacitors that hold a charge after power is off.</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanical:</strong> rotating shafts, flywheels, blades and belts that keep moving after shutdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydraulic:</strong> pressurized fluid in refuse truck packers, dump bodies, plow lifts and loader arms.</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumatic:</strong> compressed air in shop lines, air brakes and actuators.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical:</strong> chlorine, sulfur dioxide, caustics and fuels in piping and tanks.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal:</strong> steam, hot water, engine parts and exhaust.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity:</strong> raised dump bodies, plow blades, buckets, gates and anything that can fall or drift down.</li>\n<li><strong>Stored energy:</strong> compressed springs, charged capacitors, pressure trapped in lines and accumulators, and elevated parts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A machine often has more than one source. A refuse truck has the engine, hydraulics, air brakes and a tailgate that gravity will close.</p>"}, {"heading": "The energy control program and who does what", "html": "<p>The standard requires a written <strong>energy control program</strong> with three parts: energy control procedures, employee training and periodic inspections.</p>\n<p><strong>Written procedures</strong> must be specific to each machine or group of similar machines. Each one states the steps to shut down, isolate, block and secure the equipment, the steps for placing, removing and transferring locks and tags, and how to test that isolation worked. A general policy on the wall does not meet this requirement.</p>\n<p>Three groups of people are defined:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authorized employees</strong> apply locks and tags and perform the servicing. Only they may lock out equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Affected employees</strong> operate the equipment or work in the area where it is serviced. They must know what the locks and tags mean and never try to restart locked-out equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Other employees</strong> work where LOTO may be used. They are told never to restart locked-out equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>energy isolating device</strong> is a physical device such as a disconnect switch, circuit breaker, line valve or block. Push buttons, selector switches, emergency stops and other control circuit devices are <strong>not</strong> energy isolating devices and cannot be used for lockout.</p>"}, {"heading": "The lockout sequence, step by step", "html": "<p>Follow the written procedure in order. Skipping a step is how injuries happen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1. Notify</strong> affected employees that the equipment will be shut down and locked out.</li>\n<li><strong>2. Prepare for shutdown.</strong> Know the types and magnitude of energy and how each source is controlled.</li>\n<li><strong>3. Shut down</strong> the equipment using its normal stopping procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>4. Isolate</strong> every energy source: open the disconnect, close and chain the valve, pull the fuses as the procedure directs.</li>\n<li><strong>5. Apply locks and tags</strong> to each isolating device. Each authorized employee applies a personal lock.</li>\n<li><strong>6. Release or restrain stored energy.</strong> Bleed pressure, let moving parts stop, discharge capacitors, lower or block raised parts. If energy can build back up, keep checking until the job is done.</li>\n<li><strong>7. Verify zero energy.</strong> Try to start the machine with the normal controls, test with a meter where appropriate and confirm that nothing moves. Return the controls to off afterward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only then does work begin.</p>\n<p>To <strong>restore service</strong>, remove tools, replace guards and confirm everyone is clear. Each person removes their own lock, affected employees are notified, and then the equipment is reenergized. If the machine must be energized briefly for testing, clear the area, remove the locks, test, then deenergize and reapply the locks before continuing work.</p>"}, {"heading": "Locks, tags, group lockout and shift changes", "html": "<p><strong>Lockout is the rule.</strong> When an energy isolating device can accept a lock, it must be locked. A <strong>tagout</strong> alone is allowed only when the device cannot be locked, or when the employer can show that tags plus extra measures give protection equal to a lock, such as removing a fuse, blocking a controlling switch or removing a valve handle.</p>\n<p>A tag is a warning, not a restraint. It must be legible, durable, attached with a self-locking, nonreusable fastener, and it must name the person who placed it. Treat a tag as seriously as a lock and never remove or bypass one.</p>\n<p><strong>Group lockout.</strong> When a crew works together, one authorized employee takes primary responsibility, usually with a group lockbox. The keys to the isolation locks go in the box, and each crew member adds a personal lock to the box when work starts and removes it when they finish.</p>\n<p><strong>Shift changes.</strong> The program must have a procedure for handing off locks between off-going and on-coming employees so protection never lapses.</p>\n<p><strong>Lock removal when the owner is unavailable.</strong> Only the person who applied a lock may remove it. If that employee is not at the facility, the lock may be removed only under a documented procedure: verify the employee is not on site, make every reasonable effort to contact them, and make sure they know the lock is gone before they return to work.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspections, training and retraining", "html": "<p><strong>Periodic inspection.</strong> Each energy control procedure must be inspected at least annually by an authorized employee who is not using that procedure at the time. The inspector watches the procedure being used, corrects any deviations and reviews responsibilities with each authorized employee. The inspection is <strong>certified</strong> in writing with the equipment, the date, the employees included and the inspector's name.</p>\n<p><strong>Training.</strong> Authorized employees learn to recognize hazardous energy, its type and magnitude, and how to isolate and control it. Affected employees learn the purpose and use of the procedure. All other employees learn that locked or tagged equipment is never to be restarted. The employer certifies training with each employee's name and the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Retraining</strong> is required when job assignments change, when equipment or processes change in a way that creates a new hazard, when procedures change, or when an inspection shows an employee is not following the procedure.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Wastewater and water plants have pumps, clarifier drives, blowers, screens and chemical feed systems. Lock out the motor disconnect, close and tag the suction and discharge valves, bleed pressure and block any rake arm or gate that gravity can move. At solid waste transfer stations, the conveyor and baler carry electrical, hydraulic and gravity energy. Clear jams only after full lockout, never with the machine merely stopped by a button.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Refuse trucks, snowplows and dump trucks hold hydraulic pressure and raised loads. Lower the body or plow, or install the manufacturer's body prop or pin, bleed the hydraulics and remove the key before working underneath. Chipper discs and drums coast after shutdown. Wait for them to stop and disable the engine before clearing the throat or changing knives. Traffic signal cabinets, facility generators and automatic transfer switches need the feeder breaker and the generator source locked out, because a transfer switch can start the generator and energize a bus on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Work on electric power generation, transmission and distribution installations follows 29 CFR 1910.269(d) and (m), which rely on clearances, system operator tags and protective grounds, rather than 1910.147. The sequence is the same in spirit: notify, deenergize, isolate, lock or tag, then test and ground before touching. Shop equipment, hydraulic tools and building systems still fall under 1910.147.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Lockout/tagout exists to prevent unexpected startup or the release of stored energy while equipment is being serviced or maintained.", "Every machine needs a written, machine-specific procedure that names the energy sources, the isolating devices and how to verify zero energy.", "Only authorized employees apply locks and tags, and only the person who applied a lock may remove it, except under a documented absent-employee procedure.", "Never start work until stored energy is released or restrained and you have tried to start the machine to prove it is dead.", "Push buttons and emergency stops are not energy isolating devices; a lock belongs on the disconnect, breaker or valve.", "Use a lock whenever the device can accept one; a tag alone is allowed only when the device cannot be locked or extra measures give equal protection.", "Each procedure must be inspected at least annually and the inspection certified, and training must be refreshed whenever jobs, equipment or procedures change."], "questions": [{"text": "What is the main purpose of lockout/tagout under 29 CFR 1910.147?", "options": ["To make sure machines are repaired only by licensed contractors", "To document equipment downtime for maintenance records", "To prevent unexpected startup or the release of stored energy while equipment is serviced", "To keep operators from running equipment above its rated speed"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard exists to keep machines from energizing, starting up or releasing stored energy while someone is performing servicing or maintenance."}, {"text": "Which of these counts as an energy isolating device?", "options": ["A manually operated disconnect switch", "The emergency stop button on the control panel", "A selector switch turned to off", "A push button labeled stop"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Energy isolating devices physically block the flow of energy, such as disconnects, breakers, line valves and blocks. Push buttons, selector switches and emergency stops are control circuit devices and cannot be used for lockout."}, {"text": "After locks are applied and stored energy is released, what must happen before work begins?", "options": ["The supervisor signs the work order", "Affected employees are sent home", "The equipment is cleaned and lubricated", "The authorized employee tries to start the equipment to verify it is deenergized"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Verification is the last step before work starts. Trying the normal start controls, and testing with a meter where appropriate, proves the isolation actually worked."}, {"text": "When is a tag alone allowed instead of a lock?", "options": ["Whenever the job will take less than one hour", "When the energy isolating device cannot accept a lock, or when tagout plus extra measures gives protection equal to a lock", "When the authorized employee prefers tags", "When the machine has been serviced before without incident"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Lockout is required whenever the device can be locked. Tagout alone is limited to devices that cannot accept a lock, or to cases where the employer shows that extra measures make tagout as protective as a lock."}, {"text": "How often must each energy control procedure be inspected, and who performs the inspection?", "options": ["At least annually, by an authorized employee who is not using that procedure at the time", "Every five years, by an outside consultant", "Monthly, by the affected employees who operate the machine", "Only after an injury, by the plant manager"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires a periodic inspection at least annually by an authorized employee other than those using the procedure, and the inspection must be certified in writing."}, {"text": "A mechanic locked out a pump and left for the day with the lock still on. What is required before anyone else removes that lock?", "options": ["Nothing, as long as the shift supervisor has a master key", "A second mechanic applies a new lock and cuts the old one", "The affected operator confirms the pump is needed back in service", "Verify the mechanic is not on site, make every reasonable effort to contact them, and ensure they know the lock is gone before they return to work, following a documented procedure"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Only the person who applied a lock may remove it. The exception requires a documented procedure that includes verifying the employee is not at the facility, trying to contact them and informing them before they resume work."}, {"text": "A dump body is raised and the truck is shut off with the key removed. What hazardous energy is still present?", "options": ["Thermal energy from the hydraulic fluid", "Gravity, because the body can drift or fall if the hydraulics bleed down", "No hazardous energy remains once the engine is off", "Chemical energy in the fuel tank"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A raised body or plow is stored gravitational energy. It must be lowered or secured with the body prop or pin before anyone works underneath."}, {"text": "An operator runs the clarifier drive but never performs maintenance on it. Under the standard, this person is which type of employee?", "options": ["Authorized employee", "Qualified employee", "Affected employee", "Competent person"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An affected employee operates or uses the equipment being serviced, or works in the area where it is serviced. They must understand the procedure but do not apply locks."}, {"text": "In a group lockout, how does each crew member stay protected?", "options": ["Each member places a personal lock on the group lockbox when starting work and removes it when finished", "The lead worker's single lock covers everyone on the crew", "Each member keeps a copy of the lockbox key in their pocket", "The crew signs a sign-in sheet instead of using locks"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Group lockout gives one authorized employee primary responsibility, but every member still attaches a personal lock to the group device or lockbox so no one can reenergize the equipment while they are exposed."}, {"text": "A line crew is deenergizing a distribution circuit to replace a pole. Which standard governs that hazardous energy control work?", "options": ["29 CFR 1910.147 only", "29 CFR 1910.212", "29 CFR 1910.178", "29 CFR 1910.269(d) and (m)"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Electric power generation, transmission and distribution work follows 1910.269(d) and (m) rather than 1910.147, though the sequence of notify, deenergize, isolate, lock or tag and test is the same in spirit."}]}, {"slug": "scaffolding", "title": "Scaffolding", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "standard_title": "Scaffolding, general requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 4, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Scaffolds put public works crews at height over pump stations, clarifiers, bridge decks, substation structures and facility walls. This course covers the parts of 29 CFR 1926.451 that matter most in the field: capacity, planking, foundations, access, guardrails, power line clearance and the competent person inspection. It also reviews the mistakes that draw the most OSHA citations so you can spot them before anyone climbs.", "objectives": ["Explain the capacity rules for scaffolds, suspension ropes and guardrails.", "Describe how a scaffold platform must be planked, supported and restrained from tipping.", "Identify acceptable means of access and the fall protection required above 10 feet.", "Apply the minimum clearance distances between scaffolds and energized power lines.", "Recognize when a competent person must inspect a scaffold and when work must stop for weather.", "Spot the most commonly cited scaffold violations on a public works job."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Scaffold violations appear on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards year after year. Scaffolds are often set up quickly, by crews who do it a few times a year, for a job that lasts a day or two. That is exactly when guardrails get skipped and nobody is named as the <strong>competent person</strong>.</p>\n<p>The citations inspectors write most often are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Missing or incomplete <strong>guardrails</strong> on platforms more than 10 feet up.</li>\n<li>Improper <strong>planking</strong>: gaps, short overlaps, or levels that are not fully decked.</li>\n<li>Unsafe <strong>access</strong>: climbing cross braces or frames instead of using a ladder or stair.</li>\n<li>No <strong>competent person inspection</strong> before the shift.</li>\n<li>Poor <strong>foundations</strong>, such as legs resting on blocks or soft ground.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works crews face the same hazards when they repair a bridge wall or repaint a treatment building. Although 1926.451 is a construction standard, OSHA's general industry rule at 1910.27(a) requires scaffolds used in general industry to meet the same requirements, so these rules apply to maintenance jobs too.</p>"}, {"heading": "Capacity and platform construction", "html": "<p>Every scaffold and every scaffold component must support its own weight plus at least <strong>4 times the maximum intended load</strong> without failing (1926.451(a)(1)). The maximum intended load is everyone on the platform plus their tools and materials. On suspension scaffolds, each rope and its hardware must support at least <strong>6 times</strong> the maximum intended load (1926.451(a)(4)).</p>\n<p>Platforms must be built so nobody can step through or off the edge (1926.451(b)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each working level must be <strong>fully planked or decked</strong> between the front uprights and the guardrail supports.</li>\n<li>Gaps between planks, and between the platform and the uprights, may be no more than <strong>1 inch</strong> wide.</li>\n<li>Platforms and walkways must be at least <strong>18 inches wide</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each plank end must extend at least <strong>6 inches</strong> past the centerline of its support unless cleated or hooked, and no more than <strong>12 inches</strong> on platforms 10 feet or shorter, or <strong>18 inches</strong> on longer platforms, unless the overhang is guarded.</li>\n<li>Where planks overlap to make a long platform, the overlap must be at least <strong>12 inches</strong> and must sit over a support.</li>\n<li>Use scaffold-grade planks or manufactured decks.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Foundations, stability and tipping", "html": "<p>A scaffold is only as good as what it stands on. Supported scaffold legs and frames must rest on <strong>base plates</strong> and <strong>mud sills</strong> or another firm foundation (1926.451(c)(2)). Footings must be level, sound, rigid and able to carry the loaded scaffold without settling. Never set a leg on bricks, blocks, boxes, barrels or other unstable objects. Legs must be plumb and braced so the scaffold cannot sway.</p>\n<p>Tall, narrow scaffolds tip. When the <strong>height to base width ratio is more than 4 to 1</strong> (counting outriggers, if used), the scaffold must be restrained with <strong>guys, ties or braces</strong> to the structure (1926.451(c)(1)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Install the first tie at the closest horizontal member to the 4 to 1 height. A scaffold 5 feet wide needs its first tie at about 20 feet.</li>\n<li>Repeat ties every 20 feet or less going up for scaffolds 3 feet wide or narrower, and every 26 feet or less for wider scaffolds, with ties at each end and no more than 30 feet apart.</li>\n<li>A cantilevered or other off-center load also requires ties, braces or outriggers.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Safe access", "html": "<p>If the platform is more than <strong>2 feet</strong> above or below the point of access, crews need a proper way up (1926.451(e)(1)). Acceptable means include portable, hook-on or attachable ladders, stair towers, ramps and walkways, built-in frame ladders designed for climbing, or direct access from another scaffold or structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Cross braces are never a means of access.</strong> Climbing the X braces is one of the most common violations inspectors see, and many scaffold falls happen getting on and off.</p>\n<p>Access rules worth remembering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hook-on and attachable ladders need their bottom rung no more than 24 inches above the supporting level.</li>\n<li>Frame rungs may be climbed only if the manufacturer designed that frame as a ladder.</li>\n<li>Ladders, boxes, barrels and other makeshift devices may not be used on top of a platform to gain height (1926.451(f)(14) and (f)(15)).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Fall protection, guardrails and falling objects", "html": "<p>Each employee on a scaffold platform more than <strong>10 feet above a lower level</strong> must be protected from falling (1926.451(g)(1)). For most supported scaffolds that means a <strong>guardrail system</strong>. Where guardrails are not practical, a personal fall arrest system tied to a proper anchorage is used.</p>\n<p>Guardrail requirements (1926.451(g)(4)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toprail</strong> between <strong>38 and 45 inches</strong> above the platform on supported scaffolds placed in service after January 1, 2000, able to withstand 200 pounds of force.</li>\n<li><strong>Midrail</strong> about halfway between the toprail and the platform, able to withstand 150 pounds.</li>\n<li>Guardrails on all open sides and ends.</li>\n<li>Cross bracing may serve as a midrail if the braces cross between 20 and 30 inches above the platform, or as a toprail if they cross between 38 and 48 inches, but not both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hard hats are required on and around scaffolds. Where objects could fall on people below, add <strong>toeboards</strong> at least <strong>3.5 inches</strong> high with no more than a quarter inch gap at the platform (1926.451(h)), or use screens, nets, canopies or a barricaded zone below.</p>"}, {"heading": "Power line clearance", "html": "<p>Scaffolds are usually metal, and the people on them handle conductive pipe and tools. Before erecting, moving or working from a scaffold near overhead lines, apply the clearance table in 1926.451(f)(6):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uninsulated lines</strong> up to 50 kV: at least <strong>10 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Insulated lines</strong> under 300 volts: at least <strong>3 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Insulated lines</strong> 300 volts to 50 kV: at least <strong>10 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li>Any line over 50 kV: 10 feet plus 0.4 inch for each kV over 50, or two times the length of the line insulator, and never less than 10 feet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The distance applies to the scaffold and to any conductive material handled on it. Treat every line as uninsulated and energized unless the utility has confirmed otherwise. The weatherproof covering on many distribution conductors is not insulation.</p>\n<p>Crews may work closer only after the utility or system operator has <strong>de-energized the lines, relocated them or installed protective coverings</strong>. For Electric Services, that means a documented hold from the system operator. For Environmental and Public Services crews, call the utility and get the answer before the first frame goes up.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection, training, mobile scaffolds and weather", "html": "<p>A <strong>competent person</strong> must inspect the scaffold for visible defects <strong>before each work shift</strong> and after any event that could affect its structural integrity, such as a vehicle strike, a storm or an overload (1926.451(f)(3)). A competent person can identify scaffold hazards and has authority to correct them. Damaged parts must be repaired, replaced or removed from service right away. Scaffolds may be erected, moved, dismantled or altered only under a competent person's direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Training</strong> (1926.454): everyone who works from a scaffold must be trained by a qualified person on the electrical, fall and falling object hazards, the fall protection in use, material handling and the maximum intended load. Erectors, dismantlers and inspectors need training from a competent person.</p>\n<p><strong>Mobile scaffolds</strong> (1926.452(w)): lock the casters before anyone climbs. Push from near the base, never more than 5 feet up, and warn everyone on the platform first. Riding is allowed only when the surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of holes, the height to base ratio during the move is 2 to 1 or less, and nobody is on a part that extends past the wheels.</p>\n<p><strong>Weather</strong>: no scaffold work during storms or high winds unless the competent person decides it is safe and workers are protected by personal fall arrest or wind screens (1926.451(f)(12)). Clear snow and ice before use.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p>Public works scaffold jobs tend to be short and varied. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Environmental Services</strong>: replacing clarifier weirs, repairing a pump station roof, painting digester walls, working over an open channel. Surfaces are wet and a fall may end in water or a tank, so the rescue plan belongs in the job briefing.</li>\n<li><strong>Public Services</strong>: bridge rail and culvert headwall repair, replacing lights in a fleet garage or gym, exterior painting, sign and signal work from a rolling tower. Mobile scaffolds on sloped aprons tip easily. Level them with screw jacks and lock the casters.</li>\n<li><strong>Electric Services</strong>: substation structure painting and bus work with equipment de-energized and grounded, and support for contractors building new structures. Clearance rules and the system operator's hold control the job.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before anyone climbs, ask five questions. Who is the competent person, and did they inspect it this shift? Is every level fully planked with proper overlap? Is there a ladder or stair, not a cross brace? Are guardrails up above 10 feet? How far is the nearest energized line? If any answer is wrong, fix it on the ground. Rented scaffolds are no exception; your competent person still owns the inspection.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Scaffolds must support at least 4 times the maximum intended load, and suspension ropes at least 6 times.", "Platforms must be fully planked, at least 18 inches wide, with gaps of no more than 1 inch and plank ends extending 6 to 12 inches past the support.", "Legs rest on base plates and mud sills on level, sound footing, never on blocks, boxes or barrels.", "Tie, guy or brace any scaffold taller than 4 times its base width.", "Get on and off with a ladder, stair tower or ramp; cross braces are never access.", "Above 10 feet, use guardrails with a toprail 38 to 45 inches high and a midrail, or personal fall arrest, plus toeboards where objects could fall on people.", "Stay at least 10 feet from uninsulated lines up to 50 kV, and farther for higher voltages, unless the utility has de-energized, moved or covered them.", "A competent person inspects every scaffold before each shift and after anything that could damage it."], "questions": [{"text": "A scaffold and each of its components must be able to support its own weight plus at least how many times the maximum intended load?", "options": ["2 times", "3 times", "4 times", "6 times"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.451(a)(1) requires every scaffold and scaffold component to support, without failure, its own weight plus at least 4 times the maximum intended load. The 6 times figure applies to suspension ropes and their hardware."}, {"text": "Which platform meets the planking requirements of 1926.451(b)?", "options": ["A fully decked platform 20 inches wide with no gap wider than 1 inch between planks", "A platform with a 3 inch gap left open along the uprights so tools can be passed up", "A 16 inch wide platform made of two planks laid side by side", "A platform where the planks overlap by 8 inches midway between the supports"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Platforms must be fully planked and at least 18 inches wide, with gaps of no more than 1 inch. Overlaps must be at least 12 inches and must occur over a support."}, {"text": "Which of these is an acceptable foundation for a supported scaffold leg?", "options": ["A stack of two concrete blocks on packed gravel", "An empty 55 gallon drum turned upside down", "A wood pallet laid flat on asphalt", "A base plate on a mud sill set on level, compacted ground"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "1926.451(c)(2) requires legs to bear on base plates and mud sills or another firm, level, rigid foundation. Blocks, drums, pallets and other unstable objects may not be used."}, {"text": "A supported scaffold is 5 feet wide at the base with no outriggers. At about what height must it first be tied, guyed or braced to prevent tipping?", "options": ["10 feet", "20 feet", "26 feet", "30 feet"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A height to base width ratio of more than 4 to 1 requires restraint. Four times 5 feet is 20 feet, so the first tie goes at the closest horizontal member to that height."}, {"text": "A crew needs to reach a platform 6 feet above the ground. Which means of access is allowed under 1926.451(e)?", "options": ["A hook-on ladder with its bottom rung no more than 24 inches above the supporting level", "The cross braces, as long as the worker faces the scaffold", "The end frame, since any horizontal member can serve as a rung", "A 5 gallon bucket placed next to the lowest frame member"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Portable, hook-on and attachable ladders, stair towers, ramps and built-in access designed for climbing are acceptable. Cross braces are never a means of access, and frame members may be climbed only when designed as ladder rungs."}, {"text": "On a supported scaffold placed in service after January 1, 2000, the top edge of the toprail must be installed how high above the platform?", "options": ["30 to 36 inches", "36 to 42 inches", "38 to 45 inches", "42 to 48 inches"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.451(g)(4)(ii) sets the toprail height at 38 to 45 inches for supported scaffolds placed in service after January 1, 2000, with a midrail about halfway between the toprail and the platform."}, {"text": "A toeboard used to protect people below a scaffold from falling objects must be at least how tall?", "options": ["2 inches", "3.5 inches", "4 inches", "6 inches"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "1926.451(h)(4)(ii) requires toeboards at least 3.5 inches high, securely fastened at the outer edge of the platform with no more than a quarter inch gap above the walking surface."}, {"text": "A crew is setting up a frame scaffold under an uninsulated 12.47 kV distribution line. What is the minimum clearance that must be kept between the scaffold, or any conductive material handled on it, and the line?", "options": ["3 feet", "6 feet", "8 feet", "10 feet"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "For uninsulated lines up to 50 kV, 1926.451(f)(6) requires at least 10 feet of clearance. The 3 foot distance applies only to insulated lines under 300 volts."}, {"text": "When must a competent person inspect a scaffold for visible defects?", "options": ["Before each work shift and after any occurrence that could affect its structural integrity", "Once when it is first erected and again when it is dismantled", "Weekly, or whenever a worker reports a problem", "Only after a storm, vehicle strike or other damaging event"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1926.451(f)(3) requires inspection before each work shift and after any event that could affect the scaffold's integrity. Erection-only or weekly checks do not meet the rule."}, {"text": "Workers may ride a mobile scaffold while it is being moved only if certain conditions are met. Which is one of them?", "options": ["The crew leader gives a verbal warning after the move begins", "The scaffold is pushed from a point at least 6 feet above the ground for better leverage", "The surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of pits, holes and obstructions", "The height to base width ratio during the move is no more than 4 to 1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.452(w)(6) allows riding only when the surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of holes and obstructions, the height to base ratio during the move is 2 to 1 or less, and no one is on a part that extends past the wheels."}]}, {"slug": "ladders", "title": "Ladders", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "standard_title": "Ladders (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 5, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Ladders are on nearly every public works truck and in every shop, which is why ladder violations stay on OSHA's Top 10 list every year. This course covers how to pick the right ladder, inspect it, set it up at the correct angle, climb it safely and know when a ladder is the wrong tool. It also explains the rules for fixed ladders on tanks and structures and the training required under 1926.1060.", "objectives": ["Select a ladder with the correct duty rating and material for the task, including nonconductive ladders near electrical hazards.", "Inspect a ladder before use, identify defects that require removal from service, and tag the ladder so no one else uses it.", "Set up an extension ladder at the 4 to 1 angle with the side rails extending at least 3 feet above the landing or secured with a grab device.", "Apply the use rules in 1926.1053(b), including three points of contact, facing the ladder and staying off the top steps.", "Recognize when fixed ladders on tanks and structures require cages, wells or personal fall arrest systems.", "Describe the ladder training required under 1926.1060 and how it is documented."], "sections": [{"heading": "Choosing the right ladder", "html": "<p>Ladder safety starts before you climb. Three choices decide whether the ladder can do the job.</p>\n<p><strong>Duty rating.</strong> Every portable ladder carries a label with its duty rating, the maximum load it can carry, including you, your tools and your materials. Ratings run from Type III (200 pounds) up to Type IAA (375 pounds). The department buys Type IA (300 pounds) and IAA ladders for field work. Add up your body weight, tool belt and whatever you are carrying before you choose.</p>\n<p><strong>Material.</strong> Near any electrical hazard, use a <strong>nonconductive fiberglass ladder</strong>. Section 1926.1053(b)(12) requires nonconductive side rails when the employee or the ladder could contact exposed energized equipment. <strong>Never use a metal ladder near energized lines or equipment</strong>, including service drops, street light poles, signal cabinets and anything in a substation. Aluminum conducts, and so does wet wood.</p>\n<p><strong>Type.</strong> A stepladder is for free-standing work, fully opened with the spreaders locked, never leaned against a wall. An extension ladder is for reaching a landing or wall. Either must be long enough to work from without standing on the top steps.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Ladder citations are common because ladders are everywhere and the mistakes are visible from the street.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often under 1926.1053 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(b)(1)</strong> Side rails not extending at least 3 feet above the upper landing, with no grab device or securing in its place.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(4)</strong> Ladders used for something other than their designed purpose, such as a closed stepladder leaned against a wall.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(13)</strong> Workers standing on the top step or cap of a stepladder.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(16)</strong> Defective ladders left in service instead of being tagged and removed.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(22)</strong> Workers carrying loads that could cause them to lose their balance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of these take special equipment to fix. They take the extra minute to pick the right ladder, set it correctly and tie it off. Ladder falls are usually short falls, and short falls still break bones and end careers.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection and removing defective ladders", "html": "<p>Section 1926.1053(b)(15) requires a competent person to inspect ladders periodically and after any event that could affect their safe use, such as being dropped or run over. Every user should also look the ladder over before each climb. Check for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cracked, split, bent or dented side rails.</li>\n<li>Missing, loose, bent or broken rungs, cleats or steps.</li>\n<li>Worn or missing nonslip feet.</li>\n<li>Loose rivets, bolts or hinges, spreaders that do not lock, and rung locks that do not hold.</li>\n<li>Oil, grease, mud or ice on rungs or rails.</li>\n<li>Fiberglass that is chalky, cracked or shows exposed fibers, which weakens the rail and reduces its insulating value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under 1926.1053(b)(16), a ladder with a structural defect must be <strong>immediately marked as defective or tagged 'Do Not Use'</strong> and <strong>withdrawn from service</strong> until it is repaired to its original design or discarded. Do not set it back on the truck rack for the next shift to grab. Tag it, pull it and tell your supervisor. Do not make field repairs with tape, wire or scrap lumber.</p>"}, {"heading": "Setting up the ladder", "html": "<p>Most ladder falls happen because the ladder moved. The setup rules exist to keep it still.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The 4 to 1 angle.</strong> Set a non-self-supporting ladder so the horizontal distance from the foot to the top support is <strong>one quarter of the working length</strong>. For a ladder reaching 16 feet up, the feet go 4 feet out.</li>\n<li><strong>Extend 3 feet above the landing.</strong> Side rails must <strong>extend at least 3 feet above an upper landing</strong>. If the ladder is too short, it must be <strong>secured at the top to a rigid support</strong> with a <strong>grab rail or grab device</strong> installed so you have something to hold while stepping on and off.</li>\n<li><strong>Firm, level footing.</strong> Section 1926.1053(b)(6) requires stable and level surfaces unless the ladder is secured against displacement. Use leg levelers on slopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Never on boxes, barrels or other unstable bases.</strong> The general industry rule, 1910.23(c), says this directly. Get a longer ladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure the ladder.</strong> Tie it off at the top, have someone foot the bottom, or both. In a passageway, doorway or driveway, secure or barricade it.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the top and bottom clear</strong> so you can step on and off without tripping.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Climbing and working from a ladder", "html": "<p>Once the ladder is set, the rules in 1926.1053(b) govern how you use it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Face the ladder</strong> going up and coming down.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep three points of contact</strong>: two hands and one foot, or two feet and one hand, on the ladder when climbing. Use at least one hand to grasp the ladder at all times.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not carry loads that could cause you to lose your balance.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keep your belt buckle between the rails.</strong> Leaning out tips the ladder. Climb down and move it instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay off the top of a stepladder.</strong> The <strong>top or top step</strong> of a stepladder shall not be used as a step.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay off the top rungs of an extension ladder.</strong> Never stand above the highest standing level marked on the manufacturer's label. OSHA's ladder safety guidance is not to stand on the top three rungs of a straight or extension ladder. Need more height? Get a longer ladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not exceed the maximum intended load</strong>, which includes everything on the ladder with you.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not move, shift or extend a ladder while anyone is on it.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Use ladders only for their designed purpose</strong>, never as a scaffold plank or ramp, and <strong>do not tie ladders together</strong> unless they are designed for it.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Fixed ladders on tanks, towers and structures", "html": "<p>Public works facilities are full of fixed ladders: water towers, clarifiers, digesters, wet wells and shop mezzanines. Most are general industry walking-working surfaces, so <strong>29 CFR 1910.23</strong> (ladders) and <strong>1910.28</strong> (duty to have fall protection) apply alongside the construction rule.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Under 1926.1053(a)(19), a fixed ladder with a length of climb of <strong>24 feet or more</strong> must have a ladder safety device, a self-retracting lifeline, or a cage or well with the ladder broken into sections. Under 1910.28(b)(9), a fixed ladder extending <strong>more than 24 feet</strong> above a lower level must have a personal fall arrest system, a ladder safety system, a cage or a well. Cages and wells are being phased out: a ladder installed or replaced since November 19, 2018 needs a <strong>ladder safety system or PFAS</strong>, and every fixed ladder over 24 feet must have one by <strong>November 18, 2036</strong>.</li>\n<li>A ladder safety system uses a sleeve that locks onto a rail or cable if you fall. Be trained on the sleeve before you clip in.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A cage will not stop a fall. It only keeps you close to the ladder if you slip. Do not treat it as fall protection.</p>"}, {"heading": "Training under 1926.1060", "html": "<p>Section <strong>29 CFR 1926.1060</strong> requires the employer to provide a training program for each employee using ladders and stairways. The program must enable each worker to recognize ladder and stairway hazards and know the procedures that minimize them. Training must be given by a <strong>competent person</strong> and must cover, as applicable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nature of fall hazards in the work area.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for erecting, maintaining and disassembling the fall protection systems used.</li>\n<li>Proper construction, use, placement and care in handling of all ladders and stairways.</li>\n<li>The <strong>maximum intended load-carrying capacity</strong> of the ladders used.</li>\n<li>The standards in Subpart X themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Retraining is required as necessary so that workers keep the understanding the standard calls for. Unlike 1926.503, this section does not spell out a written certification record. The department documents it anyway, through this platform. Your completion record here, plus the ladder portion of your division's hands-on session, is your training record.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Fiberglass only, no exceptions. Line crews, substation technicians and meter technicians work around energized conductors, service drops, meter bases and padmount transformers every day. Keep the ladder and anything you carry outside the minimum approach distance, and never lean a ladder against a conductor, guy wire or pole-mounted enclosure.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Plant operators climb fixed ladders on clarifiers, digesters and elevated tanks, many of them over 24 feet. Know which ladders have a safety system and bring the right sleeve. Collection crews using portable ladders in manholes and vaults are also under confined space rules; the ladder is the escape route, so it must be secured and clear.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Signal and streetlight technicians near energized equipment follow the same fiberglass rule as Electric. Facilities staff working through a roof hatch must have the ladder extend 3 feet above the hatch or be secured with a grab bar. Forestry crews never set a ladder near overhead lines. Fleet mechanics working on truck bodies and plows should use a platform ladder or rolling stairs when a task takes more than a minute.</p>\n<p>For everyone: if the ladder in the truck is the wrong one, go get the right one. The trip back to the shop is cheaper than the fall.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Choose a ladder with a duty rating that covers you plus your tools and materials, and use nonconductive fiberglass anywhere there is an electrical hazard.", "Never use a metal ladder near energized lines or equipment.", "Inspect every ladder before use, and tag and remove any ladder with a structural defect so no one else climbs it.", "Set extension ladders at a 4 to 1 angle on firm, level footing, extend the rails at least 3 feet above the landing or secure the ladder with a grab device, and never set a ladder on boxes or barrels.", "Face the ladder, keep three points of contact, keep your belt buckle between the rails, and do not carry loads that could throw off your balance.", "Do not stand on the top step or cap of a stepladder, or above the highest standing level marked on an extension ladder.", "Fixed ladders with a climb of 24 feet or more need a cage, well or ladder safety system, and ladders installed or replaced since November 2018 must have a ladder safety system or personal fall arrest system.", "Ladder training under 1926.1060 is given by a competent person, covers hazards, setup, care and load capacity, and is documented on this platform."], "questions": [{"text": "A technician needs to replace a photocell on a streetlight pole next to an energized service drop. Which ladder is correct?", "options": ["A Type IA aluminum extension ladder because it is lighter", "A Type I wood ladder as long as it is dry", "A Type IA or IAA fiberglass extension ladder", "Any ladder, as long as a second worker foots it"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Near energized equipment the ladder must have nonconductive side rails, and the department uses Type IA and IAA ratings for field work. Metal ladders are never used near energized lines, and wet wood conducts too."}, {"text": "What must happen to a portable ladder found with a cracked side rail?", "options": ["Tag it 'Do Not Use' and remove it from service until repaired or discarded", "Wrap the crack with tape and finish the job", "Set it back on the truck rack and report it at the end of the week", "Keep using it for light-duty tasks only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(16) requires a ladder with structural defects to be immediately marked or tagged 'Do Not Use' and withdrawn from service until it is repaired to its original design or discarded."}, {"text": "An extension ladder is set so its top rests 20 feet up a wall. How far out from the wall should the feet be?", "options": ["2 feet", "10 feet", "8 feet", "5 feet"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The 4 to 1 rule puts the feet one quarter of the working length away from the wall. One quarter of 20 feet is 5 feet."}, {"text": "A ladder used to reach a roof is too short for the rails to extend 3 feet above the edge. What does the standard allow?", "options": ["Set the ladder on a sturdy box to gain the missing height", "Secure the ladder at the top to a rigid support and install a grab rail or grab device", "Use it as long as a worker foots the bottom", "Lean a second ladder beside it for a handhold"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(1) requires the rails to extend at least 3 feet above the landing, or the ladder must be secured at the top to a rigid support and a grab device provided. Boxes are never an acceptable base."}, {"text": "Which of these is prohibited by the standard?", "options": ["Facing the ladder while climbing", "Using a hand line to raise tools after reaching the work", "Placing a ladder on a barrel to reach a higher point", "Tying the top of the ladder off to a rigid support"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(6) requires stable and level footing, and the general industry rule in 1910.23(c) says directly that ladders are not placed on boxes, barrels or other unstable bases to gain height. The other choices are correct practices."}, {"text": "What does 'three points of contact' mean when climbing a ladder?", "options": ["Both feet on the same rung and one hand on a rail", "Two hands and one foot, or two feet and one hand, on the ladder at all times", "One hand, one foot and one tool in contact with the ladder", "Three rungs between you and the top of the ladder"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Three points of contact means two hands and one foot or two feet and one hand are always on the ladder. The standard also requires at least one hand to grasp the ladder at all times while climbing."}, {"text": "Where may a worker stand on a stepladder?", "options": ["On the top cap, as long as the spreaders are locked", "On the top step, if a second worker holds the ladder", "On any step, including the pail shelf, if the duty rating allows it", "On the steps below the top step, never on the top step or top cap"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(13) prohibits using the top or top step of a stepladder as a step. The cap is not a step, no matter who is holding the ladder."}, {"text": "A worker on an extension ladder needs to reach a little higher. Which choice follows the standard and the manufacturer's label?", "options": ["Climb down and get a longer ladder, since the label marks the highest standing level", "Stand on the top rung and hold the wall for balance", "Have a coworker extend the ladder while the worker stays on it", "Stand on the second rung from the top and lean out past the rail"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Workers must not stand above the highest standing level shown on the manufacturer's label, must not have a ladder moved or extended while they are on it, and must keep their body centered between the rails."}, {"text": "At what length of climb do the fixed ladder rules require a cage, well, ladder safety system or personal fall arrest system?", "options": ["10 feet", "16 feet", "24 feet", "50 feet"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Both 1926.1053(a)(19) and the general industry rule 1910.28(b)(9) use 24 feet as the trigger. Fixed ladders installed or replaced since November 2018 need a ladder safety system or PFAS rather than a cage alone."}, {"text": "Which topic must ladder training under 1926.1060 include?", "options": ["How to repair a cracked fiberglass rail in the field", "The maximum intended load-carrying capacity of the ladders used", "The purchase history of each ladder on the truck", "How to tie two ladders together to make a longer one"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.1060(a)(1)(iv) requires training on the maximum intended load-carrying capacity of ladders used, along with hazards, proper construction, use, placement and care. Field repairs and tying ladders together are prohibited practices."}]}, {"slug": "respiratory-protection", "title": "Respiratory Protection", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "standard_title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 6, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Respiratory Protection, 29 CFR 1910.134, sets the rules for selecting, fitting, using and caring for respirators whenever engineering controls cannot keep airborne hazards below safe levels. This course explains required versus voluntary use, the written program, medical evaluation, fit testing, seal checks, cartridge change schedules and IDLH atmospheres, with examples from fleet shops, concrete cutting, water and wastewater plants, sewers and older facilities. It also covers the program gaps that most often draw citations.", "objectives": ["Distinguish required respirator use from voluntary use and explain what Appendix D requires.", "Describe the elements of a written respiratory protection program and the role of the program administrator.", "Select the right type of NIOSH approved respirator, filter or cartridge for a given hazard using assigned protection factors.", "Explain why medical evaluation must come before fit testing and why fit testing is repeated at least annually.", "Perform a user seal check and identify conditions, such as facial hair, that defeat the seal.", "Recognize IDLH atmospheres and the respirators required for confined space and emergency entry."], "sections": [{"heading": "When respirators are required, and when use is voluntary", "html": "<p>Silica dust, welding fume, chlorine and sewer gas can damage your lungs or take your life, in minutes or over years. The standard puts <strong>engineering controls first</strong>: local exhaust, wet cutting and ventilation. When those cannot keep exposures below the <strong>permissible exposure limit (PEL)</strong>, the employer must provide respirators and run a full program.</p>\n<p>Respirator use falls into two categories:<br />\n- <strong>Required use.</strong> The employer has determined a respirator is necessary to protect your health. Every part of the standard applies.<br />\n- <strong>Voluntary use.</strong> Exposures are below the limits but an employee wants to wear a respirator anyway. The employer must make sure the respirator will not itself create a hazard and must give the employee <strong>Appendix D</strong>. If the voluntary respirator is more than a <strong>filtering facepiece</strong> (a disposable dust mask such as an N95), the employee also needs a medical evaluation and the respirator must be maintained under a written program.</p>\n<p>Appendix D tells voluntary users to follow the manufacturer instructions, use a NIOSH certified respirator only for what it is designed for, and never share it.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Respiratory Protection is on OSHA's most cited list every year. Inspectors rarely find a crew with no respirators. They find respirators handed out without the program behind them.</p>\n<p>The most common citations are:<br />\n- <strong>No medical evaluation.</strong> An employee was fit tested or assigned a respirator before a health care professional cleared them to wear one.<br />\n- <strong>No fit test.</strong> Tight fitting respirators were issued without an initial fit test, or the annual fit test lapsed.<br />\n- <strong>No written program.</strong> The department has respirators in the stockroom but no written program, or no one named to run it.</p>\n<p>Other frequent findings include no training, no hazard evaluation, voluntary users who never received Appendix D, and tight fitting respirators worn over beards.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written program and the administrator", "html": "<p>Wherever respirators are required, the employer must have a <strong>written respiratory protection program</strong> with worksite specific procedures, run by a trained <strong>program administrator</strong> who oversees it and evaluates how well it works.</p>\n<p>The program must cover:<br />\n- Respirator selection<br />\n- Medical evaluations<br />\n- Fit testing<br />\n- Routine and emergency use procedures<br />\n- Cleaning, storing, inspecting, repairing and discarding respirators<br />\n- Air quality for atmosphere supplying respirators<br />\n- Training in hazards and in respirator use and limitations<br />\n- Regular evaluation of the program</p>\n<p>The administrator should check with users regularly and review the program whenever work processes or chemicals change. The employer pays for required respirators, medical evaluations, fit tests and training.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hazard evaluation and respirator selection", "html": "<p>The employer must <strong>identify and evaluate</strong> the hazard before choosing a respirator: measure or estimate the exposure, identify the contaminant and its form, and check for oxygen deficiency. If exposure cannot be reasonably estimated, the atmosphere is treated as <strong>IDLH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Only <strong>NIOSH approved</strong> respirators may be used. There are two families:<br />\n- <strong>Air purifying respirators (APR)</strong> clean the air through filters or cartridges. They never add oxygen, so they cannot be used where oxygen is below 19.5 percent or in IDLH atmospheres.<br />\n- <strong>Atmosphere supplying respirators</strong> deliver clean air from a tank or compressor: SCBA and supplied air respirators.</p>\n<p><strong>Particulate filters</strong> are rated by letter and number. <strong>N</strong> means not oil resistant, <strong>R</strong> oil resistant and <strong>P</strong> oil proof. The number is the minimum efficiency: 95, 99 or 99.97 percent (labeled 100). An <strong>N95</strong> handles most dusts. A <strong>P100</strong> is the choice for silica, lead, asbestos and welding fume. <strong>Chemical cartridges</strong> are color coded by contaminant. Match the cartridge to the SDS.</p>\n<p>The <strong>assigned protection factor (APF)</strong> is how much a properly fitted respirator reduces exposure. A half mask APR has an APF of 10, a full facepiece APR 50 and a pressure demand SCBA 10,000. Pick a respirator with an APF high enough for the measured exposure.</p>"}, {"heading": "Medical evaluation, fit testing and the seal", "html": "<p>Wearing a respirator is work for your heart and lungs. Before anyone is fit tested or wears a required respirator, they must have a <strong>medical evaluation</strong>. It begins with the Appendix C questionnaire, reviewed by a physician or other licensed health care professional. The employer is told only whether the person can wear the respirator and under what limits.</p>\n<p>After medical clearance comes the <strong>fit test</strong>. Every tight fitting respirator, including N95 filtering facepieces, must be fit tested before first use, at least <strong>every 12 months</strong>, whenever a different make, model or size is used, and whenever a change in the face could affect the fit. <strong>Qualitative</strong> fit testing uses a test agent such as Bitrex or saccharin; you pass if you cannot taste or smell it. <strong>Quantitative</strong> fit testing uses an instrument to measure leakage.</p>\n<p>Fit testing proves the respirator can seal. The <strong>user seal check</strong> proves it did seal <strong>this time</strong>. Do one every time you put a tight fitting respirator on. Positive pressure: cover the exhalation valve and breathe out gently; the facepiece should bulge with no leaks. Negative pressure: cover the inlets and breathe in; the facepiece should collapse and stay collapsed.</p>\n<p><strong>Facial hair</strong> between the sealing surface and the skin is not allowed with a tight fitting respirator. Stubble is enough to break the seal. Employees who cannot shave need a loose fitting PAPR hood or helmet.</p>"}, {"heading": "Care, cleaning, storage and cartridge changes", "html": "<p><strong>Clean and disinfect</strong> reusable respirators as often as needed to keep them sanitary: after each day for one user, after each use when shared, and after every fit test.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspect</strong> before each use and during cleaning for cracks, tears, stiff valves and missing parts. Emergency respirators and SCBA are inspected monthly.</p>\n<p><strong>Store</strong> respirators away from damage, contamination, dust, sunlight, extreme temperatures and moisture. A sealed bag in a clean locker works. A dusty dashboard does not.</p>\n<p>Chemical cartridges do not last forever, and smell is not a reliable warning. The employer must set a <strong>change schedule</strong> based on objective information, such as manufacturer service life data and the measured exposure, or use cartridges with an <strong>end of service life indicator (ESLI)</strong>. Replace particulate filters when breathing becomes harder.</p>\n<p>Employees must be allowed to leave the work area to wash the facepiece, replace a cartridge or filter, or whenever they smell or taste the contaminant.</p>"}, {"heading": "IDLH atmospheres and confined spaces", "html": "<p>An atmosphere <strong>immediately dangerous to life or health (IDLH)</strong> could kill, permanently harm or disable your ability to escape. Oxygen below 19.5 percent is IDLH. So is a manhole with hydrogen sulfide, a chlorine room after a leak, or any space where the hazard is unknown.</p>\n<p>IDLH entry requires a <strong>full facepiece pressure demand SCBA</strong> or a pressure demand supplied air respirator with an auxiliary self contained air supply. At least one trained standby person must be outside, in contact with the entrant, equipped with the same respirator and retrieval gear, and must call for help before entering to rescue.</p>\n<p>For Public Works the IDLH rule meets the confined space rule most often in <strong>wastewater and stormwater work</strong>: manholes, wet wells, lift stations and vaults. Hydrogen sulfide deadens the sense of smell at dangerous levels, and methane displaces oxygen. Air purifying respirators, including cartridge masks sold for sewer gas, are <strong>never</strong> acceptable for entry into an atmosphere that is or may be IDLH. Test the air before and during entry, ventilate, and follow the confined space program. Operators who respond to a chlorine leak need SCBA, not a cartridge respirator, because leak concentrations are unknown.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> The fleet shop is where most required respirator use happens. <strong>Welding fume</strong> on galvanized or painted metal, <strong>sandblasting</strong> and spray painting all call for an exposure evaluation and often a respirator. Streets and sign shop crews who <strong>cut, grind or core concrete or asphalt</strong> are exposed to <strong>respirable crystalline silica</strong>. Wet cutting and vacuum shrouds come first. Facilities staff disturbing insulation or floor tile in <strong>older buildings</strong> must assume <strong>asbestos</strong> until a survey proves otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants face <strong>chlorine</strong>, sulfur dioxide and ammonia in daily work and SCBA level hazards during leaks. Collections crews face <strong>sewer gases</strong> in manholes and lift stations. Forestry crews applying <strong>pesticides and herbicides</strong> must use the respirator shown on the EPA product label.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line and substation crews meet respirator hazards less often: cutting concrete pads and vault lids, battery room acid mist, lead paint on older structures and SF6 by-products after a breaker fault. Underground vaults carry the same gas hazards as a manhole.</p>\n<p>In every division the sequence is the same: evaluate the hazard, control it at the source, then choose a respirator. Medical clearance comes before fit testing, fit testing before use, and a seal check before every use.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Engineering controls come first, and respirators are required only when those controls cannot keep exposures below the permissible exposure limit.", "Voluntary users of filtering facepieces must receive the Appendix D information, and voluntary users of any other respirator also need a medical evaluation and basic maintenance rules.", "A written, worksite specific program run by a trained program administrator is required wherever respirators are required.", "Only NIOSH approved respirators may be used, matched to the hazard with the right filter or cartridge and an assigned protection factor high enough for the exposure.", "Medical evaluation comes before fit testing, and fit testing comes before first use and at least every 12 months after that.", "A user seal check is done every time a tight fitting respirator is put on, and facial hair in the seal area is not allowed.", "Air purifying respirators never add oxygen and are never used in IDLH atmospheres, including manholes, wet wells and chlorine leaks, where SCBA or supplied air with escape bottle is required.", "The most common citations, no medical evaluation, no fit test and no written program, all come from handing out respirators without the program behind them."], "questions": [{"text": "An employee wants to wear a disposable N95 while sweeping the shop, even though exposure is below the permissible limit. What must the employer do?", "options": ["Nothing, because voluntary use is outside the standard", "Provide the Appendix D information and make sure the respirator will not itself create a hazard", "Require a quantitative fit test before the employee can wear it", "Prohibit the use, because respirators are allowed only when required"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Voluntary use of a filtering facepiece requires the employer to give the employee Appendix D and to confirm the respirator will not create a hazard. Medical evaluation and a written program are required for voluntary use only when the respirator is more than a filtering facepiece."}, {"text": "What is the correct order of steps before an employee uses a tight fitting respirator for the first time?", "options": ["Fit test, then medical evaluation, then user seal check", "User seal check, then fit test, then medical evaluation", "Medical evaluation, then fit test, then user seal check each time it is worn", "Medical evaluation and fit test are both optional if the employee has worn respirators before"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires medical clearance before fit testing, fit testing before first use, and a user seal check every time the respirator is put on."}, {"text": "How often must fit testing be repeated for an employee who continues to use the same respirator?", "options": ["At least every 12 months", "Every 6 months", "Every 2 years", "Only when the employee changes jobs"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Fit testing is required before first use, at least annually, and whenever the respirator model or size changes or a change in the wearer's face could affect the fit."}, {"text": "Which filter is the right choice for respirable crystalline silica, lead or asbestos work where the highest level of particulate filtration is needed?", "options": ["N95", "R95", "Organic vapor cartridge", "P100"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A P100 filter is the most efficient particulate class (99.97 percent) and is oil proof, making it the standard choice for the most toxic particulates. An organic vapor cartridge does not filter particles."}, {"text": "A collections crew needs to enter a manhole where the gas meter reads 17 percent oxygen. What respirator is required?", "options": ["A half mask with acid gas cartridges", "A full facepiece air purifying respirator with P100 filters", "A full facepiece pressure demand SCBA, or supplied air with an auxiliary escape cylinder", "An N95 filtering facepiece with a user seal check"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Oxygen below 19.5 percent is IDLH. Air purifying respirators cannot add oxygen, so IDLH entry requires pressure demand SCBA or a pressure demand supplied air respirator with an auxiliary self contained air supply."}, {"text": "What is the assigned protection factor of a properly fitted half mask air purifying respirator?", "options": ["5", "10", "50", "1,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A half mask APR has an APF of 10, meaning it should reduce exposure to one tenth of the airborne concentration when properly fitted and used. A full facepiece APR has an APF of 50."}, {"text": "Which statement about facial hair and respirators is correct?", "options": ["A short, neatly trimmed beard is acceptable under a half mask if the fit test is passed", "Facial hair matters only for full facepiece respirators", "Facial hair is acceptable as long as the employee does a user seal check", "Facial hair that comes between the sealing surface and the face is not allowed with any tight fitting respirator"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standard prohibits tight fitting respirators on employees with facial hair that interferes with the seal or the valves. Employees who cannot shave need a loose fitting hood or helmet style PAPR."}, {"text": "What must a cartridge change schedule be based on?", "options": ["The user's sense of smell or taste for the contaminant", "Objective information such as manufacturer service life data and measured exposure, or an end of service life indicator", "A fixed rule of one new cartridge per month", "Whether the cartridge looks dirty"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Smell is not a reliable warning for many chemicals, so the employer must base the change schedule on objective data or use cartridges with an end of service life indicator."}, {"text": "Who is told the full results of an employee's respirator medical evaluation?", "options": ["The employee, who receives a written recommendation, while the employer is told only whether the employee can wear the respirator and any limits", "The supervisor, who keeps a copy in the employee's personnel file", "The program administrator and the safety committee", "The employer, who must post the results with the written program"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The health care professional gives the employee a written recommendation and tells the employer only whether the employee is medically able to use the respirator and under what limits. The medical details stay confidential."}, {"text": "A streets crew is dry cutting a concrete curb with a gas powered saw. What should happen first under the respiratory protection approach?", "options": ["Issue N95 respirators to the crew and continue dry cutting", "Issue P100 respirators without a fit test because the job is short", "Switch to wet cutting or a vacuum shroud to control the silica dust at the source, then evaluate whether a respirator is still needed", "Have the crew hold their breath while making the cut"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires engineering controls such as wet cutting or dust collection first. A respirator is selected only after the remaining exposure has been evaluated, and then only after medical clearance and fit testing."}]}, {"slug": "powered-industrial-trucks", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (Forklifts)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "standard_title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 7, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Forklifts, telehandlers and powered pallet jacks move heavy loads in public works warehouses, parts rooms, material yards and plants, and they tip over, strike pedestrians and drop loads when operated carelessly. This course covers the training, evaluation and certification rules in 29 CFR 1910.178, daily inspections, the stability triangle and load handling, safe travel and parking, and refueling and charging. The content supports the hands-on training and workplace evaluation that every operator must complete.", "objectives": ["Identify which equipment counts as a powered industrial truck and which does not.", "Describe the three parts of operator training and when refresher training and reevaluation are required.", "Perform a pre-operation inspection and explain why a defective truck is removed from service.", "Explain the stability triangle, load center and capacity plate, and how they relate to tipovers.", "Apply safe practices for traveling, pedestrians, ramps, blind corners, raising people and parking.", "Follow the rules for refueling, propane cylinder handling and battery charging areas."], "sections": [{"heading": "What counts as a powered industrial truck", "html": "<p>29 CFR 1910.178 covers <strong>powered industrial trucks</strong>: mobile, power-propelled trucks used to carry, push, pull, lift, stack or tier material. In public works that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counterbalanced forklifts</strong>, sit-down and stand-up, electric or engine powered.</li>\n<li><strong>Rough terrain forklifts</strong> and <strong>telehandlers</strong> used in material yards and at plants.</li>\n<li><strong>Powered pallet jacks</strong> and walkie stackers, whether ridden or walked behind.</li>\n<li><strong>Order pickers</strong> and other trucks that lift the operator with the load.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The standard does <strong>not</strong> cover vehicles intended for earth moving or over-the-road hauling. Loaders, backhoes, skid steers and dump trucks fall under other rules even when they happen to move a pallet. Manual pallet jacks with no motor are also outside the standard.</p>\n<p>Every truck has a <strong>designation</strong> (such as LP, LPS, E, ES, EE, EX, G, GS, D or DS) based on its power source and built-in safeguards. Only trucks with the right designation may operate in hazardous locations where flammable gases, vapors or dusts may be present, such as digester and gas handling areas at treatment plants.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Powered industrial trucks appear on OSHA's most cited list every year, and the reasons are consistent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operators not trained or evaluated.</strong> People who drive a forklift a few times a month are often never formally trained, or were trained years ago and never reevaluated.</li>\n<li><strong>No certification records.</strong> Training happened, but no record shows the operator's name, the training and evaluation dates and who conducted them.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing refresher training</strong> after an accident, a near miss or an observed unsafe act.</li>\n<li><strong>Defective trucks kept in service.</strong> Worn forks, dead horns, bad brakes, leaking hydraulics and missing seat belts on trucks still in daily use.</li>\n<li><strong>No daily inspection.</strong> Trucks are started and driven without anyone checking them.</li>\n<li><strong>Unsafe operation.</strong> Speeding, carrying loads high, riders on the forks and forks left raised when parked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works departments are exposed because forklift use is often occasional, spread across many sites and done by staff whose main job is something else.</p>"}, {"heading": "Operator training, evaluation and certification", "html": "<p>No one may operate a powered industrial truck until they have completed training and been evaluated. Trainees may operate only under the direct supervision of a trained, experienced person and where it does not endanger others.</p>\n<p>Training has <strong>three required parts</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formal instruction</strong>, such as classroom time, videos, written material or online modules like this course.</li>\n<li><strong>Practical training</strong>, where a trainer demonstrates and the operator performs exercises on the actual type of truck.</li>\n<li><strong>An evaluation of the operator's performance in the workplace</strong>, handling real loads in the real environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training covers truck-related topics (controls, capacity, stability, inspection, refueling) and workplace-related topics (surfaces, ramps, pedestrians, narrow aisles, hazardous locations and the loads handled), and it must match the truck types the operator will use.</p>\n<p>Each operator's performance must be <strong>evaluated at least once every three years</strong>. <strong>Refresher training</strong> and a new evaluation are required sooner when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the operator is involved in an accident or near miss,</li>\n<li>the operator is observed operating unsafely,</li>\n<li>an evaluation shows the operator is not driving safely,</li>\n<li>the operator is assigned to a different type of truck, or</li>\n<li>workplace conditions change in a way that affects safe operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The employer must <strong>certify</strong> that each operator has been trained and evaluated. The record includes the operator's name, the date of training, the date of evaluation and the identity of the person who did the training or evaluation. Under federal child labor rules, operators must be at least <strong>18 years old</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "Pre-operation inspection", "html": "<p>The standard requires every truck to be examined <strong>before it is placed in service and at least daily</strong>, and after each shift when it runs around the clock. In practice, check it before every shift using the checklist for the truck type. A typical walkaround covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>tires, wheels and lug nuts,</li>\n<li>forks for cracks, bends, wear at the heel and a secure locking pin,</li>\n<li>mast, chains and hoses for damage and leaks,</li>\n<li>fluid levels, and fuel or battery condition,</li>\n<li>overhead guard, load backrest and seat belt,</li>\n<li>data plate present and readable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then a seated check with the truck running: horn, lights, backup alarm, brakes, parking brake, steering, lift and tilt controls, and gauges.</p>\n<p>If any defect affects safety, the truck is <strong>taken out of service</strong> and tagged until it is repaired. A truck that is defective or in any way unsafe does not run, and repairs are done only by authorized personnel.</p>"}, {"heading": "Stability, load center and capacity", "html": "<p>A forklift stays upright as long as its combined center of gravity stays inside the <strong>stability triangle</strong>, the area between the two front wheels and the pivot point of the rear axle. Lifting a load, tilting the mast forward, turning sharply or driving on a slope all move the center of gravity toward the edge of that triangle. Push it outside and the truck tips.</p>\n<p>The <strong>capacity plate</strong> (data plate) on every truck states how much it can lift at a rated <strong>load center</strong>, usually 24 inches from the face of the forks, at a stated height. Capacity drops when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the load center is farther out because the load is long or sits away from the backrest,</li>\n<li>the mast is raised high or tilted forward,</li>\n<li>attachments such as a drum clamp, jib or rotator are added.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Never exceed the plate, and never add counterweight to lift more. Modifications and attachments need the manufacturer's approval and an updated plate.</p>\n<p>Handle loads this way:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Center the load, seat it against the backrest and tilt the mast back slightly.</li>\n<li>Travel with the forks low, about 4 to 6 inches off the floor.</li>\n<li>Raise and lower only when stopped.</li>\n<li>Avoid sudden starts, stops and turns, especially with the load raised.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Traveling, pedestrians, ramps and raising people", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Speed.</strong> Drive at a speed that lets you stop safely, and slow down on wet or uneven surfaces and in congested areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Pedestrians.</strong> Pedestrians have the right of way. Keep a safe distance, make eye contact and never let anyone walk or stand under raised forks.</li>\n<li><strong>Blind corners, doorways and aisle crossings.</strong> Slow down, <strong>sound the horn</strong> and look in the direction of travel.</li>\n<li><strong>Visibility.</strong> If the load blocks your view, travel in reverse with the load trailing, except when going up a ramp.</li>\n<li><strong>Ramps and grades.</strong> With a load, drive <strong>with the load upgrade</strong>: forward going up and in reverse going down. Travel straight up and down, never turn on a grade, and keep the forks low with the mast tilted back.</li>\n<li><strong>Seat belt.</strong> Wear it every time on a sit-down truck. In a tipover, stay in the seat, hold the wheel, brace your feet and lean away from the fall. Jumping is how operators get crushed.</li>\n<li><strong>Riders and raising people.</strong> No passengers. Never lift a person on the forks, a pallet or a bucket. People may be elevated only on a <strong>manufacturer-approved work platform</strong> secured to the forks, with the operator at the controls, fall protection for the person on the platform and the truck not moving.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Refueling, propane, charging and parking", "html": "<p><strong>Refueling and charging areas</strong> must be set up for the purpose: adequate ventilation, no open flames or smoking, fire protection nearby and spill control. Engines are shut off during refueling.</p>\n<p><strong>Propane (LP) cylinders.</strong> Close the cylinder valve before changing cylinders. Wear eye protection and gloves because escaping propane can cause frostbite. Check the O-ring and connections, and seat the cylinder with the relief valve pointing up. Store spare cylinders outdoors or in an approved cage, away from exits and heat.</p>\n<p><strong>Battery charging.</strong> Park, set the brake and turn the truck off before connecting the charger. Keep the vent caps in place, charge with the compartment open, and keep sparks and flames away because charging produces hydrogen gas. Know where the eyewash is before you need it.</p>\n<p><strong>Parking an unattended truck</strong> means forks fully lowered, controls in neutral, parking brake set and power shut off. A truck is unattended when the operator is 25 feet or more away, or whenever the operator cannot see it. Even within 25 feet and in view, the load goes down, the controls go to neutral and the brake is set. Never park where you block an aisle, exit, fire equipment or electrical panel.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Transformers, cable reels, pole hardware and meter stock are heavy, awkward loads. Use the right attachment for reels, verify the transformer weight against the capacity plate at the real load center, and keep the load low on the way out of the yard. Yard surfaces are often gravel and uneven, so slow down.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Telehandlers at treatment plants move chemical totes, pump assemblies and spare parts across rough ground and into tight pump rooms. Confirm the truck's designation is allowed in any area with methane or flammable vapors. Powered pallet jacks at the scale house and plant storerooms are covered too, including the training rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> The central warehouse and parts rooms see the most forklift traffic, often mixed with walk-in customers from other divisions. Mark pedestrian lanes, sound the horn at aisle ends and keep forks down when staged. Sign shops handle long bundles of posts and sheeting that push the load center out. Facilities crews using powered pallet jacks at community buildings still need training, inspection and a certification record.</p>\n<p>Across all three divisions: if you were never trained and evaluated on that type of truck, you do not drive it.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Powered industrial trucks include forklifts, telehandlers, powered pallet jacks and order pickers, but not earth moving equipment or manual pallet jacks.", "No one operates a truck without formal instruction, practical training and a workplace evaluation, and operators must be at least 18.", "Operators are reevaluated at least every three years, and sooner after an accident, near miss, unsafe operation, a change in truck type or a change in conditions.", "The employer keeps a certification record with the operator's name, training date, evaluation date and the trainer or evaluator's identity.", "Inspect the truck before every shift and take any truck with a safety defect out of service until it is repaired.", "Stay inside the stability triangle: respect the capacity plate and load center, keep the load low and tilted back, and drive with the load upgrade on ramps.", "Wear the seat belt, sound the horn at blind corners, give pedestrians the right of way and never lift people without an approved platform.", "Park with forks down, controls in neutral, brake set and power off, and handle propane and battery charging only in areas set up for it."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of these is covered by 29 CFR 1910.178?", "options": ["A skid steer loader moving a pallet of salt", "A manual pallet jack in the parts room", "A battery-powered walkie pallet jack at the facilities storeroom", "A dump truck hauling road gravel"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Powered pallet jacks are powered industrial trucks and fall under the standard. Earth moving equipment, over-the-road trucks and manual pallet jacks do not."}, {"text": "What are the three required parts of powered industrial truck operator training?", "options": ["Formal instruction, practical training and an evaluation of performance in the workplace", "A written test, a driving test at a training center and a license from the state", "Watching a video, signing an acknowledgment and completing a probation period", "Classroom time, a physical exam and a supervisor's recommendation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires formal instruction, practical training on the truck and an evaluation of the operator's performance in the actual workplace before operating without supervision."}, {"text": "How often must an operator's performance be evaluated if no accidents or changes occur?", "options": ["Every year", "At least once every three years", "Every five years", "Only when first hired"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard requires an evaluation of each operator's performance at least once every three years, with refresher training sooner when specific events occur."}, {"text": "An operator has a near miss when a load slides off the forks. What does the standard require?", "options": ["A written warning placed in the personnel file", "A new state operator's license", "Nothing, as long as no one was hurt", "Refresher training and a new evaluation"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An accident or near miss is one of the events that triggers refresher training and reevaluation, along with unsafe operation, a new truck type and changed workplace conditions."}, {"text": "During the pre-shift check, the horn on a forklift does not work. What should the operator do?", "options": ["Take the truck out of service and tag it until the horn is repaired", "Drive slowly and shout at blind corners until the next scheduled service", "Use the truck only in the yard where there are fewer pedestrians", "Note the problem on the checklist and continue the shift"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Any defect that affects safe operation means the truck is removed from service until it is repaired. The horn is required for warning pedestrians at blind corners and doorways."}, {"text": "Which action moves a forklift's center of gravity toward the edge of the stability triangle?", "options": ["Lowering the forks to travel height", "Tilting the mast back with the load seated against the backrest", "Raising a load high and tilting the mast forward", "Driving slowly in a straight line on level ground"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Raising the load and tilting forward shift the combined center of gravity up and forward, toward the front edge of the triangle, where the truck can tip forward."}, {"text": "You are driving a loaded forklift down a ramp. How should you travel?", "options": ["Forward, with the forks raised to clear the slope", "In reverse, with the load upgrade and forks low", "Forward, turning slightly to slow the descent", "In reverse, with the mast tilted forward"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "On a grade the load always points upgrade. Going down, that means backing down with the forks low and the mast tilted back."}, {"text": "A coworker needs to reach a light fixture 12 feet up. What is the only acceptable way to lift them with a forklift?", "options": ["Standing on a pallet strapped to the forks", "Standing on the forks while holding the mast", "Sitting in a bucket chained to the carriage", "On a manufacturer-approved work platform secured to the forks, with the operator at the controls"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "People may be elevated only on an approved work platform that is secured to the truck, with the operator staying at the controls and the person on the platform protected from falling."}, {"text": "An operator walks 40 feet from a forklift to check a parts bin. What is required before leaving the truck?", "options": ["Only that the engine is left idling so it restarts easily", "Forks lowered, controls in neutral, parking brake set and power shut off", "Forks raised to keep them off the floor and the horn tapped twice", "The key left in the ignition so a coworker can move the truck"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A truck is unattended when the operator is 25 feet or more away or out of sight of it. An unattended truck must have the forks fully lowered, controls neutral, brake set and power off."}, {"text": "Which item must be in the employer's operator certification record?", "options": ["The operator's driver's license number", "The serial number of each truck the operator has driven", "The operator's insurance carrier", "The identity of the person who performed the training or evaluation"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The certification must include the operator's name, the training date, the evaluation date and the identity of the person who conducted the training or evaluation."}]}, {"slug": "fall-protection-training", "title": "Fall Protection: Training Requirements", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "standard_title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 8, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 25, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Owning fall protection equipment is not enough. OSHA requires every worker who might be exposed to a fall hazard to be trained by a competent person, and it requires the employer to prove that training happened with a written record. This course explains who must be trained, what the training must cover, when retraining is required, and how this platform and your hands-on sessions together create the record OSHA expects to see.", "objectives": ["State who must receive fall protection training under 1926.503 and what the training program must enable workers to do.", "List the subject areas the training must cover, from recognizing fall hazards to the correct use of each protection system.", "Explain the difference between a competent person and a qualified person and describe the role each plays in fall protection.", "Identify the three items that must appear on the written training certification record.", "Recognize the three situations that require retraining and apply them to real changes on a public works crew.", "Describe how this course, the platform record and your hands-on session together document compliance."], "sections": [{"heading": "Who must be trained", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(a)(1)</strong> requires the employer to provide a training program for <strong>each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards</strong>. The word is 'might.' You do not have to work at height every day to be covered. If your job could put you on a roof, a bridge deck, a tank top, an open excavation edge or an aerial lift even once a year, you must be trained before that exposure happens.</p>\n<p>The program has two goals. It must enable each employee to <strong>recognize the hazards of falling</strong>, and it must train each employee in the <strong>procedures to follow to minimize those hazards</strong>. Knowing that a harness exists is not the same as knowing when it is required, how to inspect it, where to anchor it and how far you will fall if it has to work.</p>\n<p>Our department renews this course every 12 months so the record never goes stale.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>The training standard usually gets cited in the same inspection as the general requirements standard, 1926.501. An inspector sees a worker on an edge without protection, asks when fall protection is required and how the equipment is used, and gets a vague answer. Then the inspector asks for the training record. There is none, or it is years old and does not cover the equipment in use.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(a)(1)</strong> No training program at all for exposed workers.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(1)</strong> No written certification record, or a record missing the name, date or signature.</li>\n<li><strong>(c)</strong> Failure to retrain after a change in equipment or after a worker showed they did not understand the system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fix is a program that covers the required topics, delivered by a competent person, with a dated and signed record for every exposed worker. That is what this course and your hands-on session are built to produce.</p>"}, {"heading": "What the training must cover", "html": "<p>Under <strong>1926.503(a)(2)</strong>, each employee must be trained, as needed for their work, in these areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>nature of fall hazards</strong> in the work area: open edges, holes, leading edges, excavations, roofs, tanks and structures.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for <strong>erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting</strong> the fall protection systems to be used.</li>\n<li>The <strong>use and operation</strong> of guardrail systems, personal fall arrest systems, safety net systems, warning line systems, safety monitoring systems, controlled access zones and any other protection to be used.</li>\n<li>Each employee's <strong>role in a safety monitoring system</strong>, when one is used.</li>\n<li>Limits on using mechanical equipment during roofing work on low-slope roofs.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for the <strong>handling and storage of equipment and materials</strong> and for the <strong>erection of overhead protection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each employee's <strong>role in a fall protection plan</strong>, when a plan is used instead of conventional systems.</li>\n<li>The standards in <strong>Subpart M</strong> itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The phrase 'as needed' matters. A fleet mechanic who only uses the shop's overhead fall arrest rail does not need to know how to rig a safety net. A bridge crew that erects temporary guardrails does. Training has to match the systems your crew actually uses, which is why the hands-on portion is done by division.</p>"}, {"heading": "Competent person and qualified person", "html": "<p>OSHA's construction standards use two terms that are often mixed up. The difference affects who can train you and who can design your anchor.</p>\n<p>A <strong>competent person</strong> is someone who can identify existing and predictable fall hazards in the work area <strong>and</strong> has the <strong>authority to take prompt corrective action</strong> to eliminate them. Both parts are required. A crew member who can spot a missing guardrail but cannot stop the job is not a competent person. The standard requires fall protection training to be given <strong>by a competent person qualified in the subject areas</strong> being taught. The competent person also inspects fall arrest equipment that has caught a fall before it returns to service, serves as the monitor in a safety monitoring system, and supervises the implementation of a fall protection plan where one is used.</p>\n<p>A <strong>qualified person</strong> has a recognized degree, certificate or professional standing, or extensive knowledge, training and experience, and has demonstrated the ability to solve problems in the subject. A qualified person designs engineered anchorages that do not meet the 5,000 pound rule on their own, certifies horizontal lifeline systems and prepares fall protection plans.</p>\n<p>One person can hold both roles, but the roles come from knowledge and authority, not job title. Your division safety lead will tell you who serves as competent person on your crew.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written certification record", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(b)</strong> requires the employer to verify training with a <strong>written certification record</strong>. The record must contain three things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>name or other identity</strong> of the employee trained.</li>\n<li>The <strong>date or dates</strong> of the training.</li>\n<li>The <strong>signature</strong> of the person who conducted the training, or the signature of the employer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the employer relies on training a worker received from a previous employer, the record must show the date the employer <strong>determined that prior training was adequate</strong>. A new hire's old wallet card is not a record until a competent person here has reviewed what it covered and signed off.</p>\n<p>The employer must keep the <strong>latest</strong> certification. A record that cannot be found is treated the same as training that never happened.</p>"}, {"heading": "When retraining is required", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(c)</strong> requires retraining whenever the employer has reason to believe a trained worker <strong>does not have the understanding and skill</strong> the standard requires. The standard lists three situations, and the list is not meant to be complete:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Changes in the workplace</strong> make previous training obsolete. Examples: the plant adds a new elevated walkway, a crew is assigned to a bridge project for the first time, or a facility gets a new roof hatch and skylights.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes in the types of fall protection systems or equipment</strong> make previous training obsolete. Examples: the division switches from shock-absorbing lanyards to self-retracting lifelines, buys a new style of harness, or installs a horizontal lifeline on the water tower.</li>\n<li><strong>Inadequacies in a worker's knowledge or use</strong> of fall protection show the worker has not retained the understanding or skill. Examples: a supervisor sees someone tied off to a guardrail, wearing a harness with loose leg straps, or working an edge with the gear still in the truck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Retraining is not punishment. It means the system has a gap, and the fix is more training, documented with a new dated and signed record.</p>"}, {"heading": "How this course and the annual platform satisfy the record", "html": "<p>This platform records your name, the date you completed each course, your quiz score and the attestation of the department safety officer who serves as the employer's signature. That gives the three items 1926.503(b) requires, kept as the latest record and available to an inspector on request.</p>\n<p>Online training alone does not finish the job. OSHA has said in interpretation letters that computer-based training can be part of a program, but it must be paired with <strong>hands-on practice</strong> and a chance to <strong>ask a trainer questions</strong>. The hands-on portion is where you put on a harness, inspect a lanyard, set up the guardrails your crew uses, and show your competent person you can do it. Your division schedules that session, and its date and the trainer's signature are entered on your record next to this course.</p>\n<p>If you change divisions, start using a different system, or your supervisor sees a problem, you will be assigned this course again along with a new hands-on session. If you are handed equipment you have not been trained on, say so before you use it.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line crews and substation technicians are trained on bucket truck fall protection and pole climbing under 1910.269 and Subpart V, and that covers much of what 1926.503 requires. The gap is usually in work that does not feel like line work: meter technicians on commercial rooftops and substation crews on steel during a construction project. Those exposures need to show up on the same record.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Plant operators, collection crews and solid waste staff rotate through many sites and systems: tank hatches, clarifier bridges, vault entries and transfer station edges. When a crew is assigned to a facility it has not worked before, that is a change in the workplace. Ask your supervisor whether retraining is needed before the first shift, not after.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Streets and bridge crews erect and take down temporary guardrails, so their training must include erecting, maintaining and disassembling those systems, not just using them. Signal technicians and forestry crews tie off in buckets. Facilities staff on roofs need to know warning lines and roof anchor use. Fleet mechanics who use the shop fall arrest rail need the retraining trigger explained: a new trolley or lanyard type means a new session.</p>\n<p>In every division, the supervisor's job is to notice the triggers and request retraining. The worker's job is to speak up when a system is unfamiliar. The record is what proves both jobs were done.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Every employee who might be exposed to a fall hazard must be trained before that exposure, not just workers who are at height every day.", "Training must enable workers to recognize fall hazards and to follow the procedures that minimize them, including erecting, inspecting and using the systems their crew actually uses.", "Fall protection training must be delivered by a competent person who is qualified in the subject areas being taught.", "A competent person can identify hazards and has authority to correct them; a qualified person has the credentials or experience to design and engineer solutions.", "The written certification record must contain the employee's name, the training date and the signature of the trainer or employer, and the latest record must be kept.", "Retraining is required when the workplace changes, when the types of systems or equipment change, or when a worker shows they have not retained the knowledge or skill.", "This platform supplies the written record, and the hands-on session with your competent person supplies the practice and the chance to ask questions."], "questions": [{"text": "Under 1926.503, which employees must receive fall protection training?", "options": ["Only employees who work at height on a daily basis", "Each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards", "Only supervisors and designated competent persons", "Only employees who use personal fall arrest systems"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard covers each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards. Occasional exposure counts, and the training must happen before the exposure."}, {"text": "What must the training program enable each employee to do?", "options": ["Design anchorages that meet the 5,000 pound requirement", "Write the department's fall protection plan", "Pass a written test on Subpart M once every five years", "Recognize fall hazards and follow the procedures that minimize them"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(a)(1) states the program must enable workers to recognize the hazards of falling and train them in the procedures to follow to minimize those hazards."}, {"text": "Which of the following is a required training topic under 1926.503(a)(2)?", "options": ["Correct procedures for erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting the fall protection systems to be used", "The purchase price and warranty terms of fall protection equipment", "How to file an OSHA complaint about a contractor", "The history of the Subpart M rulemaking"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard specifically lists erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting the systems to be used, along with the nature of fall hazards and the use and operation of each type of system."}, {"text": "Who is permitted to conduct fall protection training under the standard?", "options": ["Any employee with at least one year of field experience", "The equipment vendor's sales representative", "A competent person qualified in the subject areas being taught", "Any licensed professional engineer"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(a)(2) requires training by a competent person qualified in the areas covered. Experience or a license alone does not meet that requirement."}, {"text": "Which three items must appear on the written certification record?", "options": ["Employee name, job title and division", "Employee name or identity, training date, and signature of the trainer or employer", "Training date, quiz score and equipment serial numbers", "Trainer name, course length and location of training"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(b)(1) requires the name or other identity of the employee, the date or dates of training, and the signature of the person who conducted the training or of the employer."}, {"text": "Which training certification must the employer maintain?", "options": ["The latest certification for each employee", "Every certification ever issued, going back to hire date", "Only certifications issued within the last 30 days", "Only certifications for employees who use personal fall arrest systems"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires the latest training certification to be maintained. Older records may be kept, but the current one is what must be available."}, {"text": "A division replaces its shock-absorbing lanyards with self-retracting lifelines. What does the standard require?", "options": ["Nothing, because both devices connect to the same harness D-ring", "A note in the equipment log but no training change", "Retraining only for workers who ask for it", "Retraining, because a change in the type of equipment makes previous training obsolete"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(c)(2) lists changes in the types of fall protection systems or equipment as a retraining trigger. Self-retracting lifelines have different inspection, anchoring and clearance rules than lanyards."}, {"text": "A supervisor sees a trained worker tied off to a guardrail. What does the standard call for?", "options": ["A written warning in the personnel file", "Removing the worker from fall protection duties permanently", "Retraining, because the worker has shown an inadequacy in knowledge or use of the system", "Replacing the guardrail with a stronger one"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(c)(3) requires retraining when a worker's knowledge or use of fall protection shows they have not retained the required understanding or skill. A guardrail is not an approved anchorage."}, {"text": "What is the key difference between a competent person and a qualified person?", "options": ["A competent person can identify fall hazards and has authority to correct them; a qualified person has the credentials or extensive experience to design and engineer solutions", "A competent person must be a professional engineer; a qualified person must be a supervisor", "A qualified person conducts training; a competent person only inspects harnesses", "There is no difference; OSHA uses the terms interchangeably"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The competent person's role rests on hazard recognition plus authority to act. The qualified person's role rests on credentials or demonstrated expertise, such as designing an engineered anchorage."}, {"text": "According to the course, how do this platform and your hands-on session together satisfy the training requirement?", "options": ["Completing the online quiz alone satisfies every part of the standard", "The hands-on session replaces the need for any written record", "The platform records name, date and the employer's attestation, and the hands-on session with a competent person adds practice and the chance to ask questions", "The platform record is only valid if printed and kept in the truck"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The platform supplies the three items the written record requires, while the hands-on session supplies the practice and trainer access that OSHA expects to accompany computer-based training."}]}, {"slug": "eye-face-protection", "title": "Eye and Face Protection", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "standard_title": "Personal Protective and Lifesaving Equipment, eye and face protection (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 9, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 25, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Eye and Face Protection, 29 CFR 1926.102, requires employers to ensure that employees wear the right eye and face protection whenever work exposes them to flying particles, molten metal, chemicals, gases, vapors or harmful light. This course covers the ANSI Z87.1 marking requirement, the main types of protection and when each is used, prescription and over-the-glasses options, hazard assessment, care and replacement, and the public works tasks that most often lead to eye injuries and citations.", "objectives": ["State the employer duty under 1926.102 and the six hazard categories that trigger it.", "Identify ANSI Z87.1 compliant eye and face protection by its markings.", "Match safety glasses, goggles, face shields, welding protection and arc rated shields to the hazards they control.", "Explain why a face shield is worn over safety glasses or goggles and never alone.", "Select the correct welding filter shade and the right option for employees who wear prescription lenses.", "Inspect, clean and replace eye and face protection and recognize the conditions most often cited."], "sections": [{"heading": "The employer duty and the hazards that trigger it", "html": "<p>Under 29 CFR 1926.102 the employer must <strong>ensure</strong> that each employee uses appropriate eye or face protection when exposed to hazards from:<br />\n- <strong>Flying particles</strong> from grinding, chipping, cutting, drilling and sawing<br />\n- <strong>Molten metal</strong> from welding, brazing and torch cutting<br />\n- <strong>Liquid chemicals</strong>, including splashes when pouring, mixing or spraying<br />\n- <strong>Acids or caustic liquids</strong> such as battery acid and sodium hydroxide<br />\n- <strong>Chemical gases or vapors</strong> that irritate or burn the eyes<br />\n- <strong>Potentially injurious light radiation</strong> from welding arcs, cutting torches, lasers and ultraviolet lamps</p>\n<p>Ensure is the key word. It is not enough to hand out safety glasses or post a sign. Supervisors must make sure protection is worn whenever the hazard is present. The general industry rule at 1910.133 says the same thing in nearly the same words, so the duty follows you whether the work is classified as construction or maintenance.</p>\n<p>Most eye injuries come from small, fast particles that strike before the worker can blink, and many happen to people standing near the work rather than doing it. The protection zone is the hazard area, not just the person holding the tool.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Eye and face protection stays on OSHA's most cited list because the violation is easy to see. An inspector walking a jobsite or a shop does not need a monitor or a records review. If someone is <strong>grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection</strong>, the citation writes itself.</p>\n<p>The most common findings are:<br />\n- Employees grinding, cutting, chipping or drilling with no eye protection at all<br />\n- Employees wearing ordinary prescription glasses or sunglasses instead of safety eyewear<br />\n- <strong>Face shields used without safety glasses or goggles underneath</strong><br />\n- Side shields missing from safety glasses<br />\n- Welding helpers and bystanders exposed to arc flash with no filter lenses<br />\n- Eyewear so scratched or fogged that workers push it up on their heads</p>\n<p>Every one of these is a supervision and habit problem more than an equipment problem. The equipment is cheap and usually in the truck.</p>"}, {"heading": "The ANSI Z87.1 marking", "html": "<p>Protective eye and face devices must comply with the <strong>ANSI/ISEA Z87.1</strong> consensus standard. OSHA incorporates specific editions of Z87.1 by reference in 1926.102 and 1910.133 and also accepts devices that the employer can show are at least as protective. In practice, look for the <strong>Z87</strong> marking molded or stamped on the frame and lens.</p>\n<p>Common markings:<br />\n- <strong>Z87</strong> means the device meets the basic impact requirements.<br />\n- <strong>Z87+</strong> means the device passed the <strong>high impact</strong> tests, which is what you want for grinding, chipping, chainsaw and chipper work.<br />\n- <strong>D3</strong> indicates splash and droplet protection, <strong>D4</strong> dust and <strong>D5</strong> fine dust.<br />\n- <strong>W</strong> followed by a number is a welding filter shade. <strong>U</strong> followed by a number is an ultraviolet filter.<br />\n- <strong>Z87-2</strong> on prescription frames means the frame meets the standard.</p>\n<p>Ordinary eyeglasses, reading glasses and sunglasses are not safety eyewear even if the lenses are polycarbonate. They do not carry the marking, lack side protection, and can shatter into the eye on impact. If it does not say Z87, it does not count.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of protection and what each is for", "html": "<p><strong>Safety glasses with side shields</strong> are the baseline for flying particle hazards: grinding, drilling, sawing, mowing, trimming, hammering and most shop work. Side shields are required when particles can come from the side, which is nearly always. Wraparound frames provide them built in.</p>\n<p><strong>Goggles</strong> seal around the eyes. <strong>Impact goggles</strong> with direct vents are for heavy particle work. <strong>Chemical splash goggles</strong> with indirect vents or no vents are for pouring, mixing and transferring liquids, and for dusty work. Goggles fit over most prescription glasses.</p>\n<p><strong>Face shields</strong> protect the whole face from large particles, splash and spray, but they are <strong>secondary protection</strong>. A face shield is always worn <strong>over safety glasses or goggles</strong>, never alone, because particles and liquid can get around and under the shield. Use them for grinding, chipping, chainsaw and chipper work, pressure washing and chemical transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Welding helmets and goggles</strong> with filter lenses protect against arc flash, sparks and molten metal. The lens shade must match the process. OSHA gives minimum shades in Table E-1 of 1926.102: roughly shade 10 to 12 for common arc welding and shade 3 to 6 for gas welding and oxygen cutting. Start darker and go lighter until you can see the work. A welding helmet is worn over safety glasses.</p>\n<p><strong>Laser protection</strong> must be rated for the specific wavelength and power of the laser in use, as marked on the eyewear.</p>\n<p><strong>Arc rated face shields and balaclavas</strong> protect against the heat of an electrical arc flash. They come from the electrical safety rules rather than 1926.102, but they are worn with safety glasses underneath for the same reason.</p>"}, {"heading": "Prescription lenses and fit", "html": "<p>Employees who wear corrective lenses have three compliant choices:<br />\n- <strong>Prescription safety glasses</strong> with Z87-2 marked frames, impact rated lenses and side shields. This is the most comfortable option for daily wear and many employers pay for a pair through a safety eyewear program.<br />\n- <strong>Over-the-glasses (OTG) safety glasses</strong> or goggles designed to fit over regular eyeglasses without disturbing them. These work well for visitors, occasional tasks and employees waiting on a prescription pair.<br />\n- <strong>Safety goggles or a full face respirator</strong> that fit over regular glasses, where the task also calls for a seal.</p>\n<p>Contact lenses are not protection on their own, and employees should tell their supervisor they wear them so first aid responders know.</p>\n<p>Fit matters for every type. Eyewear that slides down, pinches or fogs will end up on top of someone's head. Try different frame sizes, use anti-fog lenses, add a retention strap for overhead work, and make sure goggles seal without gaps.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hazard assessment, care and replacement", "html": "<p>The PPE rules require a <strong>hazard assessment</strong> of each task to decide what protection is needed. Walk through the job and ask: Can anything fly, splash, spray or arc toward the face? From which direction? Can it reach anyone nearby? Then choose protection that fully controls the hazard, combining devices when one hazard calls for a seal and another calls for face coverage.</p>\n<p>Good eyewear care:<br />\n- <strong>Clean</strong> lenses daily with water or lens cleaner and a soft cloth. Dry wiping grinds dust into the lens.<br />\n- <strong>Inspect</strong> before each use for cracks, deep scratches, loose or missing side shields, stretched straps and pitted welding lenses. Pits and scratches weaken the lens and scatter light.<br />\n- <strong>Replace</strong> eyewear that is cracked, badly scratched or fogged beyond cleaning, and replace any device that has taken a hard hit even if damage is not visible.<br />\n- <strong>Store</strong> eyewear in a case or clean pouch, not loose in a toolbox or on the dash.<br />\n- <strong>Disinfect</strong> shared face shields and goggles between users.</p>\n<p>Keep spare safety glasses in every truck and shop so a scratched pair is never a reason to skip protection.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Fleet and sign shop work is where most eye injuries happen: bench and angle <strong>grinding</strong>, cutoff wheels, wire brushing, chiseling and blowing off parts with compressed air. Z87+ safety glasses are the minimum, with a face shield added for grinding and cutoff work. Welders need the right filter shade, and helpers need protection against arc flash and chipping slag. <strong>Forestry</strong> crews running chainsaws and chippers need high impact safety glasses plus a face screen on the helmet, because chips fly fast and from all directions. Streets crews <strong>cutting concrete and asphalt</strong> need Z87+ glasses and a face shield when the saw throws slurry. <strong>Pressure washing</strong> throws grit back at the face, so wear glasses and a shield. Battery service means <strong>acid</strong>, so goggles and a face shield when checking, filling or jump starting.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater operators handling <strong>chlorine, sodium hypochlorite, caustic soda and ferric chloride</strong> need chemical splash goggles, and a face shield when pouring or connecting lines. Know where the eyewash is before you open the valve. Collections and solid waste crews face splash from sewage and hydraulic lines, and grinding or cutting pipe in the field.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line crews and substation technicians need safety glasses for every task because of flying debris and hardware overhead. Energized work above the arc flash threshold requires an <strong>arc rated face shield and balaclava</strong> or arc flash hood, with safety glasses underneath. Substation battery banks call for goggles and a face shield during maintenance. Meter technicians need safety glasses whenever a meter socket is opened.</p>\n<p>The rule is the same in every division: if a task can send anything toward your face, protection is on before the work starts, and face shields always go over glasses.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["The employer must ensure eye and face protection is used whenever employees are exposed to flying particles, molten metal, liquid chemicals, acids or caustics, chemical gases or vapors, or harmful light radiation.", "Only devices marked Z87 under ANSI Z87.1 count as protective eyewear, and Z87+ means high impact rated.", "Safety glasses with side shields are the baseline, goggles seal out splash and dust, and face shields add coverage for the whole face.", "A face shield is secondary protection and is always worn over safety glasses or goggles, never alone.", "Welding filter shades must match the process, starting from the minimums in Table E-1 of 1926.102.", "Employees who need corrective lenses must use prescription safety glasses or over-the-glasses protection, because ordinary eyeglasses are not safety eyewear.", "Inspect eyewear before each use and replace anything cracked, deeply scratched or struck hard.", "The most common citations are employees grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection and face shields worn without glasses underneath."], "questions": [{"text": "A mechanic is using an angle grinder with a cutoff wheel. What is the correct eye and face protection?", "options": ["A face shield alone, because it covers the whole face", "Ordinary prescription glasses with a face shield", "Z87+ safety glasses with side shields, plus a face shield over them", "Chemical splash goggles alone"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Grinding and cutoff work produces high speed particles, so high impact safety glasses are the primary protection and a face shield is added over them for face coverage. A face shield is never worn alone."}, {"text": "What does the marking Z87+ on a pair of safety glasses mean?", "options": ["The lenses are prescription", "The device passed the high impact tests of ANSI Z87.1", "The lenses filter welding radiation", "The glasses are approved for laser work"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Z87+ marks eyewear that passed the high impact requirements of ANSI Z87.1, which is what grinding, chipping and chainsaw work call for. Plain Z87 means basic impact only."}, {"text": "Which of these is NOT one of the hazards listed in 1926.102 that requires eye or face protection?", "options": ["Flying particles", "Liquid chemicals", "Potentially injurious light radiation", "Loud noise"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standard lists flying particles, molten metal, liquid chemicals, acids or caustic liquids, chemical gases or vapors, and injurious light radiation. Noise is covered by the hearing conservation rules, not eye and face protection."}, {"text": "Why must a face shield be worn over safety glasses or goggles rather than by itself?", "options": ["Particles and liquids can travel around and under the shield and reach the eyes", "Face shields are not rated for any impact", "The standard only recognizes face shields for welding", "Face shields fog too quickly to be used alone"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A face shield is secondary protection. Its open bottom and sides let particles, dust and splash reach the eyes, so primary eye protection must be worn underneath."}, {"text": "An employee who wears prescription eyeglasses needs to run a chipper for the afternoon. Which option complies with the standard?", "options": ["Wearing the prescription glasses, since they are polycarbonate", "Wearing the prescription glasses with the chipper face screen down", "Wearing over-the-glasses safety glasses or goggles with the face screen, or Z87-2 prescription safety glasses", "Removing the glasses and working without them"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Ordinary eyeglasses are not safety eyewear, and a face screen alone is secondary protection. The employee needs prescription safety glasses or over-the-glasses protection under the screen."}, {"text": "A water plant operator is connecting a hose to a tote of sodium hypochlorite. What is the right protection?", "options": ["Safety glasses with side shields only", "Chemical splash goggles, with a face shield added for pouring or connecting lines", "A welding helmet with a shade 5 lens", "A dust mask and sunglasses"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Corrosive liquids call for splash goggles that seal around the eyes, and a face shield over them when the task can spray or splash. Safety glasses alone leave gaps for liquid to enter."}, {"text": "Where does OSHA list the minimum filter lens shade numbers for welding and cutting?", "options": ["Section 8 of the Safety Data Sheet", "The NIOSH respirator certification", "Appendix D of 1910.134", "Table E-1 of 1926.102"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Table E-1 in 1926.102 gives the minimum protective shade for each welding and cutting operation. Welders start with the minimum or darker and adjust lighter until they can see the work."}, {"text": "When should safety glasses be replaced?", "options": ["Only when the lenses crack completely", "Once every five years regardless of condition", "When they are cracked, deeply scratched, fogged beyond cleaning, or have taken a hard impact", "Only when a supervisor notices damage"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Scratches and pits weaken the lens and scatter light, and an impact can damage a lens without visible signs, so eyewear is inspected before each use and replaced when its condition is in doubt."}, {"text": "A line crew is about to perform energized work above the arc flash threshold. What face protection is required?", "options": ["An arc rated face shield or arc flash hood worn over safety glasses", "A standard polycarbonate face shield worn alone", "Chemical splash goggles", "A welding helmet with a shade 10 lens"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Arc flash exposure requires an arc rated face shield with balaclava or an arc flash hood, with safety glasses underneath. Standard face shields can melt and are not arc rated."}, {"text": "Which situation is the most common reason employers are cited under the eye and face protection standard?", "options": ["Safety glasses stored in a toolbox", "Employees observed grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection", "Eyewash stations more than 10 seconds away", "Welding lenses one shade lighter than Table E-1"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The citation inspectors write most often is for employees seen grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection, followed by face shields worn without safety glasses underneath. Both are visible on a short walk through a shop or jobsite."}]}, {"slug": "machine-guarding", "title": "Machine Guarding", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "standard_title": "Machine Guarding, general requirements (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 10, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Machine guarding keeps hands, fingers, hair and clothing out of the moving parts of shop, plant and field equipment. This course covers the general guarding requirement in 29 CFR 1910.212, the types of guards and safeguarding devices, the special rules for fans and abrasive wheel grinders, and the habits that keep guards in place. Examples come from fleet and sign shops, treatment plants, transfer stations and tree and mowing crews.", "objectives": ["Identify the four main mechanical hazards: the point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks.", "Explain why a guard must keep every part of the body out of the danger zone during the operating cycle.", "Distinguish fixed, interlocked, adjustable and self-adjusting guards from safeguarding devices such as presence sensing and two-hand controls.", "Apply the specific rules for fan blade guards, anchoring fixed machinery and bench grinder work rests and tongue guards.", "Recognize common guarding failures on public works equipment and know what to do when a guard is missing or damaged."], "sections": [{"heading": "The general requirement", "html": "<p>29 CFR 1910.212 is short, but it applies to almost every machine in a public works shop or plant. It says that <strong>one or more methods of machine guarding</strong> must be provided to protect the operator and other employees in the area from hazards such as those created by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>point of operation</strong>, where the machine actually cuts, shapes, bores or forms the material,</li>\n<li><strong>ingoing nip points</strong>, where a belt meets a pulley, a chain meets a sprocket, or two rollers turn toward each other,</li>\n<li><strong>rotating parts</strong> such as shafts, couplings, flywheels, chucks and fan blades, and</li>\n<li><strong>flying chips and sparks</strong> thrown off by grinding, cutting and drilling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The guard must do its job without creating a new hazard. A guard with a sharp edge or a pinch point of its own does not meet the standard.</p>\n<p>The word \"machine\" covers more than big production equipment. A bench grinder, a drill press, a pedestal fan, a pump coupling and the belt drive on a blower are all machines with parts that can injure.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Machine guarding sits on OSHA's most frequently cited list every year, and the citations follow a predictable pattern:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Missing or removed guards.</strong> A guard was taken off for maintenance or because it was in the way and never went back on. Belt and coupling guards leaning against the wall next to the machine are a classic finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Unguarded bench grinders.</strong> Work rests set too far from the wheel, tongue guards missing or opened too far, and side guards removed are common items on any shop inspection.</li>\n<li><strong>Exposed belts and pulleys.</strong> Blowers, compressors, conveyors and shop equipment with open belt drives within reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Point of operation exposure.</strong> Shears, presses and saws where fingers can reach the cutting or forming zone during the cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Guards that are too open or too short.</strong> Covers that leave gaps big enough for a hand or that stop short of the nip point.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works shops are small and busy, and equipment is often older. That is where guards go missing and stay missing.</p>"}, {"heading": "The point of operation and the danger zone", "html": "<p>The <strong>point of operation</strong> is the area on a machine where work is actually performed on the material. On a drill press it is where the bit enters the stock. On a metal shear it is the blade. On a band saw it is the exposed blade above the table.</p>\n<p>The standard sets a simple test for point of operation guarding: the guard or device must <strong>prevent the operator from having any part of the body in the danger zone during the operating cycle</strong>. If a finger, hand or arm can reach the hazard while the machine can move, the guarding is not adequate.</p>\n<p>Special hand tools for placing and removing material may be used, but they are an addition to guarding, not a substitute for it.</p>\n<p>The standard names several machines that usually need point of operation guarding, including guillotine cutters, shears, power presses, milling machines, power saws, jointers and forming rolls. Sign shops and fleet shops have several of these.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of guards and safeguarding devices", "html": "<p><strong>Guards</strong> are physical barriers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed guards</strong> are permanent parts of the machine, such as a bolted belt cover or a coupling guard. They are the simplest and most reliable, and they are preferred whenever the operator does not need access during operation.</li>\n<li><strong>Interlocked guards</strong> shut the machine off when the guard is opened. The machine cannot restart until the guard is back in place. Many balers and chippers use interlocks on access doors.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjustable guards</strong> can be positioned to fit different sizes of stock, such as a band saw blade guard lowered to just above the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-adjusting guards</strong> move out of the way as the stock is fed and return when it is removed, like the lower blade guard on a circular saw.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Devices</strong> control access another way.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Presence sensing devices</strong> use light curtains, laser scanners or pressure mats to stop the machine when a person enters the zone.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-hand controls</strong> require both hands on buttons away from the point of operation during the hazardous part of the cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Pullbacks</strong> attach to the operator's wrists and pull the hands away as the machine cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Restraints</strong> tie the hands back so they cannot reach the danger zone at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whenever possible, a guard must be <strong>affixed to the machine</strong>. If that is not possible, it may be secured elsewhere, but it still must stay in place during operation.</p>"}, {"heading": "Fans, anchoring and bench grinders", "html": "<p>Three specific rules come up constantly in public works inspections.</p>\n<p><strong>Fan blades.</strong> When the periphery of a fan blade is less than <strong>7 feet above the floor or working level</strong>, the blades must be guarded, and the openings in the guard may be no larger than <strong>one half inch</strong>. Pedestal fans in shops and air movers in plants fall under this rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Anchoring fixed machinery.</strong> Machines designed for a fixed location must be <strong>securely anchored</strong> so they cannot walk or tip. A pedestal grinder that wobbles under load is a citation and a hazard.</p>\n<p><strong>Abrasive wheel grinders.</strong> 29 CFR 1910.215 adds specific rules for bench and pedestal grinders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>work rest</strong> must be kept adjusted to within <strong>one eighth inch</strong> of the wheel so the work cannot be pulled into the gap and jammed against the wheel.</li>\n<li>The <strong>tongue guard</strong> (the adjustable spark guard at the top of the wheel housing) must be kept within <strong>one quarter inch</strong> of the wheel.</li>\n<li>The side guards must cover the spindle, nut and flange, leaving only the working portion of the wheel exposed.</li>\n<li>Every new wheel gets a <strong>ring test</strong> before mounting. Tap the wheel lightly with a nonmetallic tool. A sound wheel rings clearly; a cracked wheel sounds dull and must be scrapped.</li>\n<li>Wheels must be rated for the grinder's speed. Stand to the side and let a new wheel run for a minute before use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As the wheel wears down, the gaps grow. Readjust the work rest and tongue guard with the grinder off and unplugged.</p>"}, {"heading": "Keep guards on, and what to do when they are not", "html": "<p>Guarding only works when it is in place. These rules apply to everyone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Never remove or bypass a guard while the machine is running.</strong> If a guard must come off for maintenance, the machine is locked out first and the guard goes back on before the lockout is released.</li>\n<li><strong>Never tie back, block or defeat an interlock.</strong> A taped-down interlock switch turns an interlocked guard into no guard at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect before you start.</strong> Check that the guard is present, secure and not cracked, bent or missing fasteners.</li>\n<li><strong>Report and tag.</strong> A machine with a missing or damaged guard is tagged out of service and reported to a supervisor. Do not use it until the guard is repaired or replaced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Operator training</strong> is part of guarding. Before using a machine, you should know its hazards, how the guards protect you, how to check that they are working and what to do if they are not. Loose clothing, gloves near rotating parts, long hair and jewelry add risk around any rotating machinery and should be controlled before the machine is started.</p>\n<p>Replacing a guard is cheap. A finger is not.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Fleet and sign shops.</strong> Bench grinders need the work rest and tongue guard adjusted every time the wheel wears. Drill presses need chuck and belt guards in place and stock clamped so it cannot spin. Band saws need the blade guard set just above the work. Metal shears need point of operation guarding or devices that keep fingers out of the blade. Tire machines have bead breakers and turntables that can crush and pinch, so hands stay clear while the machine moves.</p>\n<p><strong>Water, wastewater and solid waste.</strong> Pump couplings, belt and chain drives on blowers and conveyors, and clarifier drives must have fixed guards that cover the nip points and the rotating shaft. Guards removed during a rebuild go back on before the equipment returns to service. Balers and compactors at transfer stations rely on interlocked access doors and point of operation guarding, and no one reaches into the chamber or clears a jam while the machine has power.</p>\n<p><strong>Streets, forestry and electric field crews.</strong> Chipper infeed chutes, feed control bars and discharge guards must be in place and working, and operators stay at the side of the infeed with no loose clothing. Mower decks need discharge chutes and belt covers, and the PTO shaft on tractors and trailed equipment needs its spinning shield intact. Portable generators, compressors and line truck hydraulic pumps have belts, fans and couplings that need the same attention as shop equipment.</p>\n<p>Across all three divisions, the simplest habit is the most effective: if the guard is not on, the machine does not run.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Every machine needs one or more guarding methods that protect the operator and nearby employees from the point of operation, nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks.", "Point of operation guarding must keep every part of the body out of the danger zone during the operating cycle.", "Fixed guards are preferred, guards should be attached to the machine where possible, and no guard may create a hazard of its own.", "Fan blades within 7 feet of the floor must be guarded with openings no larger than one half inch, and fixed machines must be anchored.", "On bench grinders, keep the work rest within one eighth inch and the tongue guard within one quarter inch of the wheel, and ring test every new wheel.", "Guards are never removed or bypassed while a machine is running, and a machine with a missing or damaged guard is tagged out until it is fixed.", "The most common citations are missing or removed guards, unguarded bench grinders and exposed belts and pulleys."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of the following is one of the hazards that 29 CFR 1910.212 says machine guarding must protect against?", "options": ["Noise above 85 decibels", "Ingoing nip points", "Poor lighting at the workstation", "Slippery floors around the machine"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard names the point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks as the hazards guarding must address. Noise, lighting and floors are covered by other standards."}, {"text": "What is the test for whether point of operation guarding is adequate?", "options": ["The guard is painted a warning color", "The guard slows the operator's hands enough to react", "The guard prevents any part of the operator's body from being in the danger zone during the operating cycle", "The operator has signed a form acknowledging the hazard"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires that the guarding device keep every part of the body out of the danger zone while the machine cycles. If a hand can reach the hazard, the guard does not meet the rule."}, {"text": "A baler door shuts the machine off when it is opened and will not let it restart until the door is closed. What type of guard is this?", "options": ["Interlocked guard", "Self-adjusting guard", "Adjustable guard", "Fixed guard"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An interlocked guard cuts power or stops the machine when the guard is opened and prevents restart until it is back in place."}, {"text": "A pedestal fan in the fleet shop has blades about 5 feet above the floor. What does the standard require?", "options": ["No guard is required because the fan is portable", "A guard with openings no larger than one inch", "A guard only if the fan is within 3 feet of a workstation", "A guard with openings no larger than one half inch"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "When the fan blade periphery is less than 7 feet above the floor or working level, the blades must be guarded and the guard openings may be no larger than one half inch."}, {"text": "How close must the work rest on a bench grinder be kept to the wheel?", "options": ["Within one half inch", "Within one quarter inch", "Within one eighth inch", "Within one inch"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1910.215 requires the work rest to be adjusted to within one eighth inch of the wheel so work cannot be pulled into the gap. The tongue guard is the component kept within one quarter inch."}, {"text": "Before mounting a new grinding wheel, what should you do?", "options": ["Soak the wheel in water to cool it", "Tap it lightly with a nonmetallic tool and listen for a clear ring", "Run it at double speed for one minute to test strength", "File the edges smooth to remove burrs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The ring test checks for cracks. A sound wheel rings clearly when tapped; a dull sound means the wheel is cracked and must be discarded."}, {"text": "A guard must be removed to replace a belt on a blower. What is the correct sequence?", "options": ["Remove the guard while the blower is running so you can see the belt track, then lock out", "Lock out the blower, remove the guard, replace the belt, reinstall the guard, then release the lockout", "Lock out the blower, replace the belt and reinstall the guard at the next scheduled shutdown", "Have a second person stand by the stop button while the guard is off and the blower runs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Guards are never removed while a machine is running. The machine is locked out first, and the guard goes back on before the lockout is released and the machine returns to service."}, {"text": "Which of these is a safeguarding device rather than a guard?", "options": ["A bolted coupling cover on a pump", "A hinged belt cover with a spring latch", "A fixed mesh enclosure around a conveyor drive", "A two-hand control that requires both hands on buttons during the press cycle"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Guards are physical barriers. Two-hand controls, presence sensing devices, pullbacks and restraints are devices that keep the operator's hands out of the danger zone without a barrier."}, {"text": "You notice the coupling guard on a wastewater plant pump is missing. What should you do?", "options": ["Tag the pump out of service and report it to a supervisor before anyone uses it", "Operate the pump carefully and stay at least arm's length from the coupling", "Hang a caution sign and keep the pump running until the next maintenance day", "Cover the coupling with a rag so nothing gets caught"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A machine with a missing or damaged guard is taken out of service and reported. It should not run until the guard is repaired or replaced."}, {"text": "Which of these is among the most common machine guarding citations in shops like fleet and sign shops?", "options": ["Missing operator manuals", "Grinding wheels stored in a locked cabinet", "Exposed belts and pulleys on compressors and other equipment", "Guards painted a color other than yellow"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Exposed belts and pulleys, along with missing or removed guards and unguarded bench grinders, are the most frequent findings under the machine guarding standard."}]}, {"slug": "bloodborne-pathogens", "title": "Bloodborne Pathogens", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1030", "standard_title": "Bloodborne Pathogens (general industry)", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Bloodborne pathogens are germs carried in human blood that can cause lifelong disease, and public works crews meet them more often than most people think: discarded needles in parks and sweeper hoppers, injured coworkers, crash scenes and restroom cleanups. This course explains how hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV spread, who the standard covers, the controls and PPE that keep you from being exposed, the free hepatitis B vaccine and exactly what to do if an exposure happens. It also covers safe cleanup of blood spills and the training the standard requires every year.", "objectives": ["Explain how hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV are transmitted on the job and why every contact with blood is treated as infectious.", "Identify who has occupational exposure under 1910.1030, including designated first aid responders, and where to find the written exposure control plan.", "Apply universal precautions, work practice controls and the right PPE when handling sharps, giving first aid or cleaning up blood.", "Describe the hepatitis B vaccination offer, the 10 working day deadline and the declination form.", "Carry out the steps that follow an exposure incident: wash, report immediately, and complete the confidential medical evaluation and follow-up.", "Clean up and dispose of a blood spill using an EPA-registered disinfectant or a fresh 1:10 bleach solution, labeled containers and the biohazard symbol."], "sections": [{"heading": "What bloodborne pathogens are and how they spread", "html": "<p><strong>Bloodborne pathogens</strong> are germs carried in human blood that cause disease in people. Three matter most:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis B virus (HBV)</strong> attacks the liver and can become a lifelong infection. A vaccine prevents it. HBV is tough: the CDC reports it can survive in dried blood for at least a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis C virus (HCV)</strong> also attacks the liver, often with no symptoms for years. There is no vaccine, but treatment now cures most infections.</li>\n<li><strong>HIV</strong> weakens the immune system and causes AIDS. There is no vaccine or cure, but treatment controls it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The standard also covers <strong>other potentially infectious materials (OPIM)</strong>: semen, vaginal secretions, spinal and joint fluids, any body fluid with visible blood in it or that you cannot identify, and unfixed human tissue.</p>\n<p>These germs get in through <strong>needlesticks and cuts</strong> from contaminated sharps, through <strong>broken skin</strong> such as scrapes, cracked knuckles, rashes and burns, and through the <strong>mucous membranes</strong> of the eyes, nose and mouth. They do not pass through unbroken skin or casual contact. Sweat, tears, urine, vomit, feces, sewage and stormwater are not OPIM unless visible blood is present, but the needles that turn up in all of them are a sharps hazard.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>You do not work in a hospital, but you handle what hospitals handle. Public works crews find needles in <strong>parks, playgrounds and restrooms</strong>; in <strong>street sweeper hoppers and catch basins</strong>, mixed with wet debris; in <strong>trash and recycling bags</strong>, where a needle goes through the bag, the glove and the hand; and in <strong>wastewater bar screens, grit chambers and wet wells</strong>. They clean blood out of vehicles and equipment after collisions and injuries. They give first aid to coworkers who are cut, crushed or burned, and often reach a crash scene before the ambulance. One needlestick or one splash to the eye can mean an infection that lasts a lifetime.</p>\n<p>These infections are preventable, and OSHA's <strong>Bloodborne Pathogens standard, 29 CFR 1910.1030</strong>, lays out the system that prevents them. It requires training <strong>at initial assignment</strong> to tasks with exposure and <strong>at least once a year</strong> after that, with a chance to ask questions. This module is the annual refresher.</p>"}, {"heading": "Who is covered and the exposure control plan", "html": "<p>The standard covers every employee with <strong>occupational exposure</strong>: reasonably anticipated contact of the skin, eyes, mucous membranes or a puncture with blood or OPIM while doing the job. In public works that usually means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Designated first aid responders</strong>, including crew members who give first aid as a collateral duty.</li>\n<li>Employees who handle needles and sharps as part of the work: park and restroom maintenance, street sweeping, refuse collection, wastewater screening.</li>\n<li>Employees who clean blood from vehicles, equipment, facilities or the right of way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Good Samaritan question.</strong> If first aid is not part of your job and you choose to help a bleeding coworker or member of the public, OSHA does not count that as occupational exposure. Help anyway, use the gloves and pocket mask on the truck, and <strong>report any contact with blood right away</strong>; the department's plan provides the same medical follow-up.</p>\n<p><strong>The written exposure control plan.</strong> Section 1910.1030(c) requires a written <strong>exposure control plan</strong> that lists which jobs and tasks have exposure, how each part of the standard is carried out, and how exposure incidents are investigated. It is <strong>reviewed and updated at least once a year</strong> and whenever new tasks, equipment or safer sharps devices change the picture. Ask your supervisor where the current copy is kept.</p>"}, {"heading": "Universal precautions, work practices and PPE", "html": "<p><strong>Universal precautions</strong> is the first rule: <strong>treat all human blood and OPIM as infectious</strong>, every time. You cannot tell by looking who is infected.</p>\n<p><strong>Engineering controls</strong> remove the hazard and <strong>work practices</strong> change how you do the task:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Never pick up a needle with your fingers</strong>, even gloved. Use tongs, pliers or a grabber and drop it point first into a <strong>sharps container</strong>: closable, puncture resistant, leakproof, and labeled with the biohazard symbol or color coded red.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not bend, break or recap needles.</strong> Do not reach into trash bags, hoppers or screens by hand; look first, then use a tool.</li>\n<li><strong>Wash your hands</strong> with soap and water as soon as gloves come off and after any contact with blood. With no sink nearby, use antiseptic hand cleaner, then wash as soon as you can. Flush a splash to the eyes, nose or mouth with water immediately.</li>\n<li><strong>No eating, drinking, smoking, vaping, lip balm or contact lenses</strong> where exposure is likely, and no food stored where blood or OPIM may be.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Personal protective equipment</strong> is the last barrier, provided at no cost to you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gloves</strong> whenever your hands may touch blood, OPIM, broken skin or a contaminated surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Change disposable gloves</strong> as soon as they are torn, punctured or soiled, and between patients. Never wash or reuse them. Peel them off inside out, then wash your hands.</li>\n<li><strong>Eye and face protection</strong> whenever blood could splash or spray.</li>\n<li><strong>A gown or apron</strong> when soaking or heavy splashing is possible.</li>\n<li><strong>A pocket mask or bag valve mask</strong> for CPR, never mouth to mouth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PPE comes off before you leave the area and goes in the marked container.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hepatitis B vaccination", "html": "<p>Hepatitis B is the one bloodborne disease with a vaccine. Section 1910.1030(f) requires the department to <strong>offer the hepatitis B vaccine series free of charge</strong> to every employee with occupational exposure, <strong>within 10 working days of initial assignment</strong>, after this training, by or under a licensed health care professional. You cannot be charged or required to take a blood test first.</p>\n<p>You do not have to accept it. If you decline, you sign the <strong>declination statement</strong> from Appendix A of the standard. It says you understand the risk, you were offered the vaccine at no cost, and you may <strong>change your mind later</strong> and still receive it free.</p>\n<p>OSHA allows one narrow exception: designated first aid responders whose main job is something else may be offered the vaccine within 24 hours after they give first aid involving blood, if the department's plan meets strict conditions.</p>"}, {"heading": "If you are exposed", "html": "<p>An <strong>exposure incident</strong> is specific contact with blood or OPIM through the eyes, mouth, other mucous membrane, broken skin or a puncture.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wash.</strong> Scrub a needlestick or cut with soap and running water. Flush splashed eyes, nose or mouth with clean water. Do not squeeze the wound or put bleach on skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Report it immediately</strong> to your supervisor, not at the end of the shift. Medicine that prevents HBV or HIV infection works best when it starts within hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Get the medical evaluation.</strong> The department provides a <strong>confidential medical evaluation and follow-up at no cost</strong>. It documents how the exposure happened, identifies and tests the <strong>source individual</strong> where possible and allowed by law, tests your blood with your consent, provides <strong>post-exposure treatment when medically indicated</strong>, and includes counseling.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow up.</strong> You receive the health care professional's written opinion within 15 days. Your test results stay between you and the provider, and a needlestick also goes on the department's <strong>sharps injury log</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The unreported needlestick is the one that becomes a lifelong infection.</p>"}, {"heading": "Cleaning up blood spills, regulated waste and labels", "html": "<p>Blood on a truck seat, shop floor or restroom wall is cleaned and decontaminated <strong>as soon as feasible</strong> by a trained employee using the department's spill kit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Put on PPE:</strong> nitrile gloves at minimum, with eye protection and a gown or apron for a large spill.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove sharps first</strong> with tongs into the sharps container, and glass with a dustpan and brush.</li>\n<li><strong>Absorb</strong> the liquid with paper towels or absorbent powder. Do not spray disinfectant into a pool of blood; it splashes.</li>\n<li><strong>Disinfect</strong> with an <strong>EPA-registered disinfectant</strong> labeled for HBV and HIV, or a <strong>fresh 1:10 bleach solution</strong> (1 part household bleach to 9 parts water, mixed that day). Leave the surface <strong>wet for the contact time on the label</strong>; for bleach, let it air dry. Wiping it off right away kills nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Bag the waste.</strong> Materials soaked or caked with blood that would release it when squeezed are <strong>regulated waste</strong>. They go in a closable, leakproof <strong>red bag or biohazard-labeled container</strong>, closed before it is moved, and are disposed of as the plan and state law require.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove PPE</strong> last, inside out, then wash your hands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Labels.</strong> The <strong>biohazard symbol</strong> on a fluorescent orange or orange-red label marks regulated waste, sharps containers, refrigerators holding blood or OPIM, and contaminated equipment. <strong>Red bags and red containers</strong> may be used instead. When you see either one, assume the contents can infect you.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line crews and substation technicians are often the designated first aid responders on their crew. A coworker brought down in a pole-top or bucket rescue may be bleeding or burned, so nitrile gloves and a pocket mask ride in the rescue kit. Rubber insulating gloves protect you from voltage, not from blood. Meter technicians who find needles in meter pits leave them for a crew with tongs and a sharps container.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Refuse and recycling collectors meet needles hidden in bags. Never compress a bag against your body, never reach into a hopper by hand, and treat every puncture as an exposure incident, not a scratch. Wastewater operators pull needles out of bar screens and grit washers with tools, never gloves alone, and keep a sharps container at the headworks.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Sweeper operators find needles in hoppers and catch basins; dump the hopper and look before you shovel. Parks and facilities staff carry a sharps kit for playgrounds, restrooms and shelters. Fleet technicians cleaning a vehicle after a collision wear gloves and eye protection and bag soaked materials as regulated waste.</p>\n<p>For everyone: gloves on before you touch, tongs before you reach, sharps container before you walk away, and report every stick or splash the same hour it happens.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Treat all human blood and other potentially infectious materials as infectious, every time, no matter who it came from.", "Never pick up a needle or bloody glass by hand; use tongs or a brush and dustpan and drop sharps point first into a labeled, puncture-resistant sharps container.", "Wear gloves whenever your hands may touch blood, add eye and face protection when splashing is possible, change torn or soiled gloves right away and wash your hands as soon as gloves come off.", "The hepatitis B vaccine is offered free within 10 working days of assignment to a job with exposure, and if you sign the declination form you can still change your mind later.", "After a needlestick or splash: wash or flush, report it immediately, and complete the confidential medical evaluation and follow-up at no cost to you.", "Clean blood spills with an EPA-registered disinfectant or a fresh 1:10 bleach solution left wet for the full contact time, and dispose of soaked materials as regulated waste in red or biohazard-labeled containers.", "The written exposure control plan lists who is covered and how incidents are handled; it is reviewed every year and you can read it any time.", "Training is required when you are first assigned to a task with exposure and at least once a year after that."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of these is a way bloodborne pathogens can enter your body at work?", "options": ["Shaking hands with an infected coworker", "A splash of blood into your eye", "Sharing a water cooler in the shop", "Riding in the same truck cab as an infected coworker"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Bloodborne pathogens enter through needlesticks and cuts, broken skin, and mucous membranes such as the eyes, nose and mouth. They do not spread through casual contact, shared water coolers or shared air."}, {"text": "A fleet technician whose job does not include first aid stops to help a bleeding member of the public and gets blood on a cut finger. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["The technician is covered by the standard because anyone who touches blood is covered", "The technician should not report it because the standard does not apply", "It counts as occupational exposure only if the technician was wearing gloves", "OSHA does not treat this as occupational exposure, but the technician should still report it right away and the department arranges medical follow-up"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A Good Samaritan act by an employee whose job does not include first aid is not occupational exposure under 1910.1030, but blood on broken skin is still a real exposure. The department's plan provides the same reporting and medical follow-up, and it only works if the exposure is reported immediately."}, {"text": "You find a syringe in the grass at a park. What is the correct way to pick it up?", "options": ["With tongs or a grabber, dropping it point first into a sharps container", "With two pairs of nitrile gloves, holding the barrel", "By recapping it first so the needle is covered", "By sweeping it into a trash bag with the other litter"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Needles are never picked up by hand, even gloved, and never recapped. A tool keeps your fingers away from the point, and a closable, puncture-resistant, labeled sharps container keeps the needle from sticking the next person who handles the trash."}, {"text": "When must the department offer the hepatitis B vaccine to a new employee with occupational exposure?", "options": ["Within 30 days of hire", "Only after the first exposure incident", "Within 10 working days of initial assignment, after training", "At the next annual physical"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1910.1030(f) requires the vaccine to be made available free of charge within 10 working days of initial assignment to a job with occupational exposure, after the employee has received training. Only a narrow exception for designated first aid responders allows it to be offered after an incident."}, {"text": "An employee signs the hepatitis B vaccine declination form. Six months later she changes her mind. What happens?", "options": ["She must wait until the next annual training to request it", "The department must still provide the vaccine series at no cost", "She can get it, but through her own insurance", "The declination is permanent"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The declination statement in Appendix A says the employee may later decide to accept the vaccine and the employer must then make it available at no cost. Declining once does not close the door."}, {"text": "A collector is stuck through a glove by a needle in a trash bag. What should happen first?", "options": ["Finish the route, then mention it at the end of the shift", "Squeeze the wound hard and pour bleach on it", "Wait to see if the site becomes red or sore before reporting", "Wash the wound with soap and water and report it to the supervisor immediately"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The first steps after an exposure incident are to wash the wound with soap and water and report it immediately so the confidential medical evaluation can start. Treatment that prevents HBV or HIV infection works best when it begins within hours, so waiting for symptoms or the end of the shift gives the infection a head start."}, {"text": "Which cleanup practice is correct for blood on a truck seat?", "options": ["Absorb the blood, then apply disinfectant and leave the surface wet for the contact time on the label", "Spray disinfectant into the pool of blood and wipe it up immediately", "Wipe it with a dry rag and let the sun dry the seat", "Pour undiluted bleach on it and wipe it off right away"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Absorb the liquid first, then disinfect and let the product stay wet for its full contact time. Spraying into a pool of blood splashes it, and wiping a disinfectant off right away does not give it time to kill anything."}, {"text": "How is the bleach solution for blood cleanup mixed?", "options": ["Equal parts bleach and water", "1 part bleach to 100 parts water, stored for a month", "1 part household bleach to 9 parts water, mixed fresh that day", "Straight bleach from the bottle"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A 1:10 solution is 1 part household bleach to 9 parts water. Diluted bleach loses strength over time, so it is mixed fresh the day it is used and left on the surface until it air dries."}, {"text": "What does a fluorescent orange label with the biohazard symbol, or a red bag, tell you?", "options": ["The container holds flammable liquid", "The contents are safe to put in the regular trash", "The container holds sharps only, never soft waste", "The contents may contain blood or OPIM and can infect you"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1910.1030(g) requires the biohazard label, or red bags and containers in its place, on regulated waste, sharps containers, refrigerators holding blood or OPIM, and contaminated equipment. The symbol means treat the contents as infectious."}, {"text": "How often must the written exposure control plan be reviewed and this training repeated?", "options": ["At least once a year, and whenever tasks or equipment change", "Every three years", "Only when a new employee is hired", "Only after an exposure incident"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The exposure control plan is reviewed and updated at least annually and whenever new tasks or equipment change who is exposed. Training is given at initial assignment and at least once a year after that."}]}, {"slug": "confined-spaces", "title": "Confined Spaces", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.146", "standard_title": "Permit-Required Confined Spaces (general industry), with 1926 Subpart AA for construction", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 40, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Manholes, wet wells, vaults and tanks look the same on the day they kill as they did the day before. This course covers how to recognize a confined space, what makes it permit-required, how to test and ventilate the atmosphere, what the entrant, attendant and entry supervisor each do, and why rescue starts with a retrieval line instead of a second person going in. It is built on 29 CFR 1910.146 and notes where the construction rule in 1926 Subpart AA adds to it.", "objectives": ["Identify a confined space using the three-part definition and name the four conditions that make it permit-required.", "Test the atmosphere in the correct order and at the correct levels with a bump-tested meter, and state the oxygen, flammable and toxic limits that stop an entry.", "Describe what the entry permit records, who signs it, how long it lasts and when it is cancelled.", "Explain the duties of the authorized entrant, the attendant and the entry supervisor, including why the attendant never enters.", "Set up non-entry rescue with a tripod, winch and full body harness, and explain why would-be rescuers make up so many confined space deaths.", "Apply the sewer entry provisions, traffic control and training requirements that cover routine public works entries."], "sections": [{"heading": "What a confined space is, and what makes it permit-required", "html": "<p>Under <strong>29 CFR 1910.146(b)</strong>, a <strong>confined space</strong> meets all three tests:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is large enough and shaped so a worker can bodily enter and do assigned work.</li>\n<li>It has limited or restricted means of entry or exit.</li>\n<li>It is not designed for continuous occupancy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In public works that means manholes, sewer lines, lift stations and wet wells, water storage tanks, valve vaults, meter pits, electrical vaults, digesters, clarifiers, baghouses, tanker and vactor truck tanks, culverts and storm structures.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>permit-required confined space</strong> (a <strong>permit space</strong>) when it has any one of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>hazardous atmosphere</strong>, or the potential for one.</li>\n<li>A material that could <strong>engulf</strong> an entrant: water, sewage, sludge, grit or salt.</li>\n<li><strong>Inwardly converging walls</strong> or a <strong>floor that slopes down to a smaller opening</strong> that could trap an entrant.</li>\n<li><strong>Any other recognized serious hazard</strong>, such as an unguarded auger, exposed energized parts or a fall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The employer must identify permit spaces, post <strong>danger signs</strong> or warn employees by an equally effective means, and either keep employees out or run a <strong>written permit space program</strong>. Treat any space not on the department's inventory as a permit space until the entry supervisor rules otherwise. Construction work such as new sewer installation falls under <strong>29 CFR 1926 Subpart AA</strong>, which uses the same framework.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>Confined space deaths follow a pattern. The space looked the same as last week. There was no smell, or it faded. One worker went down within a few breaths. A coworker went in after them and went down too. <strong>NIOSH studies of confined space deaths found that more than half of the victims were would-be rescuers.</strong></p>\n<p>Public works crews face this risk more than most. Appendix E to 1910.146 says it plainly: for sewer workers, the permit space is the usual work environment. Familiarity is the danger. A sewer atmosphere can change in minutes from an upstream discharge, a rain surge or a plug of debris that lets go, and methane and carbon monoxide have no odor at all. <strong>Your senses cannot test a confined space. Only a meter can.</strong></p>"}, {"heading": "Atmospheric hazards, testing and ventilation", "html": "<p>Section 1910.146(b) defines a <strong>hazardous atmosphere</strong> as any of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oxygen below 19.5 percent</strong> or <strong>above 23.5 percent</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>flammable gas or vapor above 10 percent of its lower explosive limit (LEL)</strong>, which the standard calls the lower flammable limit.</li>\n<li>A toxic substance above its exposure limit, or any other condition immediately dangerous to life or health.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The toxic gases that matter most here are <strong>hydrogen sulfide (H2S)</strong> and <strong>carbon monoxide (CO)</strong>. Appendix E sets meter alarms at <strong>10 ppm for H2S</strong> and <strong>35 ppm for CO</strong>. H2S smells like rotten eggs at low levels, but higher concentrations deaden the sense of smell within a few breaths, so a smell that fades can mean the gas is getting worse.</p>\n<p><strong>Testing.</strong> Before anyone enters, a trained person tests the air with a <strong>calibrated multi-gas meter</strong> that was <strong>bump-tested that day</strong> with known gas to prove every sensor alarms. Sample from outside with a pump and sample line, <strong>oxygen first, then flammables, then toxics</strong>, as 1910.146(d)(5)(iii) requires; the flammable sensor does not read correctly without enough oxygen. Sample at the <strong>top, middle and bottom</strong>, because gases layer: methane rises, H2S sinks and CO mixes throughout. The entrant then carries a running meter the whole time. If any alarm sounds, everyone gets out.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventilation.</strong> A blower does not replace testing. Under 1910.146(c)(5)(ii)(E), forced air must come from a clean source, reach the entrant's work area and keep running until the last entrant is out. Set the intake <strong>upwind, away from vehicle and generator exhaust, the blower's own engine and the roadway</strong>; a blower that pulls in exhaust fills the space with CO. Ventilate, then retest before anyone enters.</p>"}, {"heading": "The permit, isolation and the three roles", "html": "<p><strong>The permit.</strong> No one enters until the <strong>entry supervisor</strong> signs an entry permit under 1910.146(f). It names the space, the purpose, the date and duration, the entrants, the attendant and the entry supervisor, and it records the hazards, isolation measures, acceptable entry conditions, test results, rescue service and how to call it, communication method and required equipment. It is posted at the opening and lasts no longer than the task. The entry supervisor <strong>cancels</strong> it when the work is done or when a condition the permit does not allow appears; cancelled permits are kept for a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolation.</strong> Cut the space off from energy and material: <strong>lockout and tagout</strong> of pumps, mixers, augers and electrical gear under 1910.147, and <strong>blanking or blinding</strong> a line with a solid plate rated for the line pressure. <strong>Line breaking</strong>, opening a line that carries sewage, chemicals or pressurized fluid, follows its own procedure and PPE. A closed valve by itself is not isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>The three roles.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authorized entrant.</strong> Knows the hazards and signs of exposure, uses the equipment correctly, stays in contact with the attendant and exits at once when told, on any alarm or when anything feels wrong. <strong>Only employees who have completed the department's hands-on confined space training and are listed on the permit may enter.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Attendant.</strong> Stays outside, keeps an exact count, keeps in contact, watches conditions inside and out, orders evacuation when needed, calls rescue as soon as help may be needed and performs <strong>non-entry rescue only</strong>. The attendant <strong>never leaves the post</strong> unless relieved by another trained attendant and <strong>never enters the space to attempt a rescue</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry supervisor.</strong> Confirms the permit is complete, tests are done, equipment is in place and the rescue service can be reached, then signs. Ends the entry, cancels the permit and removes anyone not authorized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Communication.</strong> Agree on the method before entry: voice, radio or rope signals. If contact is lost and cannot be restored, the entrants come out.</p>"}, {"heading": "Entry procedure, step by step", "html": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Plan.</strong> Confirm the space is on the inventory, review its hazards and assign trained entrants, an attendant and an entry supervisor.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up traffic control and the work zone</strong> before the cover comes off.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove the cover</strong> with a cover lifter and guard the opening.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolate.</strong> Lock out and tag out, blank or block lines, verify zero energy.</li>\n<li><strong>Test the atmosphere</strong> from outside, top to bottom, oxygen first, then flammables, then toxics. Record the readings.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventilate</strong> if needed, then retest.</li>\n<li><strong>Stage rescue.</strong> Tripod and winch set, harness on, retrieval line attached, rescue service confirmed.</li>\n<li><strong>Sign and post the permit.</strong> The entry supervisor verifies every item and signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Enter and monitor.</strong> The entrant carries a running meter; the attendant keeps count, keeps contact and watches conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Evacuate</strong> on any alarm, loss of contact, prohibited condition or order from the attendant or supervisor. No re-entry until the entry supervisor retests and reauthorizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Close out.</strong> Account for everyone and every tool, replace the cover, cancel the permit, note any problems on it and pull the traffic control last.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"heading": "Rescue: non-entry first", "html": "<p>Section 1910.146(k) builds rescue around getting a person out <strong>without sending anyone else in</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retrieval systems</strong> are used whenever an entrant goes into a permit space, unless the equipment would add risk or would not help. Each entrant wears a <strong>full body harness</strong> with the retrieval line attached at the center of the back between the shoulders or above the head, and the other end goes to a <strong>mechanical device or fixed point outside</strong> so retrieval can start the moment it is needed. A <strong>mechanical device</strong>, such as a tripod and winch, is required for vertical spaces <strong>more than 5 feet deep</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry rescue</strong> is done only by a trained, equipped rescue team with supplied air or SCBA and its own attendant and retrieval system. If the department relies on an outside service, it must confirm that the service can <strong>reach the space in time for the hazard</strong>, is available that day, knows the hazards and has practiced there.</li>\n<li><strong>Call early.</strong> The attendant calls rescue as soon as an entrant may need help, not after a collapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most important rule is also the simplest: <strong>if someone goes down, you do not go in.</strong> Start the winch, call for rescue, keep the blower running and keep talking to them.</p>"}, {"heading": "Sewer entry, alternate procedures, traffic control and training", "html": "<p><strong>Sewer entry.</strong> A sewer is part of a continuous system and cannot be fully isolated. Paragraph 1910.146(d)(5)(i) allows pre-entry testing to the extent feasible in that case but requires <strong>continuous monitoring</strong> where the entrants are working. Non-mandatory <strong>Appendix E, Sewer System Entry</strong>, adds that only employees thoroughly trained in the department's sewer entry procedures should enter, that entrants carry an alarming meter, and that crews watch the weather so work stops before a surge from rain or hydrant flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternate procedures and reclassification.</strong> Under 1910.146(c)(5), when the only hazard is an actual or potential hazardous atmosphere and monitoring data shows that <strong>continuous forced air ventilation alone</strong> keeps the space safe, entry can proceed with a signed certification instead of a full permit. Testing still applies, and an alarm empties the space. Under 1910.146(c)(7), a permit space can be <strong>reclassified</strong> as non-permit only when it has no actual or potential atmospheric hazard and every other hazard is eliminated without entry. Ventilation controls a hazard; it does not eliminate one.</p>\n<p><strong>Traffic control.</strong> Set up signs, cones and arrow boards per the department's traffic control plan and <strong>MUTCD Part 6</strong> before the cover comes off, use the truck as a shield, wear Class 2 or Class 3 high-visibility apparel and never assign the attendant to flag traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Training.</strong> Under 1910.146(g), training comes before the first assignment, before a change in duties, whenever the work changes in a way that adds a hazard and whenever the department sees deviations or gaps in knowledge. It is certified with the employee's name, the trainer's signature and the dates. This course is the classroom portion; the hands-on session with the meter, blower, tripod and harness completes it.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Underground vaults and manholes carry every confined space hazard plus energized cable. Routine line work entries may fall under the enclosed space rules of 1910.269(e); the department treats them as permit spaces. A flooded meter pit is a permit space until tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Wet wells, lift stations, digesters and clarifiers are permit spaces every time, because sewage produces H2S and methane around the clock. Watch the forecast on storm sewer work; upstream rain is an engulfment hazard. Solid waste crews treat compactor hoppers, transfer station pits and tanker tanks as permit spaces. No one climbs into a vactor debris tank to clear a clog.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Streets crews entering culverts and storm structures face sudden flow and low oxygen from rotting debris. Signal technicians entering pull boxes and vaults follow the same rules as Electric. Fleet mechanics do not enter tanker, water truck or sweeper hopper tanks without a permit. Facilities staff treat pits, tanks and crawl spaces with limited exits as potential permit spaces.</p>\n<p>If the entry is not planned, tested, permitted and rigged, it does not happen. The job can wait. The person in the hole cannot.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["A confined space is one a worker can bodily enter, with limited or restricted entry or exit, that is not designed for continuous occupancy; it is permit-required if it has a hazardous atmosphere, an engulfment hazard, converging walls or a sloping floor, or any other serious hazard.", "Only a meter can test a space: oxygen must be between 19.5 and 23.5 percent, flammables below 10 percent of the LEL, and hydrogen sulfide and carbon monoxide below the alarm points, tested oxygen first, then flammables, then toxics, at the top, middle and bottom.", "Ventilate with clean air from a blower whose intake is away from exhaust and traffic, retest, and keep monitoring the whole time anyone is inside.", "No one enters until the entry supervisor has signed the permit, and only employees with the department's hands-on confined space training who are listed on the permit may enter.", "The attendant stays outside, keeps count, keeps contact, calls rescue early and never enters the space to attempt a rescue.", "Every entrant wears a full body harness with a retrieval line to a tripod and winch or fixed point outside, and a mechanical device is required for vertical spaces more than 5 feet deep.", "Sewers cannot be isolated, so sewer entries require continuous monitoring, weather awareness and the extra precautions in Appendix E.", "If someone goes down, you do not go in; start the retrieval, call rescue, keep the blower running and keep talking to them."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of the following describes a confined space under 1910.146?", "options": ["Any space below ground level that requires a ladder to reach", "A space a worker can bodily enter, with limited or restricted entry or exit, that is not designed for continuous occupancy", "Any space that has tested positive for a hazardous atmosphere", "A space that is posted with a danger sign by the employer"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The definition in 1910.146(b) has three parts: large enough to bodily enter and work in, limited or restricted means of entry or exit, and not designed for continuous occupancy. A hazardous atmosphere or a danger sign is what makes a confined space permit-required, not what makes it a confined space."}, {"text": "An empty V-box salt spreader hopper is open to the air and tests clean. Its walls slope inward to the auger trough at the bottom. Can a mechanic climb in to clear bridged salt without a permit?", "options": ["Yes, because the atmosphere is acceptable and the hopper is open at the top", "Yes, as long as a coworker watches from the truck bed", "No. Inwardly converging walls that could trap an entrant make it a permit space, and the auger is another serious hazard that must be locked out", "No, but only because salt dust is a combustible dust hazard"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A space is permit-required if it has any one of the four conditions, and inwardly converging walls or a floor that slopes to a smaller opening is one of them. The auger adds a recognized serious hazard that requires lockout. Good air alone does not make a space non-permit."}, {"text": "Which oxygen reading meets the acceptable entry range in 1910.146?", "options": ["18.9 percent", "19.2 percent", "23.8 percent", "20.9 percent"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standard defines a hazardous atmosphere as oxygen below 19.5 percent or above 23.5 percent. Normal air is 20.9 percent. Readings of 18.9 and 19.2 percent are oxygen deficient, and 23.8 percent is oxygen enriched."}, {"text": "In what order does 1910.146(d)(5)(iii) require the atmosphere to be tested?", "options": ["Oxygen, then flammables, then toxics", "Toxics, then oxygen, then flammables", "Flammables, then oxygen, then toxics", "Any order, as long as all three are tested before entry"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires testing first for oxygen, then for combustible gases and vapors, then for toxic gases and vapors. The flammable sensor does not read correctly in an oxygen-deficient atmosphere, so the oxygen reading has to come first."}, {"text": "Why must the atmosphere be sampled at the top, middle and bottom of a manhole?", "options": ["To give the sensors time to warm up before the final reading at the bottom", "Because gases layer: methane rises, hydrogen sulfide sinks and carbon monoxide mixes throughout", "To average the three readings into a single value for the permit", "Because the standard requires one reading for each of the three gases"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Gases have different densities. Methane is lighter than air and collects near the top, hydrogen sulfide is heavier and settles at the bottom, and carbon monoxide is close to the weight of air and mixes throughout. A single reading at one level can miss a lethal layer."}, {"text": "An entrant in a wet well stops answering the radio, and the attendant sees him slumped against the wall. What does the attendant do?", "options": ["Climb down the ladder to check on him and help him up", "Send the second entrant over to check on him while calling the supervisor", "Start the winch retrieval, call rescue and stay outside the space", "Shut off the blower to hear better and wait for the entry supervisor"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The attendant performs non-entry rescue only and never enters the space. The retrieval system is rigged so retrieval can begin as soon as the attendant sees it is needed, and rescue is called as soon as an entrant may need help. The blower stays running and the other entrant is ordered out."}, {"text": "When does the entry supervisor cancel an entry permit?", "options": ["At the end of the shift, whether or not the work is finished", "Only after the rescue service has signed off on the entry", "After 24 hours, which is the longest a permit may run", "When the work covered by the permit is completed, or when a condition the permit does not allow arises in or near the space"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Under 1910.146(e), the permit lasts no longer than the task it covers, and the entry supervisor terminates the entry and cancels the permit when the work is done or when a prohibited condition arises. There is no fixed 24-hour limit; the task sets the duration."}, {"text": "A vertical manhole is 12 feet deep. What does 1910.146(k)(3) require for retrieval?", "options": ["A mechanical device, such as a tripod and winch, connected to the retrieval line on the entrant's full body harness", "A second entrant stationed on the ladder to assist if needed", "A rope tied around the entrant's waist and held by the attendant", "Nothing beyond the ladder, because the space is less than 20 feet deep"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Retrieval systems are required whenever an entrant goes into a permit space unless they would add risk or would not help, and a mechanical device must be available for vertical spaces more than 5 feet deep. The line attaches to a full body harness at the center of the back or above the head, not around the waist."}, {"text": "Why does 1910.146(d)(5)(i) call for continuous monitoring during sewer entry?", "options": ["Because sewer meters drift and must be reset every few minutes", "Because a sewer permit expires after one hour", "Because pre-entry testing is not required in sewers", "Because a sewer is part of a continuous system that cannot be fully isolated, so the atmosphere can change without warning"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A sewer cannot be isolated like a tank. Pre-entry testing is still done to the extent feasible, but because flow from upstream can change the atmosphere at any time, the standard requires continuous monitoring in the areas where entrants are working."}, {"text": "A crew sets up a blower to ventilate a lift station wet well. Where does the blower intake belong?", "options": ["Upwind and away from vehicle and generator exhaust, the blower's own engine and the roadway", "Next to the truck's exhaust pipe so it draws warm air in winter", "Inside the wet well, so the air recirculates faster", "Anywhere convenient, as long as the duct reaches the bottom of the well"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Under 1910.146(c)(5)(ii)(E), forced air must come from a clean source and may not increase the hazards in the space. A blower that pulls in exhaust pushes carbon monoxide into the space where the entrant is breathing."}]}, {"slug": "electrical-safety", "title": "Electrical Safety", "standard": "29 CFR 1910 Subpart S", "standard_title": "Electrical, general industry (1910.303 to 1910.335), with 1910.269 for power line work", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Electricity is in every shop, plant and street cabinet, and a shock that would barely hurt at the wall outlet can kill when you are wet, grounded or 30 feet up a ladder. This course covers how electricity injures, who may work on energized parts, the de-energize and lock out rule, approach distances to overhead lines, 811 before digging, GFCIs and cord inspection, panel clearances, generators, downed lines and arc flash. Line crews follow 1910.269 and the department's energized work rules on top of this course.", "objectives": ["Explain how shock, electrocution, arc flash and arc blast injure the body and why a small current is enough to kill.", "Distinguish qualified from unqualified persons under 1910.332 and state the de-energize, lock out and verify rule in 1910.333.", "Keep yourself, your tools and your equipment at least 10 feet from overhead lines up to 50 kV and call 811 before any digging.", "Inspect cords and portable tools before each shift and use GFCI protection for temporary power and wet locations.", "Recognize unsafe panels, electrical rooms and generator setups, including the 36-inch working space and the backfeed hazard.", "Respond correctly to a downed power line, including when your vehicle is in contact with it."], "sections": [{"heading": "How electricity injures", "html": "<p>Electricity injures in several ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shock</strong> is current passing through your body. It can stop your breathing or throw your heart into <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong>, which kills within minutes. <strong>Electrocution</strong> is death by shock.</li>\n<li><strong>Burns</strong> form where current enters and leaves the body and from the heat of an arc.</li>\n<li><strong>Arc flash</strong> is the explosion of light and heat when current jumps through the air. OSHA notes arc temperatures can reach about 35,000 degrees Fahrenheit.</li>\n<li><strong>Arc blast</strong> is the pressure wave from that explosion, which throws people and molten metal.</li>\n<li><strong>Falls</strong> follow shock: a jolt that would not kill you on the ground knocks you off a ladder, pole or tank.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is <strong>current, measured in amps, that does the damage, not voltage</strong>. A fraction of one amp is enough:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Around <strong>5 milliamps</strong>: a definite shock. A GFCI trips at this level.</li>\n<li>Roughly <strong>10 to 30 milliamps</strong>: muscles lock and you <strong>cannot let go</strong> of the conductor.</li>\n<li><strong>50 to 150 milliamps</strong>: severe pain, breathing stops, death is possible.</li>\n<li><strong>About 1 amp and up</strong>: the heart stops pumping and death is likely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A 100-watt light bulb draws less than 1 amp. That is why ordinary 120-volt circuits kill people, especially when sweat, rain, standing water or a metal ladder lowers the body's resistance.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>Electricity kills workers who never meant to touch a wire: tree trimmers whose pole saw reached a line, laborers who raised an aluminum ladder into a service drop, operators who lifted a dump body or boom into a line, mechanics using a damaged drill in a wet shop, and plant operators who reached into a control panel.</p>\n<p>The hazard is invisible and silent, so the only protection is knowing the rules and following them when nothing looks wrong.</p>\n<p>This course is the general electrical safety training required under <strong>29 CFR 1910.332</strong> for employees who face a risk of shock that the wiring rules alone do not remove. For most of the department that means working near energized equipment, not on it. Line crews, substation and meter technicians work under <strong>1910.269</strong> and the department's energized work procedures, minimum approach distances, rubber goods and grounding rules; that training is separate and this course does not replace it.</p>"}, {"heading": "Qualified or unqualified, and de-energize first", "html": "<p>A <strong>qualified person</strong> has been trained in and has shown the skills and knowledge to work on specific equipment: telling exposed live parts from other parts, determining their voltage, and knowing the approach distances in 1910.333(c). You can be qualified for one piece of equipment and unqualified for the one next to it. Everyone else is <strong>unqualified</strong>, including most of the department. <strong>Unqualified persons do not work on energized parts, open energized panels or test circuits.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>De-energize first.</strong> Section 1910.333(a) requires live parts to be <strong>de-energized before anyone works on or near them</strong>, unless the employer can show that shutting down creates a greater hazard or is not feasible. De-energized means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The circuit is opened at the breaker or disconnect and <strong>locked out and tagged</strong> under the department's lockout/tagout program.</li>\n<li>A <strong>qualified person tests with a meter</strong> and confirms zero voltage before anyone touches the parts. A breaker in the off position proves nothing until the test is done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Training.</strong> Under 1910.332, employees who work near exposed parts at 50 volts or more are trained in the safety-related work practices in 1910.331 to 1910.335 that apply to their jobs, and unqualified persons also learn any other electrical safety practices they need.</p>"}, {"heading": "Overhead power lines and digging", "html": "<p><strong>Look up and live.</strong> Before you raise a dump body, boom, bucket, ladder, pole saw or length of pipe, look up and find the lines.</p>\n<p>Section 1910.333(c)(3) sets the distance for <strong>unqualified persons</strong> and for <strong>vehicles and equipment</strong>: <strong>at least 10 feet from any energized overhead line rated up to 50 kV</strong>, measured to the person or the longest conductive thing they hold, and to any part of the equipment or its load. Above 50 kV, add <strong>4 inches for every 10 kV</strong>. For cranes and other hoisting equipment on construction work, <strong>1926.1408</strong> is stricter: stay <strong>20 feet</strong> away unless the utility has confirmed the voltage, and then the standard's table applies, starting at 10 feet.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a <strong>spotter</strong> whenever equipment works within a boom's length of a line.</li>\n<li>Fiberglass ladders only near lines.</li>\n<li>If a job cannot keep the distance, stop and have the utility de-energize, ground or cover the line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Call 811 before you dig.</strong> Section 1926.651 requires the location of underground utilities to be determined before an excavation is opened. Call <strong>811</strong> or use the state one-call website the required number of business days before any digging. Wait for the marks, <strong>hand dig or vacuum excavate</strong> inside the tolerance zone around each mark, and call again if the marks fade or the ticket expires.</p>"}, {"heading": "Downed power lines", "html": "<p><strong>Assume every downed line is energized</strong>, along with the fence, guardrail, puddle, vehicle or tree it touches. Lines can re-energize automatically after a fault.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stay back at least 35 feet</strong>, about the length of a city bus.</li>\n<li><strong>Never drive over a downed line</strong> or under a line hanging low.</li>\n<li>Call 911 and dispatch, and keep eyes on it until a line crew arrives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a line falls on your truck or your equipment touches one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stay in the cab.</strong> The vehicle is energized, but you are safe inside as long as you do not touch the ground. Warn everyone to stay away.</li>\n<li>If you can, drive clear, then stay put until a qualified person says it is safe.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave only if the vehicle is on fire.</strong> <strong>Jump clear with both feet together</strong>, never touching the vehicle and the ground at the same time, then <strong>shuffle away with your feet together</strong> without lifting them. Current spreads through the ground in rings of falling voltage; a long step puts each foot in a different ring and sends current through your body.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Cords, tools and GFCIs", "html": "<p>A <strong>ground fault circuit interrupter (GFCI)</strong> compares the current going out and coming back. If about 5 milliamps leaks, through you for example, it cuts the power instantly. Under 1910.304(b)(3) and 1926.404(b)(1), GFCI protection is required for receptacles powering tools on temporary wiring during construction, maintenance and repair work. <strong>Every portable tool and cord used outdoors, in a wet or damp location, in a plant or near water runs through a GFCI</strong>, in the receptacle or a portable GFCI at the cord end. Press the test button before you use it.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspect before each shift.</strong> Section 1910.334(a)(2) requires a visual check of cord- and plug-connected tools and extension cords before use on any shift. Take it out of service if you find:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cut, cracked, nicked or melted insulation, or a jacket pulled out of the plug.</li>\n<li>A <strong>missing ground pin</strong> on a three-prong plug.</li>\n<li><strong>Tape or splices</strong> on the cord. Tape is not a repair.</li>\n<li>Crushed or pinched spots that may hide broken conductors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Extension cord rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three-wire cords rated for hard or extra-hard usage</strong>, marked on the jacket.</li>\n<li><strong>No cords through doorways, windows or holes in walls</strong>, under rugs, or where they can be pinched, driven over or hung on nails.</li>\n<li><strong>No daisy chaining</strong> cords or power strips. It overloads and overheats the cord.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Double insulated tools</strong> have a two-prong plug and the square-within-a-square symbol; the missing ground pin is not a defect, but they still need a GFCI in wet locations.</p>"}, {"heading": "Panels, electrical rooms, generators and arc flash", "html": "<p><strong>Panels and disconnects.</strong> Under 1910.303 and 1910.305:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep <strong>at least 36 inches of clear working space</strong> in front of every panel, disconnect and motor control center, and at least 30 inches wide. <strong>That space is not storage.</strong></li>\n<li>Covers stay on and closed, <strong>every breaker and disconnect is labeled</strong> with what it controls, and missing knockouts and open holes in boxes get plugged. Resetting a breaker on a closed dead-front panel is fine; taking the cover off is qualified work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arc flash.</strong> Working on or near energized parts in a panel, even taking a voltage reading, risks an arc. <strong>NFPA 70E</strong> is the consensus standard for protecting against it. It requires <strong>arc-rated clothing and face protection, insulated tools and voltage-rated gloves</strong> for qualified persons working inside the arc flash boundary. If you are not trained and dressed for it, you do not open the door.</p>\n<p><strong>Portable generators.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Never run a generator indoors</strong>, in a garage, vault or pump house. Keep it at least 20 feet from doors, windows and intakes with the exhaust pointed away; carbon monoxide kills quietly.</li>\n<li><strong>Never plug a generator into a wall outlet.</strong> That <strong>backfeeds</strong> the building wiring and the utility transformer and can energize a line that a line crew believes is dead. Power a building only through a <strong>transfer switch</strong> installed by a qualified person.</li>\n<li>Ground the generator as the manufacturer requires; a unit connected to a building needs a ground rod.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Your energized work, minimum approach distances, rubber goods, grounding and bucket truck rules come from <strong>1910.269</strong> and the department's procedures, not this course. Everything else here applies around the truck and in the shop.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Plants and pump stations are <strong>wet locations</strong> full of motors, drives and control panels. Portable pumps, lights and tools run on GFCIs, cords stay out of the water, and nothing gets plugged in with wet hands. Operators reset breakers with the panel cover closed; anything inside the enclosure waits for the electrician. Collection crews opening vaults or digging call 811 every time.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Dump truck drivers check for lines before raising the bed, every load, and lower it before moving. Forestry crews treat any tree within 10 feet of a line as utility work and bring in Electric Services or the line clearance contractor. Signal and streetlight technicians are qualified for their own cabinets and poles and unqualified for everything else. Facilities staff keep 36 inches in front of panels and never use steel wool or wet mops near energized parts.</p>\n<p>For everyone: look up before you lift, call before you dig, test before you touch, and if you are not qualified, do not open it.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Current, not voltage, kills; a few milliamps locks your muscles and a fraction of an amp can stop your heart, so ordinary 120-volt circuits are deadly, especially when you are wet or grounded.", "Most crew members are unqualified persons: never work on energized parts, and de-energize, lock out and test with a meter before any electrical work.", "Keep yourself, your tools and every part of your equipment at least 10 feet from overhead lines up to 50 kV, more for higher voltage, and look up before raising a bed, boom, bucket or ladder.", "Call 811 before any digging and hand dig or vacuum excavate inside the tolerance zone around the marks.", "Use GFCI protection for portable tools, temporary power and all wet locations, and inspect cords and tools before every shift; damaged insulation, missing ground pins and taped repairs take the item out of service.", "Keep 36 inches of clear working space in front of panels, keep covers on and breakers labeled, and never store anything in an electrical room.", "Run generators outdoors at least 20 feet from the building and never plug one into a wall outlet; backfeed can kill a line worker.", "Treat every downed line as energized, stay at least 35 feet back, never drive over one, and if your vehicle contacts a line, stay inside unless it is on fire, then jump clear with feet together and shuffle away."], "questions": [{"text": "Which statement about electrical injury is correct?", "options": ["Only voltages above 600 volts can kill", "Dry skin makes shock impossible", "Voltage, not current, is what stops the heart", "A current well under 1 amp can cause the heart to fibrillate"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Current does the damage. Around 50 to 150 milliamps can stop breathing, and currents near 1 amp cause the heart to fibrillate, which is why ordinary 120-volt circuits kill people every year."}, {"text": "A mechanic has switched off the breaker for a shop air compressor and wants to work inside the motor junction box. What else must happen before anyone touches the wiring?", "options": ["Nothing, since the breaker is off", "The circuit must be locked out and tagged, and a qualified person must test it to verify zero voltage", "A coworker must stand at the breaker to make sure no one turns it on", "The compressor must be disconnected from its air line"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1910.333(b) requires the circuit to be locked out or tagged and a qualified person to test the parts and verify they are de-energized before work begins. An open breaker proves nothing until a meter confirms zero voltage."}, {"text": "A dump truck will raise its bed near a 12.47 kV distribution line. What is the minimum clearance under 1910.333(c)(3)?", "options": ["10 feet", "3 feet", "6 feet", "20 feet"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "For lines rated up to 50 kV, vehicles and mechanical equipment that can be elevated near overhead lines must keep 10 feet of clearance, measured to any part of the equipment or its load. Cranes on construction work follow the stricter 20-foot rule in 1926.1408 unless the voltage is confirmed."}, {"text": "Which of these is required before digging to set a new sign post?", "options": ["Only a visual check of the area for utility markers", "Calling 811 only if the hole will be deeper than 4 feet", "Calling 811 or the state one-call center and waiting for the utilities to be marked", "Asking the property owner where the lines are"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Underground utility locations must be determined before any excavation is opened, and the one-call center is how that is done. Depth does not matter; services and cables are often shallow, and you hand dig inside the tolerance zone around the marks."}, {"text": "A crew is running a portable pump from a temporary cord in a flooded vault. What protects the crew from a ground fault?", "options": ["A three-prong plug on the pump", "A heavier gauge extension cord", "Standing on the vault ladder while the pump runs", "GFCI protection at the receptacle or a portable GFCI at the cord"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A GFCI senses leaking current of about 5 milliamps and cuts the power before the current reaches a level that locks muscles or stops the heart. A ground pin or a heavier cord does not detect a fault in a wet location."}, {"text": "During a pre-shift check, a drill's cord has a section wrapped in electrical tape. What is the correct action?", "options": ["Take the drill out of service and tag it until it is properly repaired", "Use it, since the tape covers the damaged spot", "Add another layer of tape and use it only indoors", "Cut off the taped section and twist the wires together"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Section 1910.334(a)(2) requires damaged cord- and plug-connected equipment to be removed from service until it is repaired and tested. Tape does not restore the jacket or the insulation, so a taped cord is a damaged cord."}, {"text": "How much clear working space is required in front of an electrical panel, and may it be used for storage?", "options": ["18 inches, and storage is allowed if it is on a cart", "36 inches, and the space may not be used for storage", "24 inches, and only flammable items are prohibited", "There is no required distance as long as the cover is closed"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1910.303(g)(1) requires at least 36 inches of working space in front of equipment that may need servicing while energized, and states that the working space may not be used for storage. Someone has to be able to reach the disconnect in an emergency."}, {"text": "Why must a portable generator never be plugged into a wall outlet to power a building?", "options": ["The outlet cannot supply enough current to the generator", "It voids the generator warranty", "It backfeeds the building wiring and the utility line, which can electrocute a line worker", "It trips the GFCI on the generator"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Power fed into an outlet travels back through the building wiring and the utility transformer and can energize a line that a crew believes is dead. A transfer switch isolates the building from the utility before the generator is connected."}, {"text": "How far should you stay from a downed power line?", "options": ["At least 35 feet", "At least 10 feet", "At least 3 feet", "Far enough that you cannot hear it humming"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Treat every downed line as energized and stay at least 35 feet back, about the length of a city bus. A live line does not have to hum or spark, and current spreads through the ground around the contact point."}, {"text": "Your truck's boom contacts an energized line and the truck is not on fire. What do you do?", "options": ["Climb down the side of the truck carefully", "Step out onto the ground and run", "Have a coworker open the door from outside", "Stay in the cab, warn others to stay away and wait for the line to be de-energized"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Inside the cab you are at the same voltage as the truck and no current flows through you. Touching the truck and the ground at the same time completes the circuit. Only a fire justifies leaving, and then you jump clear with both feet together and shuffle away."}]}, {"slug": "ergonomics", "title": "Ergonomics and Safe Lifting", "standard": "General Duty Clause, OSH Act Section 5(a)(1)", "standard_title": "Ergonomic hazards and musculoskeletal disorders (General Duty Clause; NIOSH lifting guidance)", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Sprains, strains and back injuries are among the most common and most costly injuries in public works, and most of them build up from ordinary lifting, shoveling, driving and tool use. This course covers how musculoskeletal disorders happen, the risk factors that stack up on a route or a job site, how to lift and move loads safely, when to reach for a cart or a hoist instead of your back, and why reporting an ache early keeps it from becoming surgery.", "objectives": ["Recognize the common musculoskeletal disorders and the seven risk factors that cause them.", "Explain how OSHA enforces ergonomic hazards under the General Duty Clause and what the NIOSH 51-pound limit means in practice.", "Demonstrate the safe lifting steps from planning the lift to setting the load down, and know when a lift needs two people or a machine.", "Choose the mechanical aid, tool and work method that takes the load off the body for common field and shop tasks.", "Apply ergonomic practices in vehicles and equipment, including seat setup and three-point mounting and dismounting.", "Report early signs of discomfort and use warm-up, microbreaks and job rotation to reduce exposure."], "sections": [{"heading": "What musculoskeletal disorders are", "html": "<p>A <strong>musculoskeletal disorder (MSD)</strong> is an injury to the muscles, tendons, ligaments, nerves, joints or spinal discs. The ones public works crews see most are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sprains</strong> (stretched or torn ligaments) and <strong>strains</strong> (stretched or torn muscles or tendons), usually in the lower back, shoulders and knees.</li>\n<li><strong>Back injuries</strong>, from a pulled muscle to a herniated disc pressing on a nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Tendinitis</strong>, an inflamed tendon from repeated use, common in the shoulder, elbow and wrist.</li>\n<li><strong>Carpal tunnel syndrome</strong>, pressure on the nerve in the wrist that causes numbness, tingling and a weak grip.</li>\n<li>Rotator cuff tears, hernias and knee damage from kneeling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some MSDs happen in one moment, like a bad lift of a manhole cover. Most build up over months of small overloads until a tendon or disc gives way on an ordinary task. MSDs are among the most common and most costly injuries in public works: lost time, lost strength and sometimes a career for the worker, and overtime, light duty and claims for the department.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>Nearly every public works job loads the body hard. Line crews handle crossarms, transformers and reels. Water crews lift valve box lids and pull meters from pits. Collection crews lift manhole covers and drag hose. Streets crews shovel asphalt and run breakers. Refuse crews step off a truck hundreds of times a shift and tip carts that weigh more than they do. Plow and sweeper operators sit through twelve-hour routes. Operators and dispatchers hold one posture for hours.</p>\n<p>None of that is going away. What this training changes is how you do it. A back injury does not announce itself, and the day it happens is usually not the day it started. Planning the lift, getting close, keeping the back neutral, using the cart, adjusting the seat and reporting the ache early are what keep a 30-year career from ending at year 15.</p>"}, {"heading": "Risk factors and how they stack up", "html": "<p>Seven risk factors drive MSDs. Each one alone raises the odds. Combined, and held long enough, they multiply.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Force.</strong> Heavy lifting, pushing, pulling, carrying and hard gripping.</li>\n<li><strong>Repetition.</strong> The same motion over and over with little recovery: lifting bags, setting cones, pulling hose.</li>\n<li><strong>Awkward posture.</strong> Bending, reaching overhead, twisting, kneeling, working with the wrist bent or the arm out from the body.</li>\n<li><strong>Static posture.</strong> Holding one position for a long time: crouching in a trench, holding a tool overhead, sitting through a plow route.</li>\n<li><strong>Vibration.</strong> Hand-arm vibration from jackhammers, chainsaws, tampers and mowers; whole-body vibration from trucks, loaders and mowers.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact stress.</strong> A hard edge pressing into soft tissue: a shovel handle in the palm, a knee on concrete.</li>\n<li><strong>Cold.</strong> Cold cuts blood flow and grip strength, so the same task takes more force, and numb hands hide the warning signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Duration and recovery decide the damage. One 40-pound bag is work. Three hundred of them in a shift with a twist each time is an injury waiting for a date. Look at every task for which factors are present and which one you can remove.</p>"}, {"heading": "What OSHA requires and what NIOSH recommends", "html": "<p>OSHA has <strong>no specific ergonomics standard</strong>. It enforces ergonomic hazards under the <strong>General Duty Clause, Section 5(a)(1) of the OSH Act</strong>, which requires every employer to provide a workplace free from recognized hazards that are causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm. In practice that means a recognized hazard causing serious injuries with a feasible fix the employer did not use, such as repeated back injuries on a task where a cart or hoist was available.</p>\n<p>The practical guidance comes from the <strong>NIOSH lifting equation</strong>. It sets <strong>51 pounds</strong> as the maximum recommended weight for a two-handed lift under <strong>ideal conditions</strong>: load held close to the body at about waist height, lifted a short distance, no twisting, good handles, only occasional lifts. Every departure from ideal cuts that number. Hold the load 20 inches out from the body instead of 10 and the limit drops by half. Lifting from the floor, twisting or repeating it all morning cuts it further, and in a trench or a truck bed the recommended limit can fall below 20 pounds.</p>\n<p>The department's rule is simple: <strong>above about 50 pounds, or any load that is awkward, unbalanced or far from the body, get help or get equipment.</strong></p>"}, {"heading": "Safe lifting, team lifts and mechanical aids", "html": "<p><strong>Lifting by hand.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Plan the lift.</strong> Clear the path, check the footing and know where the load will be set down.</li>\n<li><strong>Test the load.</strong> Tip a corner or lift one edge to judge weight and balance before committing.</li>\n<li><strong>Get close.</strong> Load against the body, feet shoulder width apart, one foot slightly ahead for a stable base.</li>\n<li><strong>Bend the knees and hips and keep the back neutral.</strong> Natural curve, chin up, core tight.</li>\n<li><strong>Lift with the legs</strong>, smoothly, no jerk. Keep the load close at waist height.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not twist.</strong> Move your feet to turn. Twisting under load is how discs tear.</li>\n<li><strong>Set it down the same way</strong>, knees and hips, load close, fingers out of the pinch.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Team lifts.</strong> Two people and one load need one leader. Agree on the path, the grip and the commands before anyone bends. Lift and lower on the count, match pace, and say so out loud before shifting a grip.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical aids.</strong> The best lift is the one you do not make. Use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hand trucks, dollies and carts</strong> for anything that moves more than a few steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Hoists, cranes, lift gates and the digger derrick</strong> for motors, pumps, transformers, reels and poles.</li>\n<li><strong>Manhole cover lifters</strong>, magnetic or hook and lever, for every cover. A cover often weighs more than 100 pounds and sits at the worst height for a lift.</li>\n<li><strong>Cone placers and retrievers</strong> for lane closures.</li>\n<li><strong>Drum handlers and pallet jacks</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pole dollies, pole jacks and pole trailers</strong>, never shoulders alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the aid is back at the shop, go get it.</p>"}, {"heading": "Tools, vibration, vehicles and equipment", "html": "<p><strong>Tools.</strong> Pick the tool that keeps the wrist straight, the grip relaxed and the elbow near the body. A <strong>powered tool</strong> does the work a muscle used to, so use the impact wrench, the powered cover lifter and the hydraulic breaker when the department has them.</p>\n<p><strong>Vibration.</strong> Jackhammers, breakers, tampers, chainsaws and mowers send vibration into the hands and arms. Over time that causes <strong>hand-arm vibration syndrome</strong>: numb white fingers, lost grip and pain in the cold. Controls, in order: use the lowest vibration tool that does the job and keep its anti-vibration mounts in good shape; let the tool's weight do the work instead of pushing or squeezing; take breaks and rotate operators; keep hands warm and dry; and wear <strong>anti-vibration gloves</strong> that meet ANSI S2.73 as a supplement, not a substitute.</p>\n<p><strong>Vehicles and equipment.</strong> Adjust the seat before you move: hips to the back of the seat, knees slightly lower than hips, pedals reached without stretching, lumbar support against the low back, mirrors set so you do not twist. On long plow, sweeper and refuse routes, get out every couple of hours, walk and stretch. <strong>Mount and dismount facing the vehicle with three points of contact</strong>, two hands and a foot or two feet and a hand. <strong>Never jump from a cab, step or truck bed.</strong> A jump multiplies the load on knees and spine, and ice or a pothole turns it into a broken ankle.</p>"}, {"heading": "Shoveling, refuse routes, control rooms and reporting early", "html": "<p><strong>Shoveling and snow.</strong> Use a shovel that fits you, with a smaller blade for wet snow and asphalt. Push snow instead of lifting when you can. Take small bites, keep the loaded blade close, and <strong>move your feet to face where the snow goes</strong>; never throw over the shoulder or to the side with a twist. Cold air plus heavy shoveling strains the heart, so warm up, take breaks and speak up about chest pressure or dizziness.</p>\n<p><strong>Refuse collection and carts.</strong> <strong>Push carts instead of pulling</strong>, with both hands. Use the truck's cart tipper every time it is available; never lift a full cart to the hopper by hand. Lift bags and cans close and do not throw them over the hopper rim with a twist. Alternate sides and switch tasks with your partner through the route.</p>\n<p><strong>Office and control room.</strong> For operators, dispatchers and office staff, static posture is the hazard. Set the <strong>top of the monitor at or slightly below eye level</strong>, an arm's length away and straight ahead. Set the chair so feet are flat, the low back is supported and wrists are straight. Change position every 20 to 30 minutes and take a <strong>microbreak</strong> of a minute or two each hour to stand, walk and look at something far away.</p>\n<p><strong>Report early.</strong> MSDs give warning: aching, stiffness, tingling, numbness, swelling, a weak grip, pain that is still there the next morning. Report it to your supervisor when it starts. Early reporting usually means a task change or a different tool; late reporting means surgery and months away. Reporting is required and protected; the department may not discourage it.</p>\n<p><strong>Warm up, rotate, recover.</strong> Take a few minutes of easy movement before heavy work; warm muscles handle load better. Rotate between tasks that load different parts of the body and build recovery into the day.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Crossarms, insulators, reels, transformers and meters are heavy, awkward or both. Use the digger derrick, the bucket truck jib and the reel trailer, and plan two-person carries for anything over about 50 pounds. Meter technicians lift pit lids with the lifter, not fingers under the edge.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Manhole covers, valve box lids, hose, sample jugs, chemical drums and pumps make up a workday of lifts. Cover lifters, drum handlers and the plant hoists are not optional. Jet and vactor crews rotate hose handling. Refuse crews push carts, use the tipper, alternate sides and never jump from the step.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Streets crews shovel asphalt in small bites close to the body and use the cone placer for every closure. Forestry rotates saw operators and keeps chains sharp. Fleet mechanics use the hoist, creeper and transmission jack instead of holding parts overhead. Plow operators set the seat before the route and get out every couple of hours.</p>\n<p>A strain that took one lift to cause can take a year to heal. Get close, keep it neutral, do not twist, and get the cart.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Musculoskeletal disorders such as sprains, strains, back injuries, tendinitis and carpal tunnel syndrome are among the most common and most costly injuries in public works, and most build up over time.", "Force, repetition, awkward posture, static posture, vibration, contact stress and cold are the risk factors, and the risk multiplies when several are present for long periods.", "OSHA enforces ergonomic hazards under the General Duty Clause, and the NIOSH 51-pound limit applies only to ideal conditions; real limits are often far lower.", "Plan the lift, test the load, get close, keep a wide base, lift with the legs and a neutral back, do not twist, and set it down the same way.", "Above about 50 pounds, or for any awkward or unbalanced load, use a team lift or a mechanical aid such as a cart, hoist, lift gate, cover lifter or cone placer.", "Choose tools that keep the wrist straight, control vibration at the tool and with breaks and rotation, and wear anti-vibration gloves as a supplement.", "Set the seat before you drive, take breaks on long routes, mount and dismount facing the vehicle with three points of contact, and never jump from a cab or truck bed.", "Report aches, tingling and numbness early, warm up before heavy work, take microbreaks and rotate tasks."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of these is a musculoskeletal disorder?", "options": ["Carbon monoxide poisoning from a running engine in the shop", "A laceration from a utility knife", "Tendinitis in the shoulder from months of overhead work", "Hearing loss from years around a jackhammer"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Musculoskeletal disorders are injuries to muscles, tendons, ligaments, nerves, joints and spinal discs, such as sprains, strains, back injuries, tendinitis and carpal tunnel syndrome. Tendinitis from repeated overhead work is a classic MSD; the other choices are different kinds of injury."}, {"text": "A collection crew member lifts 40-pound bags from the ground, twisting to toss each one into the hopper, about 300 times a shift in January. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Force, repetition, awkward posture and cold are all present, and together they raise the risk far more than any one of them alone", "The task is acceptable because each bag is under the 51-pound NIOSH limit", "Only the twisting counts as a risk factor, since 40 pounds is a light load", "Cold weather lowers the risk because muscles work harder and stay warm"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Risk factors stack. Force, repetition, the awkward twisting posture and cold are each present, and the duration across a full shift multiplies the exposure. The 51-pound NIOSH figure applies only to ideal conditions and drops sharply with twisting, floor-level lifts and frequent repetition."}, {"text": "Under what authority does OSHA cite an employer for an ergonomic hazard?", "options": ["29 CFR 1910.900, the ergonomics program standard", "The General Duty Clause, Section 5(a)(1) of the OSH Act", "29 CFR 1926.1053, which sets lifting limits for construction", "OSHA cannot cite ergonomic hazards because there is no standard"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "OSHA has no specific ergonomics standard in force, so it enforces recognized ergonomic hazards under the General Duty Clause, which requires employers to provide a workplace free from recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm when a feasible fix exists."}, {"text": "The NIOSH lifting equation sets 51 pounds as the maximum recommended weight. Under what conditions does that number apply?", "options": ["Any two-handed lift by a worker in good physical condition", "Lifts from the floor to shoulder height with a firm grip", "Team lifts, with each person's share counted separately", "Ideal conditions only: load close to the body at about waist height, short lift distance, no twisting, good handles and only occasional lifting"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The 51-pound figure is the load constant for ideal conditions. The equation reduces it for horizontal distance from the body, lifting from low or high positions, long vertical travel, twisting, frequency and poor grip, so real-world limits are usually much lower."}, {"text": "You have lifted a pump off a pallet and need to set it on a cart to your left. What is the correct move?", "options": ["Move your feet to turn your whole body toward the cart, keep the load close and lower with your legs", "Rotate at the waist while holding the pump at arm's length so you can see the cart", "Rest the pump on your knee, then swing it onto the cart", "Lean over the cart and drop the pump the last few inches to save your back"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Twisting under load is one of the main ways spinal discs are injured. Turning with the feet keeps the spine neutral, keeping the load close reduces the force on the back, and lowering with the legs sets the load down the same way it was lifted."}, {"text": "Two workers need to carry an 80-pound motor across the shop. What makes the team lift safe?", "options": ["Each person lifts their end as soon as they are ready, so neither waits with a load", "The stronger worker takes most of the weight so the other can steer", "One person leads, they agree on the path, grip and commands before bending, and they lift, move and lower on the count", "They carry it at arm's length so they can see their feet"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A team lift needs one leader, an agreed path and commands, and lifting and lowering on the count so the load does not shift to one person. The load stays close to both bodies, and anyone who needs to adjust says so out loud first."}, {"text": "A 24-inch manhole cover needs to come off for a sewer inspection. What is the right way to lift it?", "options": ["Pry up one edge with a screwdriver and slide fingers underneath to flip it", "Use a magnetic or hook-and-lever manhole cover lifter and keep the back neutral", "Have one worker stand on the far edge while another pulls up the near edge", "Loop a chain through the pick hole and lift it straight up by hand"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A manhole cover often weighs more than 100 pounds and sits at ground level far from the feet, the worst possible lifting position. The department's cover lifters are the mechanical aid for that job, and fingers never go under a cover's edge."}, {"text": "A streets crew member runs a jackhammer for most of a shift. Which control reduces hand-arm vibration exposure the most?", "options": ["Gripping the handles harder so the tool stays steady", "Wearing two pairs of ordinary leather gloves", "Leaning body weight onto the tool to break the pavement faster", "Using a lower vibration tool with working anti-vibration mounts, letting the tool's weight do the work, rotating operators and keeping hands warm"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The controls that reduce vibration exposure are a lower vibration tool in good repair, a relaxed grip that lets the tool work, breaks and operator rotation, warm hands and anti-vibration gloves that meet ANSI S2.73. Gripping harder and leaning in increase the vibration that reaches the hands."}, {"text": "A plow operator finishes a 12-hour route and needs to get out of the cab. What is the correct dismount?", "options": ["Face the cab, keep three points of contact on the steps and grab handles and step down to the ground", "Turn to face outward and jump down so both feet land at once", "Face outward and walk down the steps like a staircase while holding the door", "Slide out of the seat onto the top step and hop to the ground"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Mounting and dismounting facing the vehicle with three points of contact keeps you in control on steps that may be icy or muddy. Jumping or hopping from a cab or truck bed multiplies the load on the knees and spine and is a common cause of ankle, knee and back injuries."}, {"text": "A water plant operator notices tingling and numbness in her hand at the end of most shifts. What should she do?", "options": ["Wait to see if it goes away over the winter before saying anything", "Switch to using the other hand and keep it to herself so it is not held against her", "Report it to her supervisor now so the task, tool or workstation can be changed before it becomes a lasting injury", "Report it only after it keeps her from working a full shift"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tingling and numbness are early warning signs of an MSD such as carpal tunnel syndrome. Early reporting usually leads to a task change, a different tool or a workstation adjustment; waiting lets the injury progress until it needs surgery and time away. Employees may not be discouraged from reporting."}]}, {"slug": "fire-safety-extinguishers", "title": "Fire Safety and Fire Extinguishers", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.157", "standard_title": "Portable Fire Extinguishers, Emergency Action Plans and Fire Prevention Plans (1910.157, 1910.38, 1910.39)", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Fuel, solvents, hot work, batteries and running engines put fire hazards in every public works shop, plant and truck. This course covers how fires start and the five classes of fire, which extinguisher works on which fire, how to decide whether to fight a fire or get out, the PASS technique, and the placement and inspection rules in 1910.157. It also explains the emergency action plan and fire prevention plan and what to do when a truck, chipper or refuse load catches fire.", "objectives": ["Name the three parts of the fire triangle and the five classes of fire, with an example of each from public works.", "Match extinguisher types to fire classes and explain why water is never used on flammable liquid or energized electrical fires.", "Decide whether to fight a small fire or evacuate, and use the PASS technique from 6 to 8 feet back with an exit behind you.", "State the travel distance limits for Class A and Class B extinguishers and the items checked in a monthly visual inspection.", "Describe what the emergency action plan and fire prevention plan require of you under 1910.38 and 1910.39.", "Apply hot work, flammable liquid storage and fuel transfer precautions in the shop, and respond correctly to a vehicle or equipment fire."], "sections": [{"heading": "The fire triangle and the classes of fire", "html": "<p>A fire needs three things at once: <strong>fuel</strong>, <strong>heat</strong> and <strong>oxygen</strong>. Take any one away and the fire goes out. Water cools, dry chemical coats the fuel, carbon dioxide pushes out the oxygen, and putting the gas can away removes the fuel before a fire starts.</p>\n<p>Fires are grouped by what is burning, because the fuel decides what will put it out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Class A: ordinary combustibles.</strong> Wood, paper, cardboard, cloth, trash, brush, plastics and tires.</li>\n<li><strong>Class B: flammable liquids and gases.</strong> Gasoline, diesel, hydraulic oil, grease, paint, solvents, propane and acetylene.</li>\n<li><strong>Class C: energized electrical equipment.</strong> Panels, motors, chargers, cords, signal cabinets and vehicle wiring. Once the power is off, it becomes a Class A or B fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Class D: combustible metals.</strong> Magnesium, titanium, sodium and lithium. Rare in public works, but magnesium shows up in some vehicle and equipment parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Class K: cooking oils and fats.</strong> Fryers and grills in a facility kitchen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many fires are a mix: a truck fire is burning plastic, diesel and live wiring at the same time.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>Look around a fleet shop: fuel in every vehicle, solvent in the parts washer, oxygen and acetylene cylinders, battery chargers, grinders throwing sparks and rags soaked in oil. A water plant has chemical rooms, switchgear and generators. A refuse truck carries whatever a resident threw away, including batteries and hot ashes. Every division has fire hazards.</p>\n<p>A hand-held extinguisher is for a fire in its early stage, smaller than a trash can. After that, the only safe move is to get everyone out and let the fire department work.</p>\n<p>OSHA's <strong>Portable Fire Extinguishers standard, 29 CFR 1910.157</strong>, applies because the department provides extinguishers for you to use. Paragraph (g) requires training on the general principles of extinguisher use and the hazards of fighting an early-stage fire, <strong>at initial employment and at least annually</strong>. Employees designated in the emergency action plan to use extinguishers get hands-on training as well.</p>"}, {"heading": "Extinguisher types and what they are for", "html": "<p>The label tells you what an extinguisher fights. Letters show the classes and numbers show capacity. A <strong>2-A:10-B:C</strong> unit fights Class A, B and C fires; higher numbers mean bigger fires.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multipurpose dry chemical (ABC).</strong> The red extinguisher on most trucks and shop walls. It works on Class A, B and C fires by coating the fuel. It leaves a corrosive powder to clean up.</li>\n<li><strong>Carbon dioxide (CO2).</strong> For Class B and C fires. It leaves no residue, so it is the choice near panels, switchgear, computers and in substations. Short range, and little effect on Class A fires.</li>\n<li><strong>Water (air-pressurized).</strong> Class A only.</li>\n<li><strong>Class D dry powder.</strong> Only for burning metal, applied to smother it.</li>\n<li><strong>Class K wet chemical.</strong> For kitchen grease fires. It turns the oil into a soapy foam that seals it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Never put water on a Class B or Class C fire.</strong> Water spreads burning fuel across the floor, and it conducts electricity straight back to you. On an electrical fire, <strong>cut the power first</strong> if you can reach the breaker or disconnect safely, then use CO2 or ABC. With the power off, the fire is Class A or B, but not before.</p>"}, {"heading": "Fight or leave, then PASS", "html": "<p>Before you pull a pin, decide whether this is your fire to fight. Fight it only if <strong>every</strong> one of these is true:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>alarm has been raised</strong> and someone is calling 911.</li>\n<li>The fire is <strong>small and contained</strong>, no bigger than a trash can, and not spreading.</li>\n<li>You have a <strong>clear exit at your back</strong>, with the fire not between you and the door.</li>\n<li>You have the <strong>right extinguisher</strong> for the class of fire, and it is charged.</li>\n<li>You are <strong>trained</strong> and feel able to do it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If any answer is no, <strong>leave</strong>, close the door behind you and go to the assembly point.</p>\n<p>If you do fight it, use <strong>PASS</strong> from <strong>6 to 8 feet back</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pull</strong> the pin.</li>\n<li><strong>Aim</strong> the nozzle or horn <strong>at the base of the fire</strong>, not the flames.</li>\n<li><strong>Squeeze</strong> the handle.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweep</strong> side to side across the base, moving in slowly as the fire dies down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most hand-held extinguishers empty in 10 to 25 seconds. If the fire is not out when the extinguisher is empty, or it flares back up, get out. <strong>Never turn your back</strong> on a fire while you walk away. A used extinguisher goes for recharge even if you squeezed it once.</p>"}, {"heading": "Where extinguishers go and how they are kept ready", "html": "<p>Section 1910.157(c) requires extinguishers to be <strong>mounted, located and identified</strong> so they are <strong>readily accessible</strong>, kept <strong>fully charged</strong> and in their <strong>designated place</strong> at all times except when in use.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Travel distance.</strong> For <strong>Class A</strong> hazards, no one should have to travel more than <strong>75 feet</strong> to reach an extinguisher. For <strong>Class B</strong> hazards, the limit is <strong>50 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mounting.</strong> On a bracket or in a cabinet, never loose on the floor, label out, area in front clear, and a sign where it is hard to see.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Monthly visual inspection.</strong> Once a month, someone checks each extinguisher:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is in its place, visible and not blocked.</li>\n<li>The <strong>gauge needle is in the green</strong>, or a CO2 unit weighs what its label says.</li>\n<li>The <strong>pin is in and the tamper seal is unbroken</strong>.</li>\n<li>No dents, rust, leaks, cracked hose or clogged nozzle.</li>\n<li>The <strong>inspection tag</strong> is initialed and dated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Annual maintenance</strong> is a thorough check by a qualified person, recorded on the tag. <strong>Hydrostatic testing</strong> pressure-tests the shell: every <strong>5 years</strong> for CO2 and water units and every <strong>12 years</strong> for the common stored-pressure dry chemical type, which is also emptied and examined inside every <strong>6 years</strong>. Out-of-date tag or gauge in the red: pull it and tell your supervisor.</p>"}, {"heading": "Emergency action plans and fire prevention plans", "html": "<p>The <strong>emergency action plan (1910.38)</strong> is written, kept in the workplace and available to you. It covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>How to <strong>report</strong> a fire or other emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Evacuation routes</strong> and exit assignments, posted on exit maps.</li>\n<li>Which employees <strong>stay to operate critical equipment</strong> before evacuating, and when they leave.</li>\n<li>The <strong>assembly point</strong> and the <strong>headcount</strong> so no one is left inside unknown.</li>\n<li>Who performs rescue or medical duties.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The alarm must be distinctive, and the plan is reviewed with you when you are hired, when your duties change and when the plan changes. When you hear it, go.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fire prevention plan (1910.39)</strong> lists the <strong>major fire hazards</strong> in each facility, how hazardous materials are stored and handled, the <strong>ignition sources</strong> and how they are controlled, and the equipment that protects against each hazard. It sets procedures to <strong>control the build-up of flammable and combustible waste</strong>, such as oily rags in closed metal cans and brush piles kept away from buildings, and names the <strong>employees responsible</strong> for ignition sources and fuel hazards. You are told the fire hazards of your job when first assigned.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hot work and flammable liquids in the shop", "html": "<p><strong>Hot work</strong> is welding, cutting, grinding and anything else that throws sparks or flame. Under 1910.252: move the work to a safe place; if you cannot, move combustibles at least <strong>35 feet</strong> away or cover them with fire-resistant blankets; if you cannot do that either, do not start. Keep a <strong>charged extinguisher within reach</strong> and post a <strong>fire watch</strong> whenever combustibles are within 35 feet or sparks could reach them through openings. The fire watch stays <strong>at least 30 minutes after the work ends</strong>. Never weld or cut on a drum, tank or container that held fuel until it has been thoroughly cleaned. On a vehicle, disconnect the battery and keep sparks away from fuel lines and tanks.</p>\n<p><strong>Flammable liquids</strong> such as gasoline and most solvents, and <strong>combustible liquids</strong> such as diesel and hydraulic oil, are stored under 1910.106:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only in <strong>approved containers</strong>. For gasoline that means a <strong>safety can</strong> of 5 gallons or less with a spring-closing lid and spout cover, or a listed plastic can.</li>\n<li>In a listed <strong>flammable storage cabinet</strong> marked 'Flammable, Keep Fire Away', with no more than <strong>60 gallons</strong> of flammable liquid per cabinet.</li>\n<li><strong>Bond and ground when transferring fuel.</strong> Clip the bonding wire between the drum or nozzle and the container so static cannot spark the vapor, and fill portable cans on the ground, never in a truck bed.</li>\n<li><strong>No smoking, grinding or open flame</strong> near fuel.</li>\n<li><strong>Oily rags</strong> go in a closed, self-closing metal can emptied daily.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p>The fires most likely to find you are on wheels. For any <strong>vehicle or equipment fire</strong>: stop away from buildings, fuel pumps and overhead lines, shut off the engine, get everyone out and away, and call 911. If it is small and you have the right extinguisher, attack from upwind. On an engine fire, <strong>do not throw the hood open</strong>; the rush of air feeds it. Crack it or discharge through the grille.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> A <strong>bucket truck hydraulic fire</strong> usually starts when a leaking hose sprays oil mist onto a hot exhaust. Shut down the engine and PTO, bring the bucket down on the emergency controls if someone is in it, and use the truck's ABC extinguisher. In substations and switchgear rooms, <strong>de-energize first</strong> and use CO2.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> A <strong>refuse truck load fire</strong>, often started by a battery or hot ashes, is handled by dumping the load. Notify dispatch and 911, drive to a safe open area away from buildings, vehicles, storm drains and overhead lines, <strong>eject the load</strong>, pull the truck clear and let the fire department put it out. Never fight a fire inside the hopper.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> <strong>Chipper fires</strong> start in hot bearings, hydraulic fluid and packed dry debris: shut down, back away and use the extinguisher on the chipper or truck. Snow crews fueling at 3 a.m. still shut off the engine and bond the can.</p>\n<p>Everyone: know the two nearest extinguishers and where you meet outside.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen; removing any one puts it out, and the class of fire (A, B, C, D or K) tells you which extinguisher will do that safely.", "ABC dry chemical covers most fires on trucks and in shops, CO2 is for electrical and flammable liquid fires near sensitive equipment, and water is for Class A only, never for burning liquids or energized equipment.", "Fight a fire only if it is small and contained, the alarm is raised, you have the right extinguisher, you are trained and you have an exit at your back; otherwise get out and close the door.", "Use PASS from 6 to 8 feet back: Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side, and never turn your back on the fire.", "Extinguishers must be mounted, visible and within 75 feet of travel for Class A hazards and 50 feet for Class B hazards, inspected visually every month and maintained by a qualified person every year.", "The emergency action plan tells you how to report a fire, where to go and how everyone is counted; the fire prevention plan controls fuel, waste and ignition sources and names who is responsible.", "Hot work needs combustibles cleared 35 feet, an extinguisher at hand and a fire watch for at least 30 minutes afterward; fuel stays in approved cans and cabinets and is bonded during transfer.", "For a burning refuse load, call it in, dump the load in a safe open area and let the fire department put it out; never fight a fire inside the hopper."], "questions": [{"text": "Wiring in a running pump motor at a lift station catches fire. What class of fire is it, and what changes once the power is cut?", "options": ["Class A, and it becomes Class B when de-energized", "Class B, and it stays Class B", "Class C, and it becomes a Class A or B fire once de-energized", "Class D, because the motor housing is metal"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A fire in energized electrical equipment is Class C. Once the equipment is de-energized, what is left burning is insulation, plastic or oil, which makes it a Class A or B fire."}, {"text": "Why must water never be used on a gasoline fire?", "options": ["Water spreads the burning fuel instead of putting it out", "Water cannot reach the base of a liquid fire", "Water evaporates too fast to cool gasoline", "Water reacts chemically with gasoline and explodes"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Gasoline floats on water, so a stream of water carries the burning fuel across the floor and makes the fire bigger. Class B fires are fought with ABC dry chemical or CO2."}, {"text": "A small fire has started in a parts washer and is spreading up the wall toward the ceiling. The alarm is sounding and you have an ABC extinguisher. What should you do?", "options": ["Fight it, because you have the right extinguisher", "Switch to a water extinguisher for more reach", "Open the overhead door to clear the smoke, then fight it", "Leave, close the door behind you and go to the assembly point"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A fire that is spreading is no longer small and contained, so one of the conditions for fighting it has failed. Having the right extinguisher does not change that. Get out, close the door to slow the fire and let the fire department handle it."}, {"text": "Where do you aim an extinguisher, and from how far away?", "options": ["At the top of the flames, from 15 feet", "At the base of the fire, from 6 to 8 feet back", "At the smoke, from as close as possible", "At the ceiling above the fire, from 3 feet"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The A in PASS is Aim at the base of the fire, where the fuel is. Starting 6 to 8 feet back keeps you out of the heat and lets you move in as the fire dies down."}, {"text": "What is the maximum travel distance to an extinguisher for Class A hazards under 1910.157?", "options": ["75 feet", "50 feet", "100 feet", "25 feet"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Section 1910.157(d)(2) sets the travel distance for Class A hazards at 75 feet or less. For Class B hazards it is 50 feet or less, because flammable liquid fires grow faster."}, {"text": "Which of these is part of the monthly visual inspection of an extinguisher?", "options": ["Discharging it briefly to confirm it works", "Weighing a dry chemical unit on a shop scale", "Checking that the pin is in place and the tamper seal is unbroken", "Hydrostatic testing of the shell"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The monthly check confirms the extinguisher is in place, charged, undamaged and ready: gauge in the green, pin and seal intact, no damage, tag initialed. Discharging a unit means it must be recharged, and hydrostatic testing is done every 5 or 12 years by a qualified service."}, {"text": "Under 1910.38, which of these must the emergency action plan include?", "options": ["The purchase price of each extinguisher", "Procedures to account for all employees after evacuation", "A list of flammable liquids in each cabinet", "The hydrostatic test dates for all extinguishers"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The emergency action plan must include procedures to account for all employees after an evacuation, along with reporting procedures, evacuation routes, duties for employees who stay to operate critical equipment, and who to contact for more information. The headcount at the assembly point is how the fire department knows whether to search."}, {"text": "A mechanic finishes welding a plow frame near a workbench with cardboard boxes 20 feet away. What does 1910.252 require?", "options": ["Nothing further, since the work is done", "A fire watch for 10 minutes", "Hosing down the area with water", "A fire watch for at least 30 minutes after the work ends"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "When combustibles are within 35 feet of hot work, a fire watch is required during the work and for at least a half hour after it ends, to catch smoldering material that sparks may have reached."}, {"text": "What prevents a static spark when filling a portable gas can from a shop dispenser?", "options": ["Filling it in the truck bed so it is above the ground", "Filling it as fast as possible to limit vapor", "Bonding the nozzle to the can and filling it on the ground", "Using a plastic funnel"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Fuel flowing through a nozzle builds a static charge. Bonding the nozzle to the container and setting the can on the ground lets that charge bleed off instead of jumping as a spark through gasoline vapor. A can in a truck bed or on a plastic liner is insulated and can spark."}, {"text": "A refuse truck's load begins to smoke and flame on the route. What is the correct response?", "options": ["Drive to the transfer station and dump it on the tipping floor", "Notify dispatch and 911, drive to a safe open area away from buildings and overhead lines, eject the load and move the truck clear", "Open the hopper and discharge an ABC extinguisher into the load", "Continue the route so the load compacts and smothers the fire"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A burning load is dumped where it can burn without spreading, in an open area clear of buildings, vehicles, storm drains and overhead lines, and the fire department puts it out. Dumping it on a tipping floor spreads the fire indoors, and fighting it inside the hopper puts you next to a compacting, burning load."}]}, {"slug": "workplace-violence", "title": "Workplace Violence and Active Shooter Response", "standard": "OSH Act Section 5(a)(1)", "standard_title": "Workplace violence prevention (General Duty Clause; OSHA and DHS guidance)", "category": "general", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": true, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Public works crews meet the public every day, often alone, on private property, and sometimes while delivering unwelcome news like a shutoff notice or a road closure. This course defines workplace violence, teaches situational awareness, explains the warning signs to report and how to calm an angry resident or driver, and covers the Run, Hide, Fight response to an active shooter. It also explains how to act when police arrive and how the department supports crews afterward.", "objectives": ["Define workplace violence and identify the four types described by OSHA and NIOSH.", "Practice situational awareness in the field by scanning, noting exits, spotting changes from the normal baseline and limiting distractions.", "Recognize warning signs of potential violence in coworkers and members of the public and report them through the department's reporting path.", "De-escalate a confrontation with an angry resident or driver and disengage safely when it is not working.", "Apply the Run, Hide, Fight framework to an active shooter event, including what to do when law enforcement arrives.", "Describe the practices the department and each crew use to reduce the risk of violence, such as check-ins for lone workers and radio duress procedures."], "sections": [{"heading": "What counts as workplace violence", "html": "<p>Workplace violence is any act or threat of physical violence, harassment, intimidation or other threatening, disruptive behavior at the work site. That is OSHA's definition. It runs from threats and verbal abuse to physical assault and homicide. A resident who yells that he will 'find out where you live' has committed workplace violence even though he never touched anyone.</p>\n<p>OSHA and NIOSH sort it into <strong>four types</strong>, based on who the attacker is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type 1, criminal intent.</strong> The attacker has no connection to the department and is there to commit a crime, such as stealing copper from a substation or tools from a truck.</li>\n<li><strong>Type 2, customer or member of the public.</strong> The attacker is someone the department serves: a resident, a ratepayer, a driver in a work zone. For crews who deal with the public all day, this is the type that comes up most.</li>\n<li><strong>Type 3, worker on worker.</strong> The attacker is a current or former employee.</li>\n<li><strong>Type 4, personal relationship.</strong> The attacker has a relationship with the victim, usually a current or former partner, and brings that conflict to the job.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>OSHA has no standard written just for workplace violence in public works. The duty comes from <strong>Section 5(a)(1) of the OSH Act</strong>, the General Duty Clause, which requires every employer to provide a workplace free from recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious harm. Violence is one of those hazards. OSHA and the Department of Homeland Security both publish guidance on preventing it, and the department's policy follows that guidance.</p>\n<p>Public works crews are more exposed than most workers because of what the job asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You work in public spaces and private yards, often uninvited.</li>\n<li>You shut off water and power, post notices, enforce codes and collect fees. People take that personally.</li>\n<li>Refuse crews hear about missed pickups and damaged carts, sometimes with a fist on the hood.</li>\n<li>Flaggers and street crews close roads and meet drivers who are late and angry.</li>\n<li>You work alone or at night at lift stations, substations, water towers and remote yards.</li>\n<li>Trucks carry tools, copper and sometimes cash, which attracts theft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The point is not fear. It is to work with your eyes open, know the warning signs, and know exactly what you will do if a situation turns.</p>"}, {"heading": "Situational awareness", "html": "<p>Situational awareness means knowing what is going on around you and what it means for your safety. Most violent events give some warning, and people who are paying attention see it.</p>\n<p>Many police and security trainers teach a simple <strong>color code</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>White.</strong> Switched off. Headphones in, eyes on a screen. Fine on the couch, never on the job.</li>\n<li><strong>Yellow.</strong> Relaxed and alert. You notice who is around, where the exits are and what is normal here. This is your working state all day.</li>\n<li><strong>Orange.</strong> Something caught your attention: a man watching the crew from his porch for twenty minutes, or a coworker who went quiet and red in the face. You focus on it and start a plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Red.</strong> The threat is real and you act on the plan: leave, call or defend yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Habits that keep you in yellow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Know the baseline.</strong> Every street, lobby and plant has a normal feel. Notice when something is off.</li>\n<li><strong>Find two exits</strong> in any building, and park so you can pull away without backing up.</li>\n<li><strong>Put the phone away.</strong> Scrolling turns you white.</li>\n<li><strong>Trust your gut.</strong> If it feels wrong, step back and call a supervisor. You do not need proof.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fighter pilots use a loop called <strong>OODA: observe, orient, decide, act</strong>. Take in the scene, work out what it means, pick a response, do it, then look again.</p>"}, {"heading": "Warning signs, reporting and protective orders", "html": "<p>Most people who turn violent at work show signs first. Watch for these in coworkers and in the public:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anger that keeps escalating, or outbursts over small things.</li>\n<li>Threats, even 'joking' ones, or talk about weapons and hurting someone.</li>\n<li>Fixation on a grievance: a discipline, a denied claim, a shutoff.</li>\n<li>Intimidation or bullying.</li>\n<li>Substance abuse, or a sudden change in behavior, hygiene or attendance.</li>\n<li>Major life stress such as divorce, money trouble or a death in the family.</li>\n<li>A history of violence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One sign alone proves nothing, and your job is <strong>not to diagnose</strong> anyone. Your job is to <strong>report</strong> what you saw and heard to your supervisor, the safety office or human resources. If a threat is immediate, call 911 first. Reports go to a <strong>threat assessment team</strong> of human resources, safety, the division manager and, when needed, law enforcement, so no one person decides alone.</p>\n<p><strong>No retaliation.</strong> Reporting a concern in good faith is protected. No one may be disciplined, reassigned or frozen out for raising one.</p>\n<p><strong>Domestic violence follows people to work.</strong> If you have a protective order, or an ex-partner who has threatened you, tell your supervisor or human resources. A photo, a vehicle description and a copy of the order let the front desk, dispatch and gate staff keep that person out, and the department can adjust your schedule or route.</p>"}, {"heading": "De-escalating an angry resident or driver", "html": "<p>Most confrontations end with words, and how you use yours decides which way it goes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stay calm and lower your voice.</strong> A quiet, steady tone is contagious. So is yelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Give space.</strong> Stay about two arm lengths away, at an angle rather than square to the person, hands open and visible.</li>\n<li><strong>Listen first.</strong> Let them finish, then repeat the problem back: 'Your water is off and nobody told you.' People calm down when they feel heard.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not argue policy.</strong> You did not write the shutoff rule or close the road. Say what you can do, not what you cannot.</li>\n<li><strong>Offer the supervisor's number</strong> or a card. It gives them a way to win without fighting you.</li>\n<li><strong>Never block someone's exit</strong>, and never let them block yours. Keep yourself between the person and your truck or the open street.</li>\n<li><strong>Disengage and leave.</strong> If the person is not calming down, say you will come back another time, walk to the truck and go. Report it before your next stop so the next crew is warned.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not touch the person or trade insults, and do not threaten to call the police as a dare. If you need the police, step away and call them.</p>"}, {"heading": "Active shooter: Run, Hide, Fight", "html": "<p>An active shooter is a person actively killing or trying to kill people in a populated area. These events are usually over in minutes, often before police arrive, so the first seconds matter most. The Department of Homeland Security and the FBI teach three options, in order of preference:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Run.</strong> If there is a clear path away from the danger, take it. Leave tools and bags behind. Bring others if they will come; do not wait for anyone who refuses. Keep your hands visible and warn people away from the area. For field crews, <strong>the truck is your escape</strong>: get in, drive away from the sound and do not stop to look. Call 911 once you are safe.</li>\n<li><strong>Hide.</strong> If you cannot get out, get out of sight. Pick a room with a door that locks, lock it, turn off the lights and barricade the door with heavy furniture. <strong>Silence your phone completely</strong>, including vibrate. Get behind something solid, spread out, and stay quiet until law enforcement tells you it is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Fight.</strong> Only as a last resort, when your life is in immediate danger. Then <strong>commit completely</strong>; a half effort is worse than none. Use whatever is at hand: a fire extinguisher, a shovel, a chair, your body weight. Work as a group, yell, throw things and go for the weapon arm.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Know <strong>two exits</strong> from every building you work in, including the shop, the plant and city hall, and decide now what you would do in each one. There will be no time to decide then.</p>"}, {"heading": "When police arrive, and what comes next", "html": "<p>Responding officers have one job: <strong>stop the shooter</strong>. They will go past wounded people to do it, and they do not know you from the attacker. Help them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drop anything in your hands. <strong>Empty hands up, fingers spread.</strong></li>\n<li>No sudden movements. Do not run toward officers or grab them for safety.</li>\n<li>Follow commands exactly, even if they are shouted.</li>\n<li>Do not stop officers to ask for help or directions. Keep moving in the direction they came from.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you call <strong>911</strong>, give the location, including the building and floor, the number of shooters, what they look like, the kind of weapons and how many people are hurt.</p>\n<p>Afterward, go to the <strong>rally point</strong> in your facility's plan and stay there. Supervisors account for every crew member by name and by radio. Do not go back in for anyone or anything, and expect to stay until police release you.</p>\n<p>The department will bring in the <strong>employee assistance program</strong> and a critical incident debriefing afterward, and you are expected to use them. Take the yearly active threat walkthrough at your facility seriously. Feet remember what they practiced.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> On a disconnect for nonpayment, send two people when the account has a history, keep the truck between you and the door, and leave if the resident escalates; it can be rescheduled. An open gate, a cut lock or a strange vehicle at a substation means back out and call police, not investigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Refuse drivers should stay in the cab when a resident runs out to argue, crack the window and offer the customer service number. Operators on night rounds at lift stations and plants should check in with dispatch at set times, lock gates behind them and keep the radio on their belt, not in the truck.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Flaggers should never step in front of a moving vehicle to stop it, and should radio the plate of anyone who runs the closure. Plow operators confronted over a plowed-in driveway should stay in the cab and keep moving. Forestry crews removing a parkway tree should expect a protest and call the supervisor rather than debate ownership. Facilities staff in public buildings should know the lockdown procedure and where the duress button is.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyone.</strong> Use the buddy system after dark and at flagged addresses. Keep your lone worker check-in times. Report broken lights, gates and locks as safety hazards. Know the radio <strong>duress code</strong> and use it early. When something feels wrong, leave; no shutoff, pickup or pothole is worth getting hurt over.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Workplace violence includes threats, harassment and intimidation, not just physical assault, and it comes in four types: criminal intent, customer or public, worker on worker, and personal relationship.", "Public works crews are exposed because they work in public and on private property, deliver unwelcome news like shutoffs and closures, and often work alone or at night.", "Stay in 'yellow' all shift: know the normal baseline, find two exits, keep the phone away and trust your gut.", "Warning signs such as escalating anger, threats, talk of weapons and fixation on a grievance call for a report to your supervisor, safety or human resources, not a diagnosis, and reporting is protected from retaliation.", "With an angry resident or driver, stay calm, give space, listen, do not argue policy, offer the supervisor's number, never block an exit, and leave if it is not working.", "In an active shooter event, Run if you can, Hide with the door locked, lights off and phone silenced if you cannot, and Fight with full commitment only as a last resort.", "When police arrive, show empty hands with fingers spread, follow commands and do not stop officers for help; then go to the rally point so your supervisor can account for the crew."], "questions": [{"text": "A resident shouts at a water technician that he will 'be waiting with his shotgun' the next time the truck comes down the street, but never leaves his porch. Under OSHA's definition, what is this?", "options": ["Not workplace violence, because there was no physical contact", "Workplace violence, because a threat of physical violence at the work site counts", "A customer service complaint to be logged with billing", "Workplace violence only if the resident has a criminal record"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "OSHA's definition covers any act or threat of physical violence, harassment, intimidation or other threatening behavior at the work site. A spoken threat is workplace violence and must be reported, even with no contact."}, {"text": "A plant operator's former partner, who has no connection to the department, shows up at the treatment plant gate demanding to see her. Which type of workplace violence is this?", "options": ["Type 1, criminal intent", "Type 2, customer or member of the public", "Type 3, worker on worker", "Type 4, personal relationship"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Type 4 is violence brought to work by someone with a personal relationship to the victim, usually a current or former partner. That is why employees are asked to tell a supervisor or human resources about protective orders and threats from an ex-partner."}, {"text": "In the color code of awareness, which level should you be in for your whole shift?", "options": ["Yellow: relaxed and alert, noticing who is around and where the exits are", "White: focused on the task with everything else tuned out", "Orange: focused on a specific person who may be a threat", "Red: ready to leave or defend yourself at any second"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Yellow is the working state: relaxed, alert and aware of the baseline. White is switched off and belongs at home, while orange and red are responses to a specific concern or a confirmed threat."}, {"text": "What do the letters in OODA stand for?", "options": ["Open, organize, direct, alert", "Observe, order, defend, alarm", "Observe, orient, decide, act", "Obey, obstruct, depart, assist"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "OODA is observe, orient, decide, act: take in the scene, work out what it means, choose a response and carry it out, then look again. The habit of looking before acting is the point."}, {"text": "A mechanic tells you his coworker has been talking for weeks about 'getting even' with the shop supervisor over a write-up and recently started bringing up the guns he owns. What should you do?", "options": ["Keep an eye on him yourself and decide whether he is serious", "Report what was said, as specifically as you can, to your supervisor, the safety office or human resources", "Confront the coworker directly and tell him to stop talking like that", "Wait until he makes a direct threat so the report will be taken seriously"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Fixation on a grievance and talk of weapons are warning signs. Your job is to report what you saw and heard, not to diagnose or confront. The threat assessment team decides what it means, and reporting in good faith is protected from retaliation."}, {"text": "You are shutting off water for nonpayment and the homeowner comes out yelling and steps toward you. Which response follows the course?", "options": ["Explain the shutoff policy in detail until he understands why he is wrong", "Stand your ground between him and his front door so he cannot get closer", "Tell him you will call the police if he says one more word", "Keep your voice low, give him space, offer the supervisor's number, and leave if he does not calm down"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "De-escalation means a calm voice, space, listening, not arguing policy and offering someone to call. Never block a person's exit, and if the person is not calming down, walk to the truck and leave. The shutoff can wait."}, {"text": "You hear gunfire in the administration building and cannot reach an exit. What should you do?", "options": ["Get into a room that locks, lock and barricade the door, turn off the lights and silence your phone completely", "Lie down in the hallway and stay still so the shooter thinks you are already hurt", "Set your phone to vibrate so you can still take calls from your supervisor", "Leave the door unlocked so police can get in quickly"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "When running is not possible, hide out of sight in a locked, barricaded room with the lights off and the phone fully silenced, including vibrate. Stay quiet and behind something solid until law enforcement says it is clear."}, {"text": "Police enter the building while you are evacuating. What should you do?", "options": ["Run to the nearest officer and hold on until you are escorted out", "Stop the first officer you see and ask which way to go", "Show empty hands with fingers spread, make no sudden movements, follow commands and keep moving in the direction the officers came from", "Point toward the shooter and shout a description so officers hear you"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Officers' first job is to stop the shooter, and they do not know you from the attacker. Empty hands up, fingers spread, no sudden movements, follow commands and do not stop officers for help or directions."}, {"text": "You have escaped to your truck and are calling 911. Which information does the dispatcher need most?", "options": ["The location including building and floor, the number of shooters, their description, the weapons and how many people are hurt", "Your employee number and your supervisor's name", "The make and model of your truck and where it is parked", "A list of coworkers you believe are still inside, with their phone numbers"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Dispatchers need the location, the number of shooters, what they look like, the kind of weapons and the number of people hurt. Accounting for coworkers happens at the rally point through your supervisor."}, {"text": "Which practice is part of the department's approach to lone and night work at lift stations, substations and remote yards?", "options": ["Turning off the radio at remote sites to avoid distraction", "Leaving gates open so you can leave quickly if threatened", "Working alone only during daylight hours, with no check-ins needed", "Checking in with dispatch at set times, locking gates behind you and keeping the radio on your belt"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Lone worker check-ins at set times, locked gates, lighting and access control, and a radio within reach with a known duress code are the practices that protect people working alone or at night."}]}, {"slug": "bucket-truck-rescue", "title": "Bucket Truck (Aerial Lift) Rescue", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.269 / 29 CFR 1910.67", "standard_title": "Aerial lifts and electric power work (rescue, first aid and CPR, emergency procedures)", "category": "rescue", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "A worker who is shocked, injured or unresponsive in a bucket needs to be brought to the ground and given CPR within minutes, and the truck itself may be energized. This refresher covers the regulatory basis under 29 CFR 1910.269 and 1910.67, scene size up, de-energizing, using the lower controls, backup lowering methods, bucket to bucket rescue and removing the victim. It supports, but does not replace, hands on drills with the department's trucks, written procedures and manufacturer instructions.", "objectives": ["Identify the situations that require a bucket truck rescue and the OSHA paragraphs that require rescue, first aid and CPR training.", "Recognize the hazards of an energized truck, including step and touch potential, and explain when the truck must not be touched.", "Describe how to bring a bucket down with the lower controls and what to do when the lower controls fail.", "Explain how a second bucket truck and a rescue strap and rope are used to reach and move a victim.", "List the rescue sequence in order, from scene size up through CPR and AED use on the ground.", "Describe crew roles and the purpose of regular timed drills on the department's own trucks."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why rescue training is required", "html": "<p>Most overhead work is done from a bucket. That makes it the most likely place for a line worker to be hurt and a place a coworker cannot simply walk over to help. Situations that call for a rescue include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The operator is <strong>shocked</strong>, <strong>injured</strong>, <strong>unresponsive</strong> or has a <strong>medical emergency</strong> such as a heart attack or heat illness.</li>\n<li>The <strong>bucket or boom contacts an energized line</strong> and the truck itself may be energized.</li>\n<li><strong>Boom or hydraulic failure</strong> strands the bucket in the air.</li>\n<li>A <strong>truck fire</strong> starts below the operator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>OSHA ties rescue training to this work. <strong>1910.269(a)(2)</strong> requires training in the emergency procedures that apply to the job. <strong>1910.269(b)</strong> requires persons trained in <strong>first aid and CPR</strong> where employees work on or near exposed lines or equipment. <strong>1910.67</strong> sets the rules for aerial lifts, including <strong>lower controls that can override the upper controls</strong> and a <strong>body belt and lanyard attached to the boom or basket</strong> whenever a worker is in the bucket (1910.67(c)(2)). <strong>1926.453</strong> carries the same aerial lift requirements for construction work.</p>\n<p>This course is a <strong>refresher</strong>. It does not replace hands on drills with the department's own trucks, written procedures and manufacturer instructions, which govern where they differ.</p>"}, {"heading": "Scene size up", "html": "<p>Look before you touch anything. The hazards in a bucket truck rescue are the ones that create a second victim.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Energized lines</strong>. Is the bucket, boom or a conductor still in contact? Is the circuit still energized?</li>\n<li><strong>An energized truck</strong>. If the boom touched a line, the truck body, outriggers and anything bonded to it can be energized. Nothing may look different.</li>\n<li><strong>Step and touch potential</strong>. Current flowing from an energized truck into the earth creates a voltage gradient on the ground around it. Walking toward the truck, or touching it from the ground, can be fatal.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic</strong>. Bucket trucks work in the road. Close the lane or get a flagger.</li>\n<li><strong>Fire or smoke</strong> at the truck, the boom tip or the pole.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather and footing</strong>, especially at night or on ice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Say what you see out loud and assign roles before anyone moves.</p>"}, {"heading": "Calling for help and de-energizing", "html": "<p>One person calls <strong>911</strong> and <strong>dispatch</strong> and asks the <strong>system operator</strong> to de-energize and hold the circuit. Give the location, the number of victims, whether the truck may be energized and whether CPR is in progress.</p>\n<p>When contact is involved, de-energizing the circuit is the most important step. Until it is done:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Do not touch the truck, the outriggers or anything connected to it.</strong> Do not climb on it. Do not grab the lower controls.</li>\n<li>Keep everyone back. If you must move away from the truck, shuffle or hop with your feet together to limit step potential.</li>\n<li>Do not try to pull a conductor off the boom with your hands.</li>\n<li>If the department has a written procedure for approaching an energized vehicle, follow it exactly. If it does not, wait for confirmation that the circuit is dead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the circuit was never involved, such as a medical emergency, go directly to the lower controls.</p>\n<p>Rescuer PPE: <strong>rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class</strong>, <strong>hard hat</strong>, <strong>eye protection</strong> and <strong>fall protection</strong> for anyone entering a bucket. Put your gloves on before you reach for a control.</p>"}, {"heading": "First choice: lower the bucket from the ground", "html": "<p>The fastest and safest rescue is usually the one where nobody climbs. Every articulating or extensible boom lift built to carry people must have <strong>lower controls that can override the upper controls</strong>, and the rule against operating them without the occupant's permission has an exception for emergencies (1910.67(c)(2)). A rescue is exactly what they are for.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start the truck or engage the PTO if needed.</li>\n<li>Operate the lower controls to <strong>bring the bucket down and in</strong> to a safe landing spot, keeping the boom clear of the lines. Watch the boom tip, not the controls.</li>\n<li>Move smoothly. A jerky movement can throw a slumped victim against the edge or out of the bucket.</li>\n<li>Land the bucket where the ground crew can reach the victim, then release their fall protection and lift them out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Only when it is safe.</strong> If the truck or boom may still be energized, do not touch the lower controls or the truck until the line is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure has been followed.</p>"}, {"heading": "When the lower controls fail", "html": "<p>Hydraulic systems fail, engines stall and electrical contact can burn out control circuits. Every operator should know how to bring their own truck's boom down without the main system, following the <strong>manufacturer's procedure</strong> for that model. The common options are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emergency hydraulic pump</strong>, electric or 12 volt, that runs off the truck battery and powers the lower controls at reduced speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand pump</strong> at the pedestal or turret for trucks so equipped.</li>\n<li><strong>Auxiliary power</strong> from a second engine or a backup power unit.</li>\n<li><strong>Controlled bleed-down</strong> on trucks with a manual lowering valve, which lets the boom come down slowly under gravity. It works only on some units and only as the manual allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practice these on the truck you drive. Find out where the hand pump handle is stored during a drill, not with a coworker unresponsive overhead.</p>\n<p>If none of these will bring the bucket down, or the truck cannot be touched, the next option is <strong>a second bucket truck</strong>. Position it where its boom can reach the victim while keeping clearance from the lines. Only a qualified operator in full PPE goes up.</p>"}, {"heading": "Bucket to bucket rescue and removing the victim", "html": "<p>When a second truck is used, the rescuer works from their own bucket.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bring the rescue bucket alongside or slightly below the victim's bucket so the victim can be moved across without a high lift over the rail.</li>\n<li>Attach a <strong>rescue strap and rope</strong> to the victim's harness D ring or body belt before releasing anything. The victim's own fall protection stays connected until the rescue strap is holding them.</li>\n<li>Work the victim across, keeping their head and airway clear. Secure them in the rescue bucket and bring both buckets down.</li>\n<li>If the victim must be lowered by rope instead, rig the rope to a rated point on the boom, never to the bucket liner or a rail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lifting a victim out of a bucket is hard. An unresponsive adult in a harness is dead weight in a tight space.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reposition the victim upright, facing you, with their arms over the rail if possible.</li>\n<li>Keep their fall protection connected <strong>until</strong> the rescue strap or the ground crew has them.</li>\n<li>Lift with the legs and a second rescuer where possible, then bring the victim over the rail to the ground or a tailgate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once the victim is down, <strong>start CPR</strong> if they are not breathing normally and attach the <strong>AED</strong> as soon as it arrives.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rescue sequence", "html": "<p>The department's written procedure and the truck manufacturer's instructions govern. The common order is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Size up the scene.</strong> Energized lines, energized truck, step and touch potential, traffic, fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Call for help.</strong> 911, dispatch, system operator to de-energize. Assign roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Put on PPE.</strong> Rubber gloves and sleeves, hard hat, eye protection, fall protection for anyone entering a bucket.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirm the truck is safe to touch.</strong> If contact occurred, wait for de-energization or follow the energized vehicle approach procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower the bucket with the lower controls</strong>, keeping the boom clear of the lines.</li>\n<li><strong>If the lower controls fail</strong>, use the emergency pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down per the manufacturer.</li>\n<li><strong>If the bucket still cannot be lowered</strong>, bring in a second bucket truck and do a bucket to bucket rescue with a rescue strap and rope.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove the victim</strong>, keeping their fall protection connected until they are secured.</li>\n<li><strong>Start CPR and attach the AED</strong> if the victim is not breathing normally. Continue until EMS takes over.</li>\n<li><strong>Brief EMS</strong> on the voltage, the contact and when it happened.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"heading": "Drills and the department procedure", "html": "<p>Crew roles should be decided before the emergency, not during it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two person crew</strong>: one calls for help and manages traffic, then goes to the lower controls or the second truck. The other prepares to receive the victim and start CPR.</li>\n<li><strong>Three person crew</strong>: add a person who controls traffic, meets EMS and stages the AED while the second runs the controls and the third receives the victim.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drill with the trucks you actually drive. Each model stores its emergency pump handle, bleed-down valve and auxiliary power switch in a different place, and one truck's procedure can damage another.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Run <strong>timed drills</strong> at least annually with a rescue mannequin in the bucket, including one where the lower controls are declared failed.</li>\n<li>Time from the alarm to CPR started. Four minutes is the goal.</li>\n<li>Practice bucket to bucket rescue when two trucks are available.</li>\n<li>After every drill, check that the rescue strap, rope and AED are on the truck and in good condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a refresher on the principles behind bucket truck rescue. <strong>The department's written procedure, the manufacturer's instructions for each truck, and hands on drills govern how the rescue is actually done.</strong> Where they differ from this course, follow them.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["1910.269(a)(2) requires emergency procedure training, 1910.269(b) requires first aid and CPR trained persons, and 1910.67 requires lower controls that override the upper controls and a body belt and lanyard in the bucket.", "If the boom or bucket contacted a line, the whole truck may be energized; do not touch it until the circuit is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure is followed.", "Step and touch potential around an energized truck can be fatal; keep people back and shuffle with feet together if you must move away.", "Call 911, dispatch and the system operator first, and get the circuit de-energized.", "The first choice is to bring the bucket down with the lower controls, smoothly, keeping the boom clear of the lines.", "If the lower controls fail, use the emergency pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down exactly as the manufacturer describes.", "Use a second bucket truck and a rescue strap and rope when the primary unit cannot be lowered, and keep the victim's fall protection connected until they are secured.", "Start CPR and attach the AED as soon as the victim is on the ground, and drill the whole sequence on your own trucks at least annually."], "questions": [{"text": "Which OSHA requirement says an aerial lift must have lower controls capable of overriding the upper controls?", "options": ["1910.67(c)(2)", "1910.269(b)", "1910.269(a)(2)", "1926.451(f)(6)"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1910.67(c)(2) requires lower controls that can override the upper controls and allows them to be used without the occupant's permission in an emergency. 1910.269(b) covers first aid and CPR and 1910.269(a)(2) covers emergency procedure training."}, {"text": "A bucket truck boom has contacted a primary conductor and the operator is slumped in the bucket. The truck looks normal. What should the ground crew do first?", "options": ["Run to the lower controls and bring the bucket down", "Pull the outrigger pads to break the truck's connection to the ground", "Stay back from the truck, call for the circuit to be de-energized, and treat the truck as energized until it is confirmed dead", "Have one person climb the boom to reach the operator"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After a line contact the truck body, outriggers and anything bonded to them may be energized even though nothing looks different. Nobody touches the truck until the circuit is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure is followed."}, {"text": "What is step potential around an energized truck?", "options": ["The height of the step a rescuer must climb to reach the pedestal controls", "The voltage difference between a rescuer's two feet caused by current flowing from the truck into the ground", "The chance that the bucket will drop one step when the hydraulics fail", "The voltage carried on the truck's access steps only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Current flowing from an energized truck into the earth creates a voltage gradient on the ground. A person walking normally has one foot at a higher voltage than the other, and current flows through the body. Shuffling or hopping with feet together limits this."}, {"text": "Which action is the first choice for getting an unresponsive operator out of a bucket when the truck is confirmed safe to touch?", "options": ["Positioning a second bucket truck beside the first", "Lowering the victim by rope from the bucket", "Climbing the boom to reach the operator", "Bringing the bucket down with the lower controls at the truck"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The lower controls are the fastest and safest option because nobody has to leave the ground. The second truck and rope methods are used only when the bucket cannot be lowered."}, {"text": "The engine stalls during a rescue and the lower controls do nothing. What should the rescuer do next?", "options": ["Use the emergency hydraulic pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down according to the manufacturer's procedure for that truck", "Disconnect the hydraulic line at the pedestal so the boom can drop", "Rock the truck by moving the outriggers to shake the boom loose", "Wait for the fire department ladder truck"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Trucks are built with backup lowering methods for exactly this situation, and the manufacturer's procedure describes how each one works on that model. Improvised methods can drop the boom or injure the victim."}, {"text": "When a second bucket truck is used to reach a victim, when should the victim's own fall protection be disconnected from the first boom?", "options": ["As soon as the rescue bucket is within reach, so the victim can be pulled across quickly", "Before the rescue bucket arrives, to save time", "Only after a rescue strap and rope attached to the victim's harness or belt is holding them", "It should never be disconnected; the victim is lowered while still attached to the first boom"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The victim must always be attached to something that will hold them. The rescue strap and rope are attached and holding before the original fall protection is released."}, {"text": "Where should a rope be rigged if a victim must be lowered from a bucket by rope?", "options": ["To the fiberglass bucket liner", "To a rated attachment point on the boom", "To the bucket's top rail", "To the victim's tool bag strap"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The bucket liner and rail are not rated to carry a person's weight on a rope. A rated point on the boom is the only acceptable anchor."}, {"text": "Before reaching for the lower controls during a rescue, what PPE must the rescuer put on?", "options": ["Leather work gloves and a reflective vest", "A face shield only", "Hearing protection and safety toe boots", "Rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class, hard hat and eye protection"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The truck or controls may be energized even after the scene looks safe. Rubber gloves and sleeves rated for the voltage, a hard hat and eye protection are required, and anyone entering a bucket also needs fall protection."}, {"text": "In the rescue sequence, what is the correct order of these three steps?", "options": ["Lower the bucket, then call for help, then confirm the truck is safe to touch", "Confirm the truck is safe to touch, then call for help, then lower the bucket", "Call for help, then confirm the truck is safe to touch, then lower the bucket", "Lower the bucket, then confirm the truck is safe to touch, then call for help"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Help and de-energization are requested right after scene size up. The crew then confirms the truck is safe to touch, and only then operates the lower controls to bring the bucket down."}, {"text": "Why should bucket truck rescue drills be run on the department's own trucks rather than only in a classroom?", "options": ["Because OSHA requires every drill to be filmed from the bucket", "Because each truck model stores its emergency pump, bleed-down valve and auxiliary power in different places and uses a different procedure", "Because the classroom cannot simulate traffic noise", "Because the AED only works when connected to a truck battery"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Backup lowering methods differ from truck to truck, and using the wrong procedure can damage the unit or drop the boom. Timed drills on the actual trucks build the muscle memory the crew will need."}]}, {"slug": "pole-top-rescue", "title": "Pole Top Rescue", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.269", "standard_title": "Electric Power Generation, Transmission, and Distribution (rescue, first aid and CPR, emergency procedures)", "category": "rescue", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "When a line worker is shocked or injured at the top of a pole, the crew on site has minutes to get them down and start CPR. This refresher covers the regulatory basis for rescue training under 29 CFR 1910.269, scene size up, clearing an energized contact, rescuer PPE, rigging and lowering, and crew roles. It supports, but does not replace, hands on timed drills using the department's written procedure and equipment.", "objectives": ["Explain why pole top rescue must be completed within about four minutes and where 1910.269 requires rescue, first aid and CPR training.", "Perform a scene size up that identifies energized conductors, downed lines, fire, traffic and pole condition before climbing.", "Describe how to clear an energized contact and what PPE a rescuer must wear.", "List the steps of a pole top rescue in order, from the call for help through CPR and AED use on the ground.", "Describe how the handline is rigged and how descent is controlled.", "Assign crew roles for two and three person crews and explain the purpose of regular timed drills."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why rescue training is required", "html": "<p>A line worker who is shocked, injured or unresponsive at the top of a pole cannot wait for the fire department. Electrical contact can stop the heart or breathing. <strong>Brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen</strong>, so the industry goal is to have the victim <strong>on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes</strong> of the incident. Only the crew on site can meet that clock.</p>\n<p>OSHA builds rescue into the electric power standard:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1910.269(a)(2)</strong> requires employees to be trained in the emergency procedures that apply to their work, and names <strong>pole top and manhole rescue</strong> as examples.</li>\n<li><strong>1910.269(b)</strong> requires persons trained in <strong>first aid and CPR</strong> where employees work on or near exposed lines or equipment, so a field crew can begin care immediately.</li>\n<li>Trained means practiced. Many utilities and public power systems <strong>drill pole top rescue at least annually</strong>, with a timer running.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a <strong>refresher</strong>. It does not replace hands on, timed drills with the department's written procedure and rescue equipment. Where this course and the department's written procedure differ, <strong>the department's procedure governs</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "Scene size up", "html": "<p>Rushing into a scene with an energized conductor turns one victim into two. Look before you climb.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Energized conductors</strong>. Is the victim still in contact with a line or equipment? Are the conductors still energized? Did anything burn down or fall?</li>\n<li><strong>Downed lines</strong> on the ground, on the truck, on a fence or in a tree. Assume they are energized.</li>\n<li><strong>Fire</strong> on the pole, in a transformer or in nearby brush.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic</strong>. Set cones or get a flagger.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather</strong>. Lightning, wind and ice change how you climb and rig.</li>\n<li><strong>Unstable pole</strong>. A pole that was struck, is burning or has a broken crossarm may not hold a second climber. Check it before you climb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Call out what you see so the whole crew hears it, then assign roles.</p>"}, {"heading": "Calling for help and clearing the contact", "html": "<p>Getting help moving is the first action, not the last. One person calls <strong>911</strong>, then <strong>dispatch</strong>, and asks the <strong>system operator</strong> to de-energize the circuit and hold it. Give the location, the number of victims and whether CPR is in progress. Keep the radio open.</p>\n<p><strong>Never touch a victim who is still in contact with an energized source.</strong> The current that injured them will pass through you. Break the contact first, in one of these ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Have the system operator <strong>de-energize the circuit</strong> and confirm it.</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>hot stick or other insulated tool</strong> rated for the voltage to move the conductor or the victim.</li>\n<li>Open a cutout or switch where that is within your training and the department procedure allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rescuer PPE is not optional because it is an emergency. Wear a <strong>hard hat</strong>, <strong>eye protection</strong>, <strong>rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper voltage class</strong> for the lines involved, and <strong>climbers with a body belt and fall protection</strong> as required by 1910.269(g)(2). If your rubber gloves are in the truck, get them. Thirty seconds is cheaper than a second victim.</p>"}, {"heading": "Reaching and assessing the victim", "html": "<p>Climb with your normal technique and your fall protection attached. Hurrying is how rescuers cut out and fall. Watch for the victim's tools and loose conductors as you go.</p>\n<p><strong>Position yourself above or beside the victim</strong>, depending on how they are hanging and what your department procedure calls for. Above lets you rig over a crossarm or through a rescue block and use your weight to control the line. Beside lets you reach the victim's belt. Either way, keep yourself and the handline clear of energized parts.</p>\n<p>Assess quickly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Responsiveness</strong>. Speak to the victim. Tap the shoulder.</li>\n<li><strong>Breathing</strong>. Look at the chest and listen for a few seconds. Do not spend time looking for a pulse at the top of a pole.</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding or burns</strong> that will matter during lowering. Lowering comes first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the victim is not breathing, your only job is to get them to the ground fast. Rescue breaths on the pole are not practical and delay what will actually save them.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rigging the handline", "html": "<p>The handline carries the victim's weight to the ground. Rig it once, rig it right.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pass the handline <strong>over a crossarm or through a rescue block</strong> above the victim, as close to directly over them as possible so they do not swing into the pole or a conductor.</li>\n<li>Attach the line to the victim using a <strong>rescue sling</strong> or the victim's own body belt D ring or strap, exactly as your department procedure specifies. Check the knot or clip by eye and by hand.</li>\n<li>Take up slack so the line holds most of the victim's weight <strong>before</strong> you release their fall protection. A victim released with slack in the line drops and may pull you off the pole.</li>\n<li>Keep the handline <strong>clear of energized parts</strong>. A rope laid across a hot phase becomes a conductor when it is wet.</li>\n<li>Make sure the line is long enough to reach the ground with rope to spare for the person controlling descent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only after the line is secure and holding do you cut or unbuckle the victim's belt or lanyard. Cut the strap, never the rope.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rescue sequence", "html": "<p>Every department writes its own version, and that written procedure governs. The common order is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Size up the scene.</strong> Energized lines, downed conductors, fire, traffic, pole condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Call for help.</strong> 911, dispatch, system operator to de-energize. Assign roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear the contact.</strong> De-energize or use insulated tools. Do not touch the victim until the source is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Put on PPE.</strong> Hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves, climbers, belt and fall protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Climb to the victim</strong> and position above or beside them, clear of energized parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess.</strong> Check responsiveness and breathing in a few seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Rig the handline</strong> over a crossarm or through a rescue block, attach it to the victim, take up slack.</li>\n<li><strong>Release the victim's fall protection</strong> once the line is holding.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower the victim.</strong> The rescuer controls descent from the pole, or a ground person takes the line, as the department procedure specifies. Lower smoothly and guide the victim away from the pole.</li>\n<li><strong>On the ground, start CPR and use the AED</strong> right away if the victim is not breathing normally. Continue until EMS takes over.</li>\n<li><strong>Descend and support</strong> the ground team. Tell EMS the voltage and the nature of the contact.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The target is step 10 within about four minutes.</p>"}, {"heading": "Crew roles, aerial lifts and the ground", "html": "<p>Rescue works best when nobody has to think about who does what.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two person crew</strong>: one climbs and rigs, one calls for help, manages traffic, handles the rope end if the procedure calls for it, and starts CPR the moment the victim touches down.</li>\n<li><strong>Three person crew</strong>: add a ground person who controls traffic, meets EMS and stages the AED while the second person takes the victim.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a <strong>bucket truck or aerial lift</strong> is on site and can reach the victim safely, it is often faster and easier on the victim than climbing. The same rules apply: clear the contact first, keep boom clearance from energized parts, and wear your rubber goods. A <strong>ladder</strong> can sometimes reach a victim on a short pole, but only when it can be set on firm ground, footed and clear of conductors. Do not waste time positioning a truck that cannot get to the pole. Climb.</p>\n<p>Once the victim is on the ground, <strong>start CPR</strong> if they are not breathing normally and attach the <strong>AED</strong> as soon as it arrives. Treat burns and bleeding after circulation is handled. Electrical contact victims can go into cardiac arrest minutes after they appear fine, so keep the AED on and someone watching until EMS takes over.</p>"}, {"heading": "Drills and the department procedure", "html": "<p>Reading about rescue does not make anyone ready to do it. The skills that matter under stress are the ones your hands know: finding the rescue block, tying the sling, controlling a loaded handline, cutting a strap one handed with gloves on.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drill <strong>at least annually</strong> with a <strong>rescue mannequin</strong> of realistic weight, in gloves and sleeves.</li>\n<li><strong>Time every drill</strong> from the alarm to the mannequin on the ground, and record it. Four minutes is the goal. Talk through what slowed you down.</li>\n<li>Rotate roles so every crew member has rigged and lowered.</li>\n<li>Drill the variations: victim hanging head down, victim on the far side of the crossarm, two person crew, night, rain.</li>\n<li>Inspect rescue equipment with the other climbing gear and keep it in the same place on every truck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a refresher on the principles behind pole top rescue. <strong>The department's written procedure, the equipment on the truck, and hands on timed drills govern how you actually do it.</strong> If anything here differs from that procedure, follow the procedure and tell your supervisor.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["The goal is the victim on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes, because brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen.", "1910.269(a)(2) requires training in emergency procedures such as pole top rescue, and 1910.269(b) requires first aid and CPR trained persons on the crew.", "Size up the scene first: energized conductors, downed lines, fire, traffic, weather and the condition of the pole.", "Never touch a victim still in contact with an energized source; de-energize or clear the contact with insulated tools first.", "Wear hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves of the right class, climbers and fall protection before you climb.", "Rig the handline over a crossarm or through a rescue block, take up slack, and only then release the victim's fall protection.", "Start CPR and attach the AED as soon as the victim is on the ground, and keep the AED on until EMS takes over.", "The department's written procedure and timed drills with a rescue mannequin govern; this course is only a refresher."], "questions": [{"text": "Why does the electric utility industry set a goal of having a pole top victim on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes?", "options": ["Because the system operator can only hold a circuit out for four minutes", "Because brain injury begins within minutes when a person is not breathing or has no pulse", "Because most rescue handlines are only rated for four minutes of load", "Because EMS response time in most areas is four minutes"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen, so the crew on site must get the victim down and start CPR fast. EMS and the system operator cannot act quickly enough to meet that clock."}, {"text": "Which paragraph of 1910.269 specifically names pole top and manhole rescue as examples of emergency procedures employees must be trained in?", "options": ["1910.269(a)(2)", "1910.269(b)", "1910.269(g)(2)", "1910.269(l)"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1910.269(a)(2) requires training in the emergency procedures that apply to an employee's work and gives pole top and manhole rescue as examples. Paragraph (b) covers first aid and CPR trained persons."}, {"text": "You arrive at a pole where a coworker is slumped in his belt and a primary conductor is lying across his shoulder. What should you do first?", "options": ["Climb immediately and pull the conductor off with your gloved hand", "Throw the handline over the crossarm so you can lower him as soon as you reach him", "Call for the circuit to be de-energized and clear the contact with an insulated tool before touching him", "Begin rescue breathing from the pole as soon as you reach him"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Never touch a victim who is still in contact with an energized source. The contact must be cleared by de-energizing the circuit or by using a hot stick or other properly rated insulated tool, or the rescuer becomes the second victim."}, {"text": "Which of these is the correct PPE for a rescuer climbing to a victim on a distribution pole?", "options": ["Hard hat and leather gloves, because speed matters more than rubber goods during a rescue", "Rubber gloves only, since sleeves slow down climbing", "Climbers and body belt without a fall protection lanyard, to save time", "Hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class, climbers, belt and fall protection"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An emergency does not remove the hazards. The rescuer needs rubber gloves and sleeves rated for the voltage, a hard hat, eye protection, and climbers with a belt and fall protection as required by 1910.269(g)(2)."}, {"text": "When should the rescuer release the victim's fall protection from the pole?", "options": ["Before rigging the handline, so the victim can be repositioned", "Only after the handline is attached and holding most of the victim's weight", "As soon as the rescuer reaches the victim, to speed up the descent", "After the victim has been lowered a few feet to test the line"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The handline must be attached and the slack taken up so it is holding the victim before the victim's belt or lanyard is released. Releasing a victim with slack in the line causes a drop that can pull the rescuer off the pole."}, {"text": "Where should the handline be passed before it is attached to the victim?", "options": ["Over a crossarm or through a rescue block as close to directly above the victim as possible", "Around the victim's chest and then down to the ground person", "Over the nearest conductor, since it provides the highest anchor point", "Through the rescuer's own D ring so the rescuer can hold the victim's weight directly"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Rigging over a crossarm or through a rescue block above the victim lets the line carry the weight and keeps the victim from swinging into the pole or conductors. The line must stay clear of energized parts."}, {"text": "In a two person crew, what is the ground person's most important job once the victim starts coming down?", "options": ["Climbing the pole to help guide the victim past the crossarm", "Photographing the scene for the incident report", "Driving the truck closer so the bucket can be used", "Being ready to start CPR and attach the AED the moment the victim reaches the ground"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The point of the four minute goal is to start CPR as fast as possible. The ground person stages the AED, handles the rope if the procedure calls for it, and begins CPR immediately on touchdown."}, {"text": "A victim of electrical contact is lowered to the ground, is breathing, and says he feels fine. What should the crew do?", "options": ["Let him return to work once he can stand without help", "Have him sit in the truck and drive him home", "Keep the AED attached and someone watching him until EMS takes over, because cardiac arrest can occur minutes later", "Cancel the ambulance so EMS is available for other calls"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Electrical contact victims can go into cardiac arrest minutes after they appear fine. Keep the AED on, keep monitoring, and let EMS evaluate and transport."}, {"text": "When may a bucket truck be used instead of climbing to reach a pole top victim?", "options": ["Only when the victim is unresponsive", "When the truck is already on site and can reach the victim safely while keeping clearance from energized parts", "Whenever a truck is within ten minutes of the scene", "Never, because pole top rescue must be done by a climber"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An aerial lift that is already on site and can safely reach the victim is often faster and easier on the victim. If a truck cannot be positioned quickly, the rescuer should climb rather than lose time."}, {"text": "What is the purpose of running timed pole top rescue drills with a rescue mannequin at least annually?", "options": ["To satisfy the warranty terms for climbing equipment", "To replace the need for CPR and first aid certification", "To build the hands on skills and crew coordination needed to meet the four minute goal under stress", "To allow the department to skip the written rescue procedure"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Rescue skills are physical and fade without practice. Timed drills with a realistic weight in gloves and sleeves show whether the crew can meet the four minute goal and reveal what slows them down."}]}, {"slug": "incident-reporting-investigation", "title": "Incident Reporting and Investigation for Supervisors and Managers", "standard": "29 CFR 1904", "standard_title": "Recording and Reporting Occupational Injuries and Illnesses, with incident investigation practice", "category": "supervisor", "top10_rank": null, "new_hire": false, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Supervisors and managers are the first link in incident reporting: they care for the injured, secure the scene, decide what must go to OSHA and when, and lead the investigation that keeps it from happening again. This course covers the reporting and recordkeeping rules in 29 CFR 1904, the recordability test, vehicle incident requirements including CDL post-accident testing, and the eight-section incident report used on this platform. It ends with how to find root causes and assign corrective actions that actually close.", "objectives": ["Explain the purpose of incident investigation and the reporting duties of employees and supervisors, including the no-retaliation rules in 1904.36 and Section 11(c).", "Report a work-related fatality to OSHA within 8 hours and an inpatient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye within 24 hours, by phone, online or through the area office.", "Maintain the OSHA 300 log, 301 incident report and 300A annual summary within the required entry, posting and retention periods.", "Decide whether an injury or illness is recordable by applying the criteria in 1904.7 and the first aid list in 1904.7(b)(5).", "Lead an investigation through the first hour, witness interviews and the eight-section incident report, identifying direct, contributing and root causes.", "Assign corrective actions using the hierarchy of controls, with a responsible party, target date and verification date, and share lessons learned without naming individuals."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why investigate at all", "html": "<p>An incident is the system telling you something. A broken wrist, a backed-over sign post or a near miss in a trench proves a hazard exists and the controls did not hold. The purpose of investigating is to <strong>find out why and fix it so it does not happen again</strong>, not to find someone to blame. Investigations that hunt for a culprit produce less reporting, not fewer injuries, because people stop telling you about the close calls that are your best warning.</p>\n<p>There are also duties you cannot skip. <strong>Part 1904</strong> sets deadlines for reporting serious events to OSHA and for recording injuries, and missing them is a citation on its own. The <strong>workers' compensation</strong> insurer or risk pool needs a prompt, accurate report to handle a claim fairly.</p>\n<p>The department follows a <strong>just culture</strong> approach: honest mistakes are coached, risky shortcuts are corrected along with the reasons behind them, and only reckless behavior goes to discipline. Say that out loud before you ask your first question.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this training matters", "html": "<p>The supervisor is the first link in the chain. You get the call, decide whether the scene is safe, and start the clock on every deadline in this course. Get the first hour wrong and evidence disappears, witnesses compare stories, an OSHA deadline passes while everyone assumes someone else called, and the crew learns that reporting is a hassle.</p>\n<p>Make sure everyone you supervise knows the rule: <strong>report every injury, illness, near miss, property damage event and vehicle incident, immediately</strong>, to you or whoever is acting for you. Immediately means before the task is finished, not at the end of the shift.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>1904.35</strong> the department must have a reasonable reporting procedure that does not discourage anyone from using it. Under <strong>1904.36</strong> and <strong>Section 11(c) of the OSH Act</strong>, no employee may be punished for reporting an injury, filing a safety complaint or asking to see the records. That includes a worse route, a lost overtime slot or a remark at the tailboard. Protect the reporter and you protect the program.</p>"}, {"heading": "Reporting to OSHA and keeping the records", "html": "<p>Reporting to OSHA and recording are two different duties.</p>\n<p><strong>Reporting to OSHA (1904.39).</strong> The department must report a <strong>work-related fatality within 8 hours</strong> of learning of it, and an <strong>inpatient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye within 24 hours</strong>. Inpatient hospitalization means formal admission for care, not an emergency room visit or observation. Report by <strong>phone to the nearest OSHA area office</strong>, by phone to <strong>OSHA's 24-hour line, 1-800-321-6742</strong>, or <strong>online at osha.gov</strong>. The safety office normally makes the call, but if you cannot reach them, you make it. Most city employees are covered through an OSHA-approved state plan that adopts these rules; know which agency covers your department.</p>\n<p><strong>Recording (1904.29 through 1904.35).</strong> The department keeps three forms or their equivalents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>300 log</strong>, a running list of recordable cases. Enter each case <strong>within 7 calendar days</strong> of learning about it.</li>\n<li>The <strong>301 incident report</strong>, one per case. The platform's incident report serves as the equivalent.</li>\n<li>The <strong>300A annual summary</strong>, the year's totals, certified by a senior official and <strong>posted from February 1 through April 30</strong> of the next year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep the records for <strong>five years</strong> after the year they cover. Employees, former employees and their representatives may see the 300 log, and their own 301, by the end of the next business day.</p>"}, {"heading": "Is it recordable?", "html": "<p>A case is <strong>work-related</strong> if an event or exposure at work caused it, contributed to it or made an existing condition significantly worse. A work-related case is <strong>recordable</strong> under 1904.7 if it results in any of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Death.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Days away from work</strong>, counted in calendar days after the day of injury, capped at 180.</li>\n<li><strong>Restricted work or job transfer</strong>, meaning the employee cannot do a routine job function or a full shift.</li>\n<li><strong>Medical treatment beyond first aid.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loss of consciousness.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A significant injury or illness diagnosed by a physician</strong> or other licensed health care professional, such as a fractured or cracked bone or tooth or a punctured eardrum, even with no treatment or lost time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>First aid is not a judgment call. Section 1904.7(b)(5) lists everything that counts, and anything not on the list is medical treatment. In plain terms: over-the-counter medicine at over-the-counter strength; tetanus shots; cleaning or soaking a wound; bandages, butterfly bandages and Steri-Strips; hot or cold packs; elastic wraps and other non-rigid supports; splints, slings and collars used only during transport; draining a blister or a nail; eye patches; flushing or swabbing something out of the eye; pulling a splinter; finger guards; massage; and fluids for heat stress. Stitches, staples, prescription medicine, rigid splints and physical therapy are medical treatment. A doctor visit for observation or an x-ray alone is not.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy cases.</strong> For an injury to an intimate body part or the reproductive system, a sexual assault, a mental illness, HIV, hepatitis, tuberculosis, a needlestick with another person's blood, or another illness the employee asks to keep private, write 'privacy case' on the 300 log in place of the name and keep the name on a separate confidential list.</p>"}, {"heading": "Vehicle incidents", "html": "<p>Any crash, backing strike or rollover involving a department vehicle also gets the vehicle section of the report.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Call the police</strong> and get the report number, even for minor damage. Describe what happened; do not argue fault.</li>\n<li><strong>Photograph</strong> before anything moves: every vehicle from all four corners, damage close up, skid marks, signs and signals, weather and lighting.</li>\n<li><strong>Get witness names and phone numbers</strong> before they drive away.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-accident testing for CDL drivers.</strong> Under <strong>49 CFR 382.303</strong>, a driver operating a commercial motor vehicle must be tested after a crash with a fatality, and after a crash with an injury treated away from the scene or a vehicle towed away if the driver is cited. The <strong>alcohol test must be done within 2 hours</strong> of the crash; if not, document why and stop trying at 8 hours. The <strong>drug test must be done within 32 hours</strong>. The driver must stay available for testing, which never means delaying medical care.</li>\n<li><strong>Preventability review.</strong> A crash is preventable if the driver did not do everything reasonably possible to avoid it, whoever was ticketed. The fleet safety committee decides, using the ANSI/ASSP Z15.1 approach, and the answer drives corrective action, not discipline.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "The investigation, step by step", "html": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Care for the injured.</strong> First aid, then 911 if there is any doubt.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure the scene.</strong> Stop work, cone or tape the area and lock out any equipment involved. In a fatality or hospitalization case, move nothing except to rescue someone or prevent further harm; OSHA may want to see the scene as it was.</li>\n<li><strong>Preserve evidence.</strong> Photographs from several angles, measurements, the tools and PPE in use, weather and lighting notes, and telematics and camera footage before it overwrites.</li>\n<li><strong>Notify.</strong> Your chain of command and the safety office right away so the OSHA clock is not missed, and the police for any vehicle incident.</li>\n<li><strong>Start the report</strong> on the platform before the end of the shift.</li>\n<li><strong>Interview promptly and separately</strong>, before memories fade and blend. Talk to the injured employee first if they are able, then each witness alone, at or near the scene. Ask <strong>open questions</strong>: 'Walk me through what you were doing.' Ask <strong>where and how it happened</strong>, not whose fault it was. Do not lead, interrupt or correct. Finish with 'What would have prevented this?' Write the statement <strong>in the witness's own words</strong>, read it back, and have them <strong>sign and date</strong> it.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete the eight-section report.</strong> The sections are (1) incident information, (2) vehicle and equipment, (3) personal injury, with a body diagram, (4) description, (5) principal statement, (6) eyewitness statements, (7) facts and analysis, with direct cause, contributing causes and root cause, and (8) signatures. The <strong>investigator</strong>, normally the supervisor, completes sections 1 through 4 and drafts 7. The <strong>principal party</strong>, the injured or involved employee, writes section 5 in their own words. <strong>Witnesses</strong> complete section 6. The <strong>safety representative</strong> reviews section 7 and the corrective actions. <strong>Management signatures</strong> in section 8 come last: supervisor, division manager, director.</li>\n<li><strong>Analyze, correct and close.</strong> The next section shows how.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"heading": "Finding causes and fixing them", "html": "<p>Every incident has layers. The <strong>direct cause</strong> is the event that did the harm: the fall, the strike, the contact. <strong>Contributing causes</strong> are the conditions and actions that made it likely; sort them as <strong>behavioral</strong> (what people did or did not do), <strong>engineered</strong> (equipment, vehicle and facility design) or <strong>environmental</strong> (weather, lighting, footing, traffic). The <strong>root cause</strong> is the system failure underneath.</p>\n<p>A worked example. A street crew laborer fractures an ankle stepping down from a dump truck tailgate onto a loose chunk of asphalt. <strong>Direct cause:</strong> a fall from the tailgate. <strong>Contributing causes:</strong> no step or grab handle on the truck (engineered); the crew was rushing to beat rain and he had a shovel in both hands (behavioral); debris piled where crews step down (environmental). Now the <strong>five whys</strong>. Why the fracture? He landed on loose material. Why was he jumping down? There is no step. Why no step? The trucks were ordered without one. Why did nobody raise it? Getting on and off trucks was never treated as a hazard. Why not? There is no job hazard analysis for mounting and dismounting, so no equipment or rule exists. That is the <strong>root cause</strong>.</p>\n<p>Corrective actions follow the <strong>hierarchy of controls</strong>, top down: <strong>elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, PPE</strong>. Here, retrofitting steps and grab handles (engineering) beats a three-points-of-contact rule (administrative), which beats ankle-supporting boots (PPE). Every action gets a <strong>responsible party</strong>, a <strong>target date</strong> and a <strong>verification date</strong> when someone confirms it was done and works. List the <strong>supporting references</strong>, the policies, procedures and regulations that applied. Then close the loop: tell the injured employee and the crew what changed.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means on the job", "html": "<p><strong>Share lessons learned.</strong> Every closed investigation becomes a tailboard topic. Describe what happened, why, and what changed, and <strong>never name the people involved</strong>. Your job is to show that the department fixes systems instead of punishing people. Near misses get the same treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> An arc flash with no injury is a near miss and gets a full investigation. Preserve the rubber goods, hot sticks and switching order. A crew member admitted to the hospital after a contact is a 24-hour OSHA report.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> A gas exposure that sends an operator to the emergency room is recordable if treatment goes beyond first aid and reportable if the operator is admitted. Save the gas monitor log before it clears.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> The 2-hour alcohol testing window after a plow or refuse truck crash starts at the moment of the crash, not when you hear about it. A worker struck by mower discharge or a falling limb needs a careful body diagram.</p>\n<p>The investigation is finished when the corrective action is verified, not when the form is signed.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Investigate to prevent the next incident, not to assign blame; a just culture coaches honest mistakes and reserves discipline for reckless behavior.", "Employees must report every injury, illness, near miss, property damage event and vehicle incident immediately, and 1904.36 and Section 11(c) forbid any retaliation for reporting.", "Report a work-related fatality to OSHA within 8 hours and an inpatient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye within 24 hours, by phone to the area office or the 24-hour line, or online.", "Enter each recordable case on the 300 log within 7 calendar days, post the 300A from February 1 through April 30, and keep the records for five years.", "A case is recordable if it involves death, days away, restricted work or transfer, medical treatment beyond the 1904.7(b)(5) first aid list, loss of consciousness, or a significant diagnosed injury or illness.", "After a CDL driver's qualifying crash, the alcohol test is due within 2 hours and the drug test within 32 hours under 49 CFR 382.303.", "Interview witnesses promptly and separately with open questions, record statements in their own words, and have them signed and dated.", "Work from direct cause to contributing causes to root cause, pick corrective actions from the top of the hierarchy of controls, assign an owner with target and verification dates, and share the lesson at a tailboard without names."], "questions": [{"text": "What is the main purpose of an incident investigation?", "options": ["To document who was at fault so discipline can be applied consistently", "To satisfy the insurer that the department was not negligent", "To find out why the incident happened and fix it so it does not happen again", "To decide whether the injured employee's account is credible"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Investigations exist to prevent recurrence. Blame-focused investigations drive reporting underground, and the just culture approach reserves discipline for reckless behavior, not honest mistakes."}, {"text": "Which incidents must employees report to their supervisor, and when?", "options": ["Every injury, illness, near miss, property damage event and vehicle incident, immediately", "Injuries that need a doctor, by the end of the shift", "Injuries and vehicle crashes only, within 24 hours", "Anything the employee believes is recordable, within 7 days"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The rule is everything, right away, including near misses and property damage. Deciding what is recordable is the supervisor's and the safety office's job, not the employee's, and early reports keep small cases from becoming serious ones."}, {"text": "A line worker is admitted to the hospital overnight after a fall from a pole at 2:00 p.m. on Tuesday. By when must the department report this to OSHA?", "options": ["Within 8 hours, by 10:00 p.m. Tuesday", "Within 24 hours of learning of the admission", "Within 7 calendar days, when the case is entered on the 300 log", "No report is needed unless the worker misses more than three days"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Under 1904.39, an inpatient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye must be reported within 24 hours. The 8-hour deadline applies to a fatality. The 7-day deadline is for entering the case on the 300 log, which is separate from reporting."}, {"text": "Which statement about OSHA recordkeeping is correct?", "options": ["The 300A annual summary is posted from January 1 through March 31", "Records are kept for three years after the year they cover", "Only the safety office may see the 300 log", "A recordable case must be entered on the 300 log within 7 calendar days of learning about it"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1904.29 gives 7 calendar days to enter a case. The 300A is posted February 1 through April 30, records are kept for five years, and employees and their representatives have a right to see the log."}, {"text": "Which of these injuries is recordable?", "options": ["A cut on the forearm closed with Steri-Strips", "A splinter removed with tweezers", "A cut on the forearm closed with two sutures", "A sore shoulder treated with an ice pack and an over-the-counter pain reliever"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Sutures are medical treatment, so the case is recordable. Steri-Strips, splinter removal by simple means, cold packs and nonprescription medicine are all on the 1904.7(b)(5) first aid list."}, {"text": "An employee reports a work-related injury to the reproductive system. How is it entered on the 300 log?", "options": ["As 'privacy case' in place of the name, with the name kept on a separate confidential list", "With the employee's name, like any other case", "It is left off the log because of its private nature", "With the employee's initials only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Injuries to intimate body parts or the reproductive system are privacy concern cases under 1904.29(b)(7). The case is still recorded, but 'privacy case' replaces the name on the log and the name goes on a separate confidential list."}, {"text": "A refuse truck driver with a CDL is cited after a crash in which the other vehicle had to be towed. Under 49 CFR 382.303, when must the alcohol test be done?", "options": ["Within 32 hours of the crash", "Within 2 hours of the crash, with the reasons documented if it is not, and attempts stopped at 8 hours", "Before the end of the driver's shift", "Only if the police officer requests it"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Post-accident alcohol testing is due within 2 hours; if it cannot be done, the employer documents why and stops attempting after 8 hours. The drug test is due within 32 hours. A tow-away crash with a citation triggers both tests."}, {"text": "Which approach to witness interviews follows the course?", "options": ["Interview all witnesses together so they can fill in each other's gaps", "Ask yes or no questions to keep the interview short and focused", "Write a summary of what the witness probably meant and file it", "Interview each witness separately and promptly, ask open questions about where and how it happened, write the statement in their own words, and have them sign and date it"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Separate, prompt interviews with open questions keep memories from blending. Statements belong in the witness's own words, signed and dated, and the questions focus on where and how, not whose fault."}, {"text": "In the course example, a laborer fractures an ankle jumping down from a dump truck tailgate because the truck has no step. What is the root cause?", "options": ["The laborer landed on a loose chunk of asphalt", "Getting on and off trucks was never analyzed as a hazard, so no equipment or rule exists", "The crew was rushing to finish before the rain", "The laborer was wearing low-cut boots"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The loose asphalt is part of the direct cause and rushing is a behavioral contributing cause. The root cause is the system gap underneath: no job hazard analysis for mounting and dismounting, which is why there is no step and no rule."}, {"text": "After that investigation, which corrective action sits highest on the hierarchy of controls?", "options": ["Issue ankle-supporting boots to the crew", "Add a three-points-of-contact rule to the tailboard", "Retrofit the trucks with steps and grab handles", "Post a reminder sign in the shop"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Steps and grab handles are an engineering control, which ranks above administrative controls like rules and signs and above PPE like boots. Elimination and substitution are higher still but are not practical for climbing on and off a truck."}]}], "top10": [{"fiscal_year": 2026, "status": "preliminary", "period": "Oct. 1, 2025 to Aug. 31, 2026", "source": "https://www.osha.gov/top10citedstandards", "standards": [{"rank": 1, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "citations": 4041, "course_slug": "fall-protection-general"}, {"rank": 2, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "citations": 1961, "course_slug": "hazard-communication"}, {"rank": 3, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "citations": 1863, "course_slug": "lockout-tagout"}, {"rank": 4, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "title": "Scaffolding (construction)", "citations": 1725, "course_slug": "scaffolding"}, {"rank": 5, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "title": "Ladders (construction)", "citations": 1659, "course_slug": "ladders"}, {"rank": 6, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "citations": 1608, "course_slug": "respiratory-protection"}, {"rank": 7, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "citations": 1379, "course_slug": "powered-industrial-trucks"}, {"rank": 8, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "citations": 1273, "course_slug": "fall-protection-training"}, {"rank": 9, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "title": "Eye and Face Protection (construction)", "citations": 1120, "course_slug": "eye-face-protection"}, {"rank": 10, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "title": "Machine Guarding (general industry)", "citations": 1072, "course_slug": "machine-guarding"}]}, {"fiscal_year": 2025, "status": "final", "period": "Oct. 1, 2024 to Sept. 30, 2025", "source": "https://www.safetyandhealthmagazine.com (OSHA final FY 2025 data)", "standards": [{"rank": 1, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "citations": 6992, "course_slug": "fall-protection-general"}, {"rank": 2, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "citations": 3010, "course_slug": "hazard-communication"}, {"rank": 3, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "title": "Ladders (construction)", "citations": 2842, "course_slug": "ladders"}, {"rank": 4, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "citations": 2562, "course_slug": "lockout-tagout"}, {"rank": 5, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "citations": 2294, "course_slug": "respiratory-protection"}, {"rank": 6, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "title": "Scaffolding (construction)", "citations": 2286, "course_slug": "scaffolding"}, {"rank": 7, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "citations": 2216, "course_slug": "fall-protection-training"}, {"rank": 8, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "citations": 2150, "course_slug": "powered-industrial-trucks"}, {"rank": 9, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "title": "Eye and Face Protection (construction)", "citations": 1965, "course_slug": "eye-face-protection"}, {"rank": 10, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "title": "Machine Guarding (general industry)", "citations": 1498, "course_slug": "machine-guarding"}]}], "inspections": [{"slug": "construction-site-inspection", "title": "Construction Site Safety Inspection", "kind": "inspection", "industry": "construction", "frequency": "Weekly or at each site visit, and after any change in site conditions", "intro": "Use this checklist on public works construction and maintenance jobs such as water main replacement, trenching and utility repairs, road and bridge work, pole setting and facility construction. Walk the site with the crew leader, check each item against what you see, and record anything that needs correction along with who will fix it and by when. Skip sections that do not apply to the work in progress.", "sections": [{"heading": "General site and housekeeping", "items": [{"text": "Work areas, passageways and stairs are clear of debris, form lumber with protruding nails, cords and other tripping hazards.", "ref": "1926.25(a)"}, {"text": "Combustible scrap and debris are removed at regular intervals, and containers are provided for waste, oily rags and flammable waste.", "ref": "1926.25(b), 1926.25(c)"}, {"text": "Materials are stacked, racked, blocked or interlocked so they cannot slide, fall or collapse, and are kept clear of openings and hoistways.", "ref": "1926.250(a)(1), 1926.250(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Work areas, accessways and stairways have adequate natural or artificial lighting for the work being done.", "ref": "1926.56(a)"}, {"text": "Potable drinking water is available with single-use cups or a fountain, and any nonpotable water outlet is clearly marked.", "ref": "1926.51(a)"}, {"text": "Toilet facilities are provided in the required number for the crew size and are kept clean and serviceable.", "ref": "1926.51(c)"}, {"text": "Hand washing facilities are available where workers handle sewage, contaminated soil, concrete or chemicals.", "ref": "1926.51(f)"}, {"text": "A competent person is designated for the site and makes frequent and regular inspections that are documented.", "ref": "1926.20(b)(2)"}, {"text": "Workers have been instructed to recognize and avoid the unsafe conditions tied to their assigned tasks, and the daily tailboard or job hazard analysis is signed.", "ref": "1926.21(b)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Traffic control and work zones", "items": [{"text": "A temporary traffic control plan consistent with MUTCD Part 6 is on site and the field setup matches it.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "Advance warning signs, tapers, channelizing devices and buffer spaces are placed for the posted speed and type of closure.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "Flaggers are trained, use STOP and SLOW paddles rather than flags, and stand where drivers can see them with an escape route.", "ref": "1926.201(a), MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "Flaggers wear high-visibility garments meeting ANSI/ISEA 107 Class 2 or Class 3, with Class 3 or retroreflective trousers at night.", "ref": "1926.201(a), MUTCD Part 6, ANSI/ISEA 107"}, {"text": "Every worker on foot who is exposed to traffic or equipment wears high-visibility apparel.", "ref": "1926.651(d), 23 CFR 634"}, {"text": "Night work zones are lit without glare to drivers, and each flagger station is illuminated.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "Signs, cones, drums and barricades are clean, retroreflective, upright and in good condition, and are removed or covered when the hazard is gone.", "ref": "1926.200, 1926.202, MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "Pedestrians have a protected, accessible route around the work zone, and sidewalk closures are signed in advance.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6, ADA Standards"}, {"text": "Shadow vehicles, truck-mounted attenuators or positive barriers are positioned to shield workers from approaching traffic where the plan calls for them.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6"}]}, {"heading": "Fall protection", "items": [{"text": "Workers on surfaces with unprotected sides or edges 6 feet or more above a lower level are protected by guardrails, safety nets or personal fall arrest systems.", "ref": "1926.501(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Guardrail toprails are 42 inches high, plus or minus 3 inches, with midrails, and withstand 200 pounds of force.", "ref": "1926.502(b)"}, {"text": "Holes, including manholes, vault openings and skylights, are covered with secured covers marked HOLE or COVER, or are guarded by guardrails.", "ref": "1926.501(b)(4), 1926.502(i)"}, {"text": "Personal fall arrest systems use full body harnesses, anchorages rated 5,000 pounds per worker or engineered with a 2 to 1 safety factor, and 6 foot maximum free fall.", "ref": "1926.502(d)"}, {"text": "Harnesses, lanyards and lifelines are inspected before each use and removed from service when damaged or after arresting a fall.", "ref": "1926.502(d)(21), 1926.502(d)(19)"}, {"text": "Workers in bucket trucks and boom lifts wear a harness with the lanyard attached to the boom or basket, not to an adjacent structure.", "ref": "1926.453(b)(2)(v)"}, {"text": "Wall openings with a bottom edge less than 39 inches above the walking surface and a drop of 6 feet or more are guarded.", "ref": "1926.501(b)(14)"}, {"text": "Edges of excavations 6 feet or deeper that are hidden by vegetation or obstructions are guarded, and walkways over excavations have guardrails.", "ref": "1926.501(b)(7), 1926.651(l)"}, {"text": "A plan exists to rescue a suspended worker promptly, and workers have been trained by a competent person on the fall hazards and systems in use.", "ref": "1926.502(d)(20), 1926.503"}]}, {"heading": "Ladders and stairways", "items": [{"text": "Ladders are inspected by a competent person for visible defects, and damaged ladders are tagged Do Not Use and removed from service.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(15), 1926.1053(b)(16)"}, {"text": "Portable ladders used for access extend at least 3 feet above the upper landing or are secured at the top with a grab rail.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Extension and straight ladders are set at a 4 to 1 pitch, with the base one quarter of the working length from the wall.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(5)(i)"}, {"text": "Ladders rest on stable, level surfaces and are secured or held when they could be displaced; they are not set on boxes, barrels or equipment.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(6), 1926.1053(b)(7)"}, {"text": "The top and top step of stepladders are not used as steps, and ladders are not loaded beyond their rated capacity.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(13), 1926.1053(b)(3)"}, {"text": "Workers face the ladder, keep at least one hand on it while climbing, and do not carry loads that could cause loss of balance.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(21), 1926.1053(b)(22)"}, {"text": "Metal ladders are not used near energized lines or equipment, and ladders with conductive side rails are marked.", "ref": "1926.1053(b)(12)"}, {"text": "Stairways with 4 or more risers or rising more than 30 inches have stairrails on each unprotected side and a handrail.", "ref": "1926.1052(c)(1)"}]}, {"heading": "Scaffolds", "items": [{"text": "Scaffolds are erected, moved, dismantled or altered only under a competent person, who inspects them before each shift and after any event that could affect integrity.", "ref": "1926.451(f)(3), 1926.451(f)(7)"}, {"text": "Each scaffold and component supports at least 4 times the maximum intended load without failure.", "ref": "1926.451(a)(1)"}, {"text": "Platforms are fully planked or decked between the front uprights and the guardrail supports, with gaps no wider than 1 inch.", "ref": "1926.451(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Legs, posts and frames bear on base plates and mud sills or another firm foundation, not on blocks, barrels or unstable objects.", "ref": "1926.451(c)(2)"}, {"text": "Scaffolds with a height more than 4 times the minimum base width are restrained from tipping by guying, tying or bracing.", "ref": "1926.451(c)(1)"}, {"text": "Guardrails with toprails 38 to 45 inches high and midrails protect all open sides of platforms more than 10 feet up, or personal fall arrest is used.", "ref": "1926.451(g)(1), 1926.451(g)(4)"}, {"text": "Ladders, stair towers, ramps or built-in frame ladders give access to platforms more than 2 feet above or below the access point; cross braces are not climbed.", "ref": "1926.451(e)(1)"}, {"text": "Scaffolds are kept at least 10 feet from energized lines up to 50 kV, plus 0.4 inch for each kV above 50 kV.", "ref": "1926.451(f)(6)"}, {"text": "Toeboards, screens or canopies protect workers below from falling objects, and casters on mobile scaffolds are locked while in use.", "ref": "1926.451(h), 1926.452(w)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Excavations and trenching", "items": [{"text": "Underground utilities have been located and marked with a current one-call (811) ticket, and are exposed by safe means as the excavation approaches them.", "ref": "1926.651(b)"}, {"text": "A competent person inspects the excavation, adjacent areas and protective systems daily before entry, after rain or thaw and as conditions change, and the inspections are recorded.", "ref": "1926.651(k)(1)"}, {"text": "Excavations 5 feet or deeper are protected by sloping, benching, shoring or a trench shield unless made entirely in stable rock.", "ref": "1926.652(a)(1)"}, {"text": "Protective systems for excavations deeper than 20 feet are designed by a registered professional engineer.", "ref": "1926.652(b)(4), 1926.652(c)(4)"}, {"text": "Spoil piles, materials and equipment are kept at least 2 feet back from the edge or are restrained from falling in.", "ref": "1926.651(j)(2)"}, {"text": "A ladder, stairway or ramp is within 25 feet of lateral travel for every worker in a trench 4 feet or deeper.", "ref": "1926.651(c)(2)"}, {"text": "Trench shields are installed to restrict movement, extend above the vertical sides where used with sloping, and no one is inside while the shield is moved.", "ref": "1926.652(g)"}, {"text": "The atmosphere is tested before entry where oxygen deficiency or a hazardous atmosphere could exist (excavations over 4 feet near sewers, landfills or chemicals), and ventilation is used.", "ref": "1926.651(g)(1)"}, {"text": "Accumulating water is controlled with pumps or diversion, and no one works in an excavation where water is accumulating without precautions.", "ref": "1926.651(h)"}, {"text": "Sidewalks, pavements, poles, foundations and adjacent structures are supported where the excavation could undermine them.", "ref": "1926.651(i)"}, {"text": "Loose rock and soil are scaled or barricaded, no one works under suspended loads, and barricades, stop logs or a spotter keep equipment back from the edge.", "ref": "1926.651(j)(1), 1926.651(e), 1926.651(f)"}]}, {"heading": "Electrical and overhead lines", "items": [{"text": "All 120-volt receptacles used for tools and equipment that are not part of permanent wiring have GFCI protection, or an assured equipment grounding conductor program is followed.", "ref": "1926.404(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Extension cords are three-wire hard or extra-hard usage type (S, ST, SO), with intact insulation, no splices and ground prongs in place; damaged cords are removed.", "ref": "1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(J), 1926.416(e)"}, {"text": "Temporary lights have guards over the bulbs and are not suspended by their cords unless designed for it.", "ref": "1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(E), 1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(F)"}, {"text": "Panels, temporary power centers and disconnects have covers in place, unused openings closed and clear access.", "ref": "1926.403(i), 1926.405(b)"}, {"text": "Overhead and underground power circuits are located before work begins, and no one works where contact is possible unless the circuit is de-energized and grounded or guarded.", "ref": "1926.416(a)"}, {"text": "Excavators, dump trucks and aerial lifts stay at least 10 feet from lines up to 50 kV, farther for higher voltages, unless lines are de-energized and grounded or insulated.", "ref": "1926.600(a)(6)"}, {"text": "Crane work that could come within 20 feet of a power line proceeds only with the line de-energized and grounded, or with required clearance and a dedicated spotter.", "ref": "1926.1408(a), 1926.1408(b)"}, {"text": "Circuits and equipment being worked on are de-energized, locked out and tagged by qualified persons before work starts.", "ref": "1926.417"}, {"text": "Work on or near energized distribution lines is done only by qualified employees using minimum approach distances and tested rubber insulating equipment.", "ref": "1926.960, 1926.97"}, {"text": "Portable generators run outdoors away from doors, windows and trench openings, and the receptacles in use are GFCI protected.", "ref": "1926.404(b)(1), 1926.404(f)(3)"}]}, {"heading": "Cranes, hoists and rigging", "items": [{"text": "The crane operator is certified or qualified for the type and capacity of equipment being operated.", "ref": "1926.1427"}, {"text": "A competent person completes a shift inspection before use, and monthly and annual inspections are documented and available.", "ref": "1926.1412(d), 1926.1412(e), 1926.1412(f)"}, {"text": "Load charts and the operating manual are in the cab, and the load is within the rated capacity for the crane configuration and radius.", "ref": "1926.1417"}, {"text": "The crane is set up on firm, level ground with outriggers fully extended per the manufacturer and on blocking or pads.", "ref": "1926.1402"}, {"text": "A qualified signal person is used when the operator cannot see the load, the landing area or the travel path.", "ref": "1926.1419, 1926.1428"}, {"text": "The swing radius of the rotating superstructure is barricaded so no one can be struck or crushed.", "ref": "1926.1424(a)"}, {"text": "No one stands under a suspended load, loads never pass over people, tag lines are used, and a qualified rigger rigs loads when workers are in the fall zone.", "ref": "1926.1425"}, {"text": "Slings, shackles, hooks and other rigging are inspected before each shift by a competent person, marked with capacity, have working hook latches and are removed when damaged.", "ref": "1926.251(a)(1), 1926.251(a)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Heavy equipment and vehicles", "items": [{"text": "Equipment with an obstructed view to the rear has a working backup alarm audible above site noise, or a spotter directs every backing move.", "ref": "1926.602(a)(9)(ii), 1926.601(b)(4)"}, {"text": "Seat belts are installed and worn in vehicles and on equipment with rollover protective structures.", "ref": "1926.602(a)(2)(i), 1926.601(b)(10)"}, {"text": "Rollover protective structures are in place on dozers, loaders, graders, scrapers and tractors, and canopies protect operators from falling objects where needed.", "ref": "1926.1000"}, {"text": "Brakes, horn, lights, mirrors, wipers, steps and grab handles are in working order, and each vehicle is inspected at the start of the shift with defects corrected before use.", "ref": "1926.601(b)(14)"}, {"text": "Spotters wear high-visibility apparel, stay in the operator's view and out of the path of travel, and use agreed hand signals.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Parked equipment has buckets, blades and forks lowered to the ground, controls in neutral, parking brake set and wheels chocked on grades.", "ref": "1926.600(a)(3)(ii)"}, {"text": "No one rides in buckets, on loads, on fenders or on tailgates; workers are transported only in seats designed for passengers.", "ref": "1926.601(b)(9)"}, {"text": "Dump bodies are lowered before travel and raised only on firm, level ground clear of overhead lines, and raised bodies are blocked before anyone works under them.", "ref": "1926.601(b)(11), 1926.600(a)(6)"}, {"text": "Fueling is done with engines off and no smoking, and operators are trained and authorized for each machine they run.", "ref": "1926.152(g), 1926.21(b)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Hand and power tools", "items": [{"text": "Hand and power tools, whether owned by the employer or the worker, are in safe condition, and damaged tools are tagged and removed from service.", "ref": "1926.300(a), 1926.301(a)"}, {"text": "Guards on belts, gears, shafts, blades and other moving parts are in place and have not been removed, wedged or defeated.", "ref": "1926.300(b)"}, {"text": "Portable circular saws have upper guards and lower retracting guards that return automatically to the closed position.", "ref": "1926.304(d)"}, {"text": "Circular saws, chain saws and jackhammers have constant pressure switches that stop the tool when released; drills and larger grinders have momentary contact controls.", "ref": "1926.300(d)"}, {"text": "Grinding wheels are rated for the spindle speed, ring tested before mounting, and used with guards and work rests adjusted to the wheel.", "ref": "1926.303"}, {"text": "Pneumatic tools are secured to hoses by a positive means such as whip checks or clips, and compressed air for cleaning is reduced below 30 psi with chip guarding.", "ref": "1926.302(b)(1), 1926.302(b)(4)"}, {"text": "Powder-actuated tools are used only by trained and carded operators, tested before loading each day, and never left loaded or pointed at anyone.", "ref": "1926.302(e)"}, {"text": "Electric tools are double insulated or grounded, have working switches and intact cords, and are plugged into GFCI-protected circuits.", "ref": "1926.302(a)(1), 1926.404(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Chisels, drift pins and wedges have no mushroomed heads, wooden handles are tight and unsplit, and chainsaws have working chain brakes with operators wearing leg protection.", "ref": "1926.301(c), 1926.301(d), ANSI Z133"}]}, {"heading": "Personal protective equipment", "items": [{"text": "Hard hats meeting ANSI Z89.1 are worn wherever falling or flying objects or electrical contact are possible, with Class E hats near energized lines.", "ref": "1926.100"}, {"text": "Safety glasses with side shields, goggles or face shields are worn for grinding, chipping, cutting, sawing, concrete work, pressure washing and chemical handling.", "ref": "1926.102(a)"}, {"text": "Welders and helpers use filter lenses of the correct shade with face and eye protection, and screens shield nearby workers from the arc.", "ref": "1926.102, 1926.351(e)"}, {"text": "Hearing protection is worn around jackhammers, saws, compactors and other loud equipment where exposure exceeds permissible levels.", "ref": "1926.52, 1926.101"}, {"text": "Protective footwear meeting ASTM F2413 is worn, with waterproof boots for wet concrete and sewer work.", "ref": "1926.96"}, {"text": "Gloves matched to the hazard are used: cut resistant for sheet metal and glass, chemical resistant for concrete and solvents, and rubber insulating gloves for electrical work.", "ref": "1926.95(a), 1926.97"}, {"text": "Rubber insulating gloves and sleeves are air tested before each use and electrically retested on schedule, gloves every 6 months and sleeves every 12 months.", "ref": "1926.97(c)(2)"}, {"text": "Wet cutting or vacuum shrouds control silica dust when cutting, grinding or drilling concrete and masonry, and fit-tested workers wear respirators where Table 1 requires them.", "ref": "1926.1153(c), 1926.103"}, {"text": "Workers over or near water with a drowning hazard wear Coast Guard approved life jackets, and a ring buoy with 90 feet of line is within 200 feet.", "ref": "1926.106(a), 1926.106(c)"}]}, {"heading": "Hazard communication and flammables", "items": [{"text": "Safety data sheets for the chemicals on site (fuels, solvents, pipe primer and cement, epoxies, coatings, form release) are available at the site or by immediate electronic access.", "ref": "1926.59, 1910.1200(g)(8)"}, {"text": "Every container, including fuel cans, spray bottles and buckets, is labeled with the product identifier and hazard information.", "ref": "1910.1200(f)(6)"}, {"text": "Workers have been trained on the hazards of the chemicals they use, and contractors on site share label and SDS information with the crew.", "ref": "1910.1200(h), 1910.1200(e)(2)"}, {"text": "Flammable liquids are kept in approved safety cans of 5 gallons or less or in approved cabinets, with no more than 25 gallons stored outside a cabinet.", "ref": "1926.152(a)(1), 1926.152(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Storage cabinets are labeled Flammable Keep Fire Away and hold no more than 60 gallons of flammable or 120 gallons of combustible liquids.", "ref": "1926.152(b)(2), 1926.152(b)(3)"}, {"text": "Outdoor fuel storage and portable tanks are at least 20 feet from buildings, graded or diked to contain spills, and free of weeds and debris.", "ref": "1926.152(c)"}, {"text": "Fuel is transferred between containers only with the containers bonded, and No Smoking signs are posted at fueling and storage areas.", "ref": "1926.152(e)(2), 1926.152(g)"}, {"text": "Oxygen and fuel gas cylinders are upright, secured, capped when not in use, and separated by 20 feet or a 5 foot fire-rated barrier; propane is stored outdoors.", "ref": "1926.350(a), 1926.153(j)"}, {"text": "Asbestos cement pipe, pipe wrap, lead paint on structures and other regulated materials are identified before disturbance, and work follows the applicable standard.", "ref": "1926.1101, 1926.62"}]}, {"heading": "Fire protection and first aid", "items": [{"text": "A fire extinguisher rated at least 2A is provided for every 3,000 square feet of building under construction, with no more than 100 feet of travel to reach one.", "ref": "1926.150(c)(1)"}, {"text": "An extinguisher rated at least 10B is within 50 feet of any point where more than 5 gallons of flammable liquid or 5 pounds of flammable gas is used.", "ref": "1926.150(c)(1)(vi)"}, {"text": "Extinguishers are charged, mounted or staged where visible and accessible, and inspected monthly with the check recorded.", "ref": "1926.150(c)(1)(viii), 1926.150(a)(4)"}, {"text": "Hot work is done with combustibles moved 35 feet away or shielded, an extinguisher at hand and a fire watch during and after cutting or welding.", "ref": "1926.352"}, {"text": "Temporary heaters are stable, clear of tarps and combustibles, vented, and not used in enclosures without ventilation.", "ref": "1926.154"}, {"text": "A person with a current first aid certificate is on site when a clinic or hospital is not within 3 to 4 minutes for serious injuries.", "ref": "1926.50(c)"}, {"text": "First aid supplies are in a weatherproof container, checked weekly, with items individually sealed and replaced after use.", "ref": "1926.50(d)"}, {"text": "Emergency phone numbers and the site location or address are posted, and a phone or radio is available to summon an ambulance.", "ref": "1926.50(f), 1926.50(e)"}, {"text": "Eyewash or quick drenching facilities are available where eyes or skin can be exposed to corrosive materials such as batteries, concrete admixtures or chemicals.", "ref": "1926.50(g)"}]}, {"heading": "Confined spaces in construction", "items": [{"text": "A competent person has identified every confined space on the job (manholes, vaults, pipe, tanks, pits) and determined which are permit-required.", "ref": "1926.1203(a)"}, {"text": "Permit space entries are made only under a completed permit, with the entrant, attendant and entry supervisor trained for their roles.", "ref": "1926.1203(d), 1926.1207"}, {"text": "The atmosphere is tested for oxygen, then flammables, then toxic gases before entry and continuously during entry, using a calibrated and bump-tested monitor.", "ref": "1926.1204(e)"}, {"text": "Continuous forced air ventilation from a clean source is directed to the work area whenever an atmospheric hazard exists or could develop.", "ref": "1926.1203(e), 1926.1204(c)"}, {"text": "An attendant remains outside the space, keeps contact with entrants, monitors conditions and does not enter to attempt rescue.", "ref": "1926.1209"}, {"text": "A harness, tripod and winch are set up for non-entry retrieval on vertical entries deeper than 5 feet, and a rescue service able to respond is confirmed before entry.", "ref": "1926.1211"}, {"text": "The permit is posted at the entry point with hazards, test results and rescue contacts, and is canceled when the entry is complete.", "ref": "1926.1205, 1926.1206"}, {"text": "The host employer, controlling contractor and entry employer have exchanged information on the permit spaces and their hazards.", "ref": "1926.1203(h)"}, {"text": "Lines are isolated by lockout, blanking or blinding where needed, and barriers protect the opening from traffic and pedestrians.", "ref": "1926.1204(c)"}, {"text": "Sewer entries where flow cannot be isolated have an early warning system for rising water, and the weather forecast is checked before entry.", "ref": "1926.1204"}]}, {"heading": "Concrete, masonry and demolition", "items": [{"text": "No construction loads are placed on a concrete structure or formwork until a qualified person confirms it can support them.", "ref": "1926.701(a)"}, {"text": "Protruding rebar and other impalement hazards are guarded with impalement-rated caps, troughs or bent over where workers could fall onto them.", "ref": "1926.701(b)"}, {"text": "Formwork and shoring are braced and inspected, and forms are removed only after the concrete has reached the strength the plans require.", "ref": "1926.703(a), 1926.703(e)"}, {"text": "No one works under concrete buckets being raised or lowered, and powered trowels have a control that stops the blades when the operator lets go.", "ref": "1926.701(e), 1926.702(c)"}, {"text": "Masonry walls more than 8 feet high have a limited access zone and are braced until permanent supports are in place.", "ref": "1926.706"}, {"text": "Concrete workers have waterproof gloves, boots and eye protection for wet concrete, with eyewash available.", "ref": "1926.95, 1926.50(g)"}, {"text": "Before demolition starts, a competent person has completed an engineering survey of the structure and utilities are shut off, capped or controlled.", "ref": "1926.850(a), 1926.850(c)"}, {"text": "Asbestos, lead and other hazardous materials are identified and handled before demolition, floor openings are protected, and debris chutes are enclosed.", "ref": "1926.850(e), 1926.852, 1926.1101"}]}]}, {"slug": "general-industry-inspection", "title": "Facility and Shop Safety Inspection (General Industry)", "kind": "inspection", "industry": "general", "frequency": "Monthly for shops and plants, quarterly for other facilities", "intro": "Use this checklist for water and wastewater plants, pump and lift stations, fleet maintenance shops, sign shops, warehouses, salt domes and office buildings. Walk the whole facility, including yards and outbuildings, and check each item against what you see rather than what the procedure says should be there. Record deficiencies with a photo, an owner and a due date, and skip sections that do not apply to the facility.", "sections": [{"heading": "Walking-working surfaces and housekeeping", "items": [{"text": "Floors, aisles and passageways are clean, orderly, dry and free of cords, hoses, stored material and other tripping hazards.", "ref": "1910.22(a)"}, {"text": "Aisles and passageways give enough clearance for carts and lift trucks, and permanent aisles are marked.", "ref": "1910.176(a)"}, {"text": "Floor holes, pits, tanks, clarifiers and hatches are covered or guarded, and hatches are closed when not in use.", "ref": "1910.28(b)(3)"}, {"text": "Guardrails are 42 inches high, plus or minus 3 inches, with midrails, and toeboards are in place where objects could fall on people below.", "ref": "1910.29(b), 1910.29(k)"}, {"text": "Platforms, mezzanines, catwalks and loading docks 4 feet or more above a lower level have guardrails or other fall protection.", "ref": "1910.28(b)(1), 1910.28(b)(10)"}, {"text": "Stairs with 4 or more risers have handrails or stair rails, uniform treads and risers, slip resistant surfaces, and are kept clear.", "ref": "1910.28(b)(11), 1910.25"}, {"text": "Fixed ladders are in good condition, and ladders extending more than 24 feet have a ladder safety system or personal fall arrest system where required.", "ref": "1910.28(b)(9), 1910.23(d)"}, {"text": "Portable ladders are inspected before first use each shift, and ladders with broken rungs, split rails or missing feet are tagged and removed.", "ref": "1910.23(b)(9), 1910.23(b)(10)"}]}, {"heading": "Exits and emergency action plan", "items": [{"text": "Each area has at least two exit routes located far enough apart that one fire will not block both, unless the size and occupancy permit one.", "ref": "1910.36(b)"}, {"text": "Exit routes are at least 28 inches wide at every point and free of stored material, equipment, snow and locked or blocked doors.", "ref": "1910.36(g), 1910.37(a)(3)"}, {"text": "Exit doors open from the inside without keys, tools or special knowledge, and swing in the direction of travel where required.", "ref": "1910.36(d), 1910.36(e)"}, {"text": "Exits are marked with illuminated EXIT signs with letters at least 6 inches high, and doors that could be mistaken for exits are marked Not an Exit.", "ref": "1910.37(b)"}, {"text": "Exit routes are lit, emergency lighting works when tested, and the exit discharge outside is clear to a safe area.", "ref": "1910.37(b)(1), 1910.36(c)"}, {"text": "Fire doors are kept closed or held open only by automatic releases tied to the alarm, never propped with wedges.", "ref": "1910.36(a)(3)"}, {"text": "A written emergency action plan covers reporting, evacuation routes, critical operations, accounting for employees, rescue and medical duties, and contacts.", "ref": "1910.38(b), 1910.38(c)"}, {"text": "Employees were trained on the plan when hired and when duties or the plan changed, and the alarm has a distinctive signal that everyone recognizes.", "ref": "1910.38(e), 1910.165(b)"}]}, {"heading": "Fire extinguishers and fire prevention", "items": [{"text": "Extinguishers are mounted, identified with signs where needed, and not blocked by stored material or equipment.", "ref": "1910.157(c)(1)"}, {"text": "Extinguisher types match the hazards, with travel distance of 75 feet or less for Class A and 50 feet or less for Class B hazards.", "ref": "1910.157(d)"}, {"text": "Each extinguisher has been visually checked within the last month, and the check is recorded on the tag or in a log.", "ref": "1910.157(e)(2)"}, {"text": "Annual maintenance has been done on each extinguisher within the last 12 months and is recorded on the tag or a record kept one year.", "ref": "1910.157(e)(3)"}, {"text": "Hydrostatic test dates are current, 12 years for stored pressure dry chemical units and 5 years for water and carbon dioxide units.", "ref": "1910.157(f)"}, {"text": "Employees expected to use extinguishers were trained when hired and within the last year.", "ref": "1910.157(g)"}, {"text": "The written fire prevention plan lists the major fire hazards, storage and handling practices, ignition sources and the people responsible for controls.", "ref": "1910.39(c)"}, {"text": "Oily rags are in covered metal cans, and cardboard, pallets and other combustibles are kept away from heaters, panels and hot work.", "ref": "1910.39(c)(2)"}, {"text": "Sprinkler heads have 18 inches of clearance below them, control valves are open and supervised, and hot work is done under a permit with a fire watch.", "ref": "1910.159(c)(10), 1910.252(a)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Electrical", "items": [{"text": "Panels and disconnects have at least 36 inches of clear working space in front and 30 inches of width, and nothing is stored in that space.", "ref": "1910.303(g)(1)"}, {"text": "Panel covers are in place, breaker blanks fill unused slots, knockouts are closed, and each breaker and disconnect is legibly labeled.", "ref": "1910.305(b)(1)(ii), 1910.303(f)"}, {"text": "Flexible cords are not used in place of fixed wiring, run through walls, ceilings or doorways, under rugs, or fastened to building surfaces.", "ref": "1910.305(g)(1)(iv)"}, {"text": "Extension cords and tool cords have no cuts, splices or missing ground prongs, and damaged cords have been removed from service.", "ref": "1910.334(a)(2), 1910.305(g)(2)"}, {"text": "GFCI protection is provided for receptacles in wet and damp locations, outdoors, near sinks and in wet process areas.", "ref": "1910.304(b)(3), NFPA 70 210.8"}, {"text": "Receptacles, switches and junction boxes have cover plates, and there are no exposed conductors, open splices or taped repairs.", "ref": "1910.303(g)(2)(i), 1910.305(b)(2)"}, {"text": "Equipment in wet wells, digester areas and other classified locations is rated for that location.", "ref": "1910.307"}, {"text": "Electrical rooms and enclosures with exposed live parts are locked or restricted to qualified persons and marked with warning signs.", "ref": "1910.303(g)(2)"}, {"text": "Work on or near exposed energized parts is justified, done only by qualified persons, and uses arc rated clothing, voltage rated gloves and insulated tools.", "ref": "1910.333(a), 1910.335"}]}, {"heading": "Machine guarding and abrasive wheels", "items": [{"text": "Points of operation, nip points, rotating parts and sources of flying chips or sparks are guarded on saws, drill presses, lathes, presses and conveyors.", "ref": "1910.212(a)(1), 1910.212(a)(3)"}, {"text": "Guards are secured in place, do not create their own pinch points, and machines that could move in use are anchored.", "ref": "1910.212(a)(2), 1910.212(b)"}, {"text": "Fans within 7 feet of the floor have guards with openings no larger than one half inch.", "ref": "1910.212(a)(5)"}, {"text": "Belts, pulleys, chains, sprockets, shafts and couplings within 7 feet of the floor or platform are enclosed.", "ref": "1910.219"}, {"text": "Bench and pedestal grinders have work rests within one eighth inch of the wheel and tongue guards within one quarter inch.", "ref": "1910.215(a)(4), 1910.215(b)(9)"}, {"text": "Wheels are ring tested before mounting, rated speed is at or above spindle speed, and guards cover the spindle end, nut and flange.", "ref": "1910.215(d)(1), 1910.215(a)(2)"}, {"text": "Table saws have hood guards, spreaders and anti-kickback fingers, radial saws return to the rear, and band saw blades are enclosed except at the cut.", "ref": "1910.213"}, {"text": "Machines that could injure the operator on restart will not restart on their own after a power failure, and stop controls are within reach and marked.", "ref": "1910.213(b)(3)"}]}, {"heading": "Lockout/tagout", "items": [{"text": "A written energy control program is in place with equipment-specific procedures for pumps, blowers, conveyors, compactors, lifts, chemical feeders and other serviced equipment.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(1), 1910.147(c)(4)"}, {"text": "Each procedure identifies the energy sources (electrical, hydraulic, pneumatic, stored, gravity, chemical, water pressure) and the steps to shut down, isolate, lock, verify and restore.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(4)(ii)"}, {"text": "Locks, tags, hasps, breaker and valve lockout devices are available where work is done, are standardized, durable and identify the user.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(5)"}, {"text": "Each person working on equipment applies a personal lock, using group hasps or a lockbox when several people are involved.", "ref": "1910.147(d)(4), 1910.147(f)(3)"}, {"text": "Isolation is verified by trying to start the equipment or testing with a meter, and stored energy is relieved, blocked or bled before work begins.", "ref": "1910.147(d)(5), 1910.147(d)(6)"}, {"text": "Disconnects and valves are labeled, can accept a lock, and energy isolating devices installed or modified since 1990 are lockable.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(2)(iii)"}, {"text": "A periodic inspection of each procedure was done within the last 12 months by an authorized employee not using it, and is certified with date, equipment, employees and inspector.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(6)"}, {"text": "Authorized and affected employees were trained, retrained when jobs or procedures changed, and the training is documented.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(7)"}, {"text": "Outside contractors and facility staff have exchanged lockout procedures, and shift changes hand off locks without a gap in protection.", "ref": "1910.147(f)(2), 1910.147(f)(4)"}]}, {"heading": "Personal protective equipment and hazard assessment", "items": [{"text": "A written hazard assessment for each work area and job identifies the PPE required and is certified with the date, workplace and person who did it.", "ref": "1910.132(d)"}, {"text": "Required PPE is provided at no cost, available in the right sizes, and kept clean and in good repair.", "ref": "1910.132(h), 1910.132(a)"}, {"text": "Employees were trained on when PPE is needed, which PPE, how to wear and adjust it, and its limitations, and the training is documented.", "ref": "1910.132(f)"}, {"text": "Eye and face protection meeting ANSI Z87.1 is worn where flying particles, chemicals, molten metal or injurious light exist, including over prescription glasses.", "ref": "1910.133(a)"}, {"text": "Head protection meeting ANSI Z89.1 is worn where falling objects or electrical hazards exist, with Class E hats for electrical exposure.", "ref": "1910.135"}, {"text": "Gloves suited to the hazard (cuts, chemicals, heat, bloodborne pathogens, electrical) are selected and used, and protective footwear is worn where required.", "ref": "1910.138, 1910.136"}, {"text": "Hearing protection is provided where noise reaches 85 dBA over 8 hours and worn where required, with audiograms and training under the hearing conservation program.", "ref": "1910.95(i), 1910.95(c)"}, {"text": "Emergency eyewash and showers are within 10 seconds of corrosive chemical use (battery charging, chlorine, acids, caustics), unobstructed, flushed weekly and deliver tepid water.", "ref": "1910.151(c), ANSI Z358.1"}, {"text": "Rubber insulating gloves and sleeves are electrically retested on schedule, gloves every 6 months and sleeves every 12 months, and air tested before use.", "ref": "1910.137(c)(2)"}]}, {"heading": "Hazard communication", "items": [{"text": "A written hazard communication program with a current chemical inventory list is available at the facility.", "ref": "1910.1200(e)(1), 1910.1200(e)(4)"}, {"text": "A safety data sheet is readily available for every hazardous chemical in the work area on every shift, in a binder or electronically with a backup.", "ref": "1910.1200(g)(8)"}, {"text": "Every shipped container carries a label with product identifier, signal word, pictograms, hazard statements and supplier information.", "ref": "1910.1200(f)(1)"}, {"text": "Secondary containers such as spray bottles, jugs and parts washer tanks are labeled with the product identifier and hazard information; nothing is unlabeled.", "ref": "1910.1200(f)(6)"}, {"text": "Employees were trained at assignment and whenever a new hazard was introduced on the chemicals, labels, SDS and protective measures.", "ref": "1910.1200(h)"}, {"text": "The inventory matches what is on the shelves, and obsolete or unknown chemicals have been removed and disposed of properly.", "ref": "1910.1200(e)(1)(i)"}, {"text": "Non-routine tasks such as tank cleaning or pesticide mixing, and chemicals in unlabeled pipes, are covered in the program.", "ref": "1910.1200(e)(1)(ii), 1910.1200(e)(1)(iii)"}, {"text": "Contractors working on site know the chemical hazards and SDS location, and the facility knows what chemicals contractors bring in.", "ref": "1910.1200(e)(2)"}, {"text": "Incompatible chemicals (acids and bases, oxidizers and fuels, chlorine and ammonia, bleach and acid) are stored apart.", "ref": "NFPA 400"}]}, {"heading": "Flammable and compressed gas storage", "items": [{"text": "Flammable liquids outside an approved cabinet or storage room do not exceed 25 gallons of Category 1 or 120 gallons of Category 2, 3 or 4 per fire area.", "ref": "1910.106(e)(2)(ii)"}, {"text": "Storage cabinets are approved, labeled Flammable Keep Fire Away, kept closed, and hold no more than 60 gallons of flammable liquid.", "ref": "1910.106(d)(3)"}, {"text": "Drums of flammable liquid are bonded and grounded when dispensing, and liquids are never transferred by air pressure.", "ref": "1910.106(e)(6)(ii)"}, {"text": "Flammable storage areas have No Smoking signs, no ignition sources, ventilation, and electrical equipment rated for the location.", "ref": "1910.106(e)(6)(i), 1910.106(d)(4)"}, {"text": "Compressed gas cylinders are stored upright, secured with chains or straps, and have valve protection caps on when not in use.", "ref": "1910.101(b), CGA P-1"}, {"text": "Oxygen cylinders are separated from fuel gas cylinders and combustibles by 20 feet or a 5 foot barrier with a half hour fire rating.", "ref": "1910.253(b)(4)(iii)"}, {"text": "Cylinders are labeled with contents, empties are marked and kept apart, and storage is away from heat, exits, elevators and corrosive chemicals.", "ref": "1910.253(b)(2)"}, {"text": "Propane cylinders for forklifts and heaters are stored outdoors in a locked cage away from exits and ignition sources.", "ref": "1910.110(f)"}]}, {"heading": "Powered industrial trucks", "items": [{"text": "Each operator has been trained and evaluated on the type of truck used, with a record showing the name, training date, evaluation date and evaluator.", "ref": "1910.178(l)(6)"}, {"text": "Every operator has been evaluated within the last 3 years, and refresher training was given after any accident, near miss or unsafe operation.", "ref": "1910.178(l)(4)"}, {"text": "Trucks are examined before each shift, defects are reported and the truck is taken out of service until repaired; pre-use checklists are on file.", "ref": "1910.178(q)(7)"}, {"text": "Capacity plates are legible and list attachments, loads stay within rated capacity, and forks are free of cracks, bends and heel wear.", "ref": "1910.178(a)(3), 1910.178(a)(6)"}, {"text": "Operators wear seat belts, carry no riders, and park with forks lowered, controls neutral, power off and brakes set.", "ref": "1910.178(m)(3), 1910.178(m)(5)"}, {"text": "Battery charging areas have eyewash, ventilation, No Smoking signs, acid neutralizer and a means to keep trucks from running over chargers.", "ref": "1910.178(g)"}, {"text": "Propane cylinders are changed outdoors or in a designated area with the engine off, and the cylinder is secured with the relief valve up.", "ref": "1910.178(f)"}, {"text": "Trucks used indoors are rated for the location, and carbon monoxide from engine trucks is controlled with ventilation or monitoring.", "ref": "1910.178(c), 1910.1000"}, {"text": "Trailer wheels are chocked or dock locks engaged before entry, dock boards are secured, and overhead guards, horns and lights are in place and working.", "ref": "1910.178(k)(1), 1910.178(e)"}]}, {"heading": "Confined spaces", "items": [{"text": "The facility has evaluated its spaces and identified permit-required confined spaces such as wet wells, digesters, tanks, vaults, manholes and clearwells.", "ref": "1910.146(c)(1)"}, {"text": "Danger signs reading Permit-Required Confined Space Do Not Enter are posted at entrances, or employees are otherwise informed.", "ref": "1910.146(c)(2)"}, {"text": "A written permit space program is in place for spaces employees enter, and spaces not entered are secured against entry.", "ref": "1910.146(c)(3), 1910.146(c)(4)"}, {"text": "Canceled permits are kept for at least one year and were reviewed within the last 12 months to improve the program.", "ref": "1910.146(e)(6), 1910.146(d)(14)"}, {"text": "Gas monitors are calibrated per the manufacturer and bump tested before use, and tests are done for oxygen, then flammables, then toxic gases.", "ref": "1910.146(d)(5)"}, {"text": "Ventilation blowers, retrieval tripods and winches, harnesses, radios, lighting and barriers are available, inspected and in working order.", "ref": "1910.146(d)(4)"}, {"text": "Retrieval systems are used for vertical entries deeper than 5 feet unless they would increase the risk or not help rescue.", "ref": "1910.146(k)(3)"}, {"text": "The rescue service has been evaluated for response time and access to the spaces, and in-house rescuers practiced within the last 12 months.", "ref": "1910.146(k)(1), 1910.146(k)(2)"}, {"text": "Entrants, attendants, entry supervisors and rescuers are trained with certified records, and contractors entering spaces are briefed on the hazards.", "ref": "1910.146(g), 1910.146(c)(8)"}]}, {"heading": "Respiratory protection and air quality", "items": [{"text": "A written respiratory protection program with a named administrator covers all required respirator use, such as chlorine response, painting and dust exposure.", "ref": "1910.134(c)(1)"}, {"text": "Medical evaluations were completed before employees were fit tested or used a respirator.", "ref": "1910.134(e)"}, {"text": "Fit tests were done before first use and within the last 12 months for each tight-fitting respirator, with records of type, size, date and method.", "ref": "1910.134(f), 1910.134(m)(2)"}, {"text": "Respirators are cleaned after use and stored away from dust, sunlight, heat and chemicals so facepieces are not deformed.", "ref": "1910.134(h)"}, {"text": "Emergency escape respirators and SCBA are inspected monthly with the inspection recorded, cylinders are at least 90 percent full, and hydrostatic test dates are current.", "ref": "1910.134(h)(3)"}, {"text": "Employees who wear filtering facepieces voluntarily have been given the Appendix D information.", "ref": "1910.134(c)(2)"}, {"text": "Welding areas have local exhaust or enough general ventilation, and stainless or galvanized work has been evaluated for hexavalent chromium and metal fumes.", "ref": "1910.252(c), 1910.1026"}, {"text": "Vehicle exhaust is captured when engines run indoors, and carbon monoxide levels are kept below the permissible limit with monitoring where idling occurs.", "ref": "1910.1000"}]}, {"heading": "Chemical feed and treatment areas", "items": [{"text": "Chlorine gas rooms are separate from other areas, open outward to the outside, can be viewed through a window, and have ventilation and light switches outside the door.", "ref": "Ten States Standards 5.4.3"}, {"text": "Continuous leak detection for chlorine, ammonia, ozone or hydrogen sulfide is working, tested on schedule, and alarms at a staffed location.", "ref": "Ten States Standards 5.4.3"}, {"text": "Chlorine Institute emergency kits (A for 150 pound cylinders, B for ton containers) are on hand with trained staff, or a response contract is in place.", "ref": "Chlorine Institute Pamphlet 1"}, {"text": "SCBA, not cartridge respirators, is staged outside the chlorine room for emergency entry and inspected monthly.", "ref": "1910.134(d)(2), 1910.134(h)(3)"}, {"text": "Chlorine cylinders are secured, ton containers rest on trunnions, valve caps are on when not connected, and containers are out of direct sun and heat.", "ref": "1910.101(b), Chlorine Institute Pamphlet 1"}, {"text": "Bulk tanks for hypochlorite, ferric chloride, caustic, alum and polymer have secondary containment sized for the largest tank plus rain, in good condition.", "ref": "NFPA 400, 40 CFR 112"}, {"text": "Pipes, pumps and fill connections are labeled with contents and flow direction, and fill ports are locked and labeled to prevent mixing incompatible chemicals.", "ref": "ASME A13.1, 1910.1200(f)"}, {"text": "Chemical-specific PPE (face shield, goggles, apron, gloves rated for the chemical) is staged at each point of use, and eyewash is within 10 seconds.", "ref": "1910.132(d), 1910.151(c)"}, {"text": "NFPA 704 placards are posted at room entrances, and emergency contacts and shutdown steps are posted; PSM and RMP requirements are met where thresholds are exceeded.", "ref": "NFPA 704, 1910.119, 40 CFR 68"}]}, {"heading": "Ergonomics and material handling", "items": [{"text": "Hoists, lift tables, carts, drum dollies, pallet jacks and manhole cover lifters are available and used for heavy or awkward loads.", "ref": "OSH Act 5(a)(1)"}, {"text": "Heavy items are stored between knee and shoulder height, and frequently used items are at waist level.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Storage racks are anchored, loaded within posted capacity, free of damaged uprights, and material is stacked stable and limited in height.", "ref": "1910.176(b), ANSI MH16.1"}, {"text": "Bagged salt, chemicals and other sacked material are stacked with cross ties and stepped back, and drums are stored on racks or secured.", "ref": "1910.176(b)"}, {"text": "Computer, dispatch, SCADA and lab workstations allow neutral postures with adjustable chairs, monitors at eye level and keyboards at elbow height.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Hoists, chain falls and slings are marked with capacity, inspected on schedule, and hooks have working safety latches.", "ref": "1910.179, 1910.184(d)"}, {"text": "Jobs with repeated lifting, sustained force or vibration have been reviewed, and rotation, tool changes or aids are in place.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Employees are trained in lifting technique, team lifts and early reporting of strains and sprains.", "ref": ""}]}, {"heading": "Vehicles and yard", "items": [{"text": "The fuel island has a clearly marked emergency shutoff, an extinguisher within 75 feet, No Smoking and Stop Engine signs, and dispensers in good repair.", "ref": "1910.106(g), NFPA 30A"}, {"text": "Above ground and underground fuel tanks have containment, current leak detection and inspection records, and fill and vent points protected from vehicles.", "ref": "NFPA 30, 40 CFR 280"}, {"text": "Spill kits with absorbents, booms and drain covers are stocked near fuel, oil storage and wash areas, and used oil and antifreeze are labeled and contained.", "ref": "40 CFR 112, 40 CFR 279"}, {"text": "Yard lighting is adequate at the fuel island, parking, loading areas and walkways, and walkways are kept clear of snow and ice.", "ref": "1910.22(a)"}, {"text": "Backing areas have defined traffic patterns, mirrors or cameras where views are blocked, marked pedestrian walkways, and spotters for large vehicles.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Vehicle lifts were inspected within the last year, have rated capacity marked, locks are used at height, and arms and pads are in good condition.", "ref": "ANSI/ALI ALOIM"}, {"text": "Raised vehicles are blocked with jack stands before anyone works under them, wheels are chocked, and plows and attachments are lowered or blocked for service.", "ref": "1910.244(a)(2)(iii), 1910.147"}, {"text": "Truck and bus tires are serviced by trained employees using a restraining cage or device, with the rim charts posted.", "ref": "1910.177"}, {"text": "Drivers complete pre-trip inspections, defects are repaired before use, and inspection reports for commercial vehicles are kept on file.", "ref": "49 CFR 396.11, 49 CFR 396.13"}]}, {"heading": "First aid and medical", "items": [{"text": "First aid kits are stocked to ANSI/ISEA Z308.1, checked monthly, and expired or used items are replaced.", "ref": "1910.151(b), ANSI/ISEA Z308.1"}, {"text": "Trained first aid responders are on each shift when a clinic or hospital is not within a few minutes, and their first aid and CPR cards are current.", "ref": "1910.151(b)"}, {"text": "AEDs are in known locations, pads and batteries are within date, and monthly readiness checks are logged.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Bloodborne pathogen kits with gloves, CPR barriers, gowns, eye protection, biohazard bags and disinfectant are stocked for responders and cleanup.", "ref": "1910.1030(d)(3)"}, {"text": "Hepatitis B vaccination has been offered to employees with occupational exposure, and signed declinations are on file.", "ref": "1910.1030(f)(2)"}, {"text": "Sharps found in the field or in solid waste are handled with tongs and placed in sharps containers, and regulated waste is labeled and disposed of properly.", "ref": "1910.1030(d)(4)(iii), 1910.1030(g)(1)"}, {"text": "Employees know how to report an exposure incident immediately and how post-exposure evaluation is arranged.", "ref": "1910.1030(f)(3)"}, {"text": "Water, shade or warming areas, rest breaks and acclimatization are provided for work in heat or cold, and employees know the warning signs.", "ref": "OSH Act 5(a)(1)"}]}]}, {"slug": "annual-safety-program-audit", "title": "Annual Safety Program Audit", "kind": "audit", "industry": "program", "frequency": "Once a year, by the safety manager with division management", "intro": "This audit is a program-level review of whether the required written programs, training, inspections, records and follow-up are in place and working across the department. It is different from a site or facility inspection: instead of looking for hazards on the floor, you are checking documents, records and systems. Complete it with each division manager, note gaps and the owner for each, and carry the open items into next year's safety goals.", "sections": [{"heading": "Written programs", "items": [{"text": "The written hazard communication program is current, the chemical inventory was updated this year, and employees know where to find it.", "ref": "1910.1200(e)"}, {"text": "The respiratory protection program names an administrator and was evaluated for effectiveness within the year.", "ref": "1910.134(c), 1910.134(l)"}, {"text": "The energy control program has equipment-specific lockout procedures, and each procedure had its periodic inspection within the year.", "ref": "1910.147(c)(4), 1910.147(c)(6)"}, {"text": "The permit-required confined space program is current, with an up-to-date space inventory and a review of canceled permits within 12 months.", "ref": "1910.146(c), 1910.146(d)(14)"}, {"text": "The bloodborne pathogens exposure control plan was reviewed and updated within the year, including the exposure determination and sharps review.", "ref": "1910.1030(c)(1)(iv)"}, {"text": "The emergency action plan and fire prevention plan are in writing, current for each facility, and available to employees.", "ref": "1910.38, 1910.39"}, {"text": "The PPE hazard assessment is certified and has been updated for new tasks, chemicals or equipment added this year.", "ref": "1910.132(d)(2)"}, {"text": "A hearing conservation program covers every employee exposed at or above 85 dBA, where that exposure exists.", "ref": "1910.95(c)"}, {"text": "A workplace violence prevention policy is in place with a reporting procedure and training, and employees know it exists.", "ref": "OSH Act 5(a)(1)"}, {"text": "Each program names a responsible person, shows a review date within the last 12 months, and is accessible in print or electronically.", "ref": ""}]}, {"heading": "Training", "items": [{"text": "Annual safety training (hazard communication, bloodborne pathogens, PPE, emergency action, extinguishers, hearing) is complete for all employees and documented with names, dates and topics.", "ref": "1910.1200(h), 1910.1030(g)(2), 1910.157(g)"}, {"text": "New hires received safety orientation and job-specific training within their first weeks and before exposure to the hazards of the job.", "ref": "1910.132(f), 1910.1200(h)(1)"}, {"text": "Every forklift and powered industrial truck operator has an evaluation on file dated within the last 3 years.", "ref": "1910.178(l)(4)(iii)"}, {"text": "Every respirator user has a fit test dated within the last 12 months and a current medical clearance.", "ref": "1910.134(f)(2), 1910.134(e)"}, {"text": "Designated first aid responders at each facility and crew hold current first aid and CPR/AED certifications.", "ref": "1910.151(b)"}, {"text": "Each line worker completed a pole top rescue and a bucket truck rescue drill within the year, with the rescue time recorded.", "ref": "1910.269(b)(1), APPA Safety Manual"}, {"text": "Confined space entrants, attendants, entry supervisors and rescuers have current training, and rescue practice was held within 12 months.", "ref": "1910.146(g), 1910.146(k)(2)(iv)"}, {"text": "Competent persons for excavation, scaffolds and fall protection, qualified electrical workers, and certified flaggers are identified with current training.", "ref": "1926.651(k), 1926.503, 1910.332"}, {"text": "Operators of aerial lifts, cranes and digger derricks, chainsaws and mowers have documented equipment-specific training, and crane operators are certified.", "ref": "1910.67(c)(2), 1926.1427, ANSI Z133"}]}, {"heading": "Inspections", "items": [{"text": "Job site inspections were completed by supervisors on the required schedule, and the records are on file.", "ref": "1926.20(b)(2)"}, {"text": "Facility inspections were completed monthly or quarterly as required, and findings were closed within the target dates.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Fire extinguishers show monthly visual checks for every month and annual maintenance within the year.", "ref": "1910.157(e)"}, {"text": "Harnesses, lanyards and self-retracting lifelines were inspected by a competent person on the manufacturer's schedule, and ladders are inspected before use.", "ref": "1910.140(c)(18), 1926.502(d)(21), 1910.23(b)(9)"}, {"text": "Aerial lifts and bucket trucks had annual inspections and, for insulated booms, dielectric tests within the year.", "ref": "ANSI/SAIA A92.2, 1926.453"}, {"text": "Cranes, hoists and digger derricks have documented monthly and annual inspections, and load tests after major repair.", "ref": "1926.1412, 1910.179(j), 1910.180(d)"}, {"text": "Vehicle pre-trip inspections and driver vehicle inspection reports are completed, defects repaired, and reports kept at least 3 months.", "ref": "49 CFR 396.11, 49 CFR 396.13"}, {"text": "Slings and rigging hardware have periodic inspections documented, and damaged items were removed from service.", "ref": "1910.184(d), 1926.251(a)"}, {"text": "Eyewash stations are flushed weekly, AEDs checked monthly, and SCBA inspected monthly, each with a record.", "ref": "ANSI Z358.1, 1910.134(h)(3)"}]}, {"heading": "Incident management", "items": [{"text": "All injuries, illnesses, vehicle collisions, property damage and near misses were reported through the defined process within the required time.", "ref": "1904.35(b)(1)"}, {"text": "Each incident has a written investigation by the supervisor, with statements, photos and a timeline where appropriate.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Investigations identify root causes using a structured method rather than stopping at employee error.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Corrective actions have owners and due dates, are closed, and were verified to be effective after implementation.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Incident trends by division, injury type, body part, cause and time of day were reviewed at least quarterly and reported to management.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Lessons learned from incidents were shared across divisions through safety alerts or tailboard topics.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Near miss reporting is encouraged and nonpunitive, and near misses are investigated the same way as injuries.", "ref": "1904.36"}, {"text": "Fatalities were reported to OSHA within 8 hours and inpatient hospitalizations, amputations and losses of an eye within 24 hours, if any occurred.", "ref": "1904.39"}]}, {"heading": "Recordkeeping", "items": [{"text": "The OSHA 300 log is current, with each recordable case entered within 7 calendar days of learning of it.", "ref": "1904.29(b)(3)"}, {"text": "The OSHA 300A summary for last year was certified by a senior official and posted from February 1 through April 30.", "ref": "1904.32"}, {"text": "An OSHA 301 incident report or equivalent is complete for every recordable case.", "ref": "1904.29(b)(2)"}, {"text": "Electronic submission of injury data was completed by March 2 where the department is required to submit.", "ref": "1904.41"}, {"text": "Injury and illness records are retained for 5 years after the end of the year they cover, and privacy cases withhold the employee name.", "ref": "1904.33, 1904.29(b)(6)"}, {"text": "Training records and certificates for forklift, confined space, lockout, respirator, first aid, flagger and licenses are retained and retrievable by employee.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "The SDS library is current, and safety data sheets for discontinued chemicals or exposure records are kept 30 years.", "ref": "1910.1020(d)(1)(ii)"}, {"text": "Medical surveillance records (audiograms, respirator clearances, vaccinations, exposure monitoring) are kept for employment plus 30 years and stored confidentially.", "ref": "1910.1020(d)(1)(i), 1910.1030(h)"}, {"text": "Equipment inspection records for cranes, lifts, extinguishers and lockout periodic inspections are retained as the standards require.", "ref": ""}]}, {"heading": "Emergency preparedness", "items": [{"text": "An evacuation drill was held at each staffed facility within the year, and drills for severe weather or shelter in place were documented with observations.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Evacuation maps are posted at each facility showing primary and secondary routes, assembly points, extinguisher and AED locations.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Fire alarms, employee alarms, chlorine leak alarms and weather warning systems were tested on schedule and employees recognize each signal.", "ref": "1910.165(d)"}, {"text": "AEDs were inspected monthly with pads and batteries in date, and first aid kits are stocked and checked monthly.", "ref": "1910.151(b)"}, {"text": "A severe weather plan identifies shelter areas, covers flooding at lift stations and plants, and gets warnings to crews in the field.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Chemical release plans for chlorine, ammonia and other hazardous materials are coordinated with the fire department, and exercises were held where RMP applies.", "ref": "1910.120(q), 40 CFR 68.95"}, {"text": "Emergency contact lists, call trees and after-hours numbers are current, and critical shutoffs are documented for each facility.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Emergency lights were tested monthly and for 90 minutes annually, and backup power for critical facilities was exercised.", "ref": "NFPA 101 7.9.3"}]}, {"heading": "Contractors and the public", "items": [{"text": "Contractor safety requirements (insurance, safety program, training, site rules) are written into contracts and verified before work starts.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Contractors received orientation on site hazards, permit spaces, chemicals, lockout and emergency procedures, and the information exchange is documented.", "ref": "1910.146(c)(8), 1926.1203(h), 1910.147(f)(2)"}, {"text": "Contractor work is monitored, staff have authority to stop unsafe work, and contractor incidents are reported to the department.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Traffic control plans are prepared for work on or near roadways, follow MUTCD Part 6 and state DOT standards, and flaggers are certified.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6"}, {"text": "The public is protected at job sites with barricades, fencing, covered or backfilled excavations at the end of the shift, accessible pedestrian detours and lighting.", "ref": "MUTCD Part 6, 1926.651"}, {"text": "Utility locates are requested through 811 before every excavation by staff or contractors, and the department responds to locate requests on time.", "ref": "1926.651(b)"}, {"text": "Facility visitors and tour groups sign in, receive PPE and an escort, and chemical rooms and electrical rooms are kept locked.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Work near schools, parks and events has a plan for separating the public from equipment and open work.", "ref": ""}]}, {"heading": "Management and employee involvement", "items": [{"text": "A safety committee with management and employee members met at least quarterly, with minutes, action items and follow-up on file.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Tailboard and toolbox talks are held and documented with topic, date and attendance, daily for field crews and at least weekly for shops.", "ref": "1910.269(c)"}, {"text": "The hazard reporting path works: employees know how to report, reports are acknowledged, acted on, and the reporter gets feedback.", "ref": "1904.35(b)(1)(i)"}, {"text": "Employees can report injuries and hazards without discipline or retaliation, and the policy says so in writing.", "ref": "1904.36, OSH Act 11(c)"}, {"text": "Management reviewed the safety program this year using audit, inspection, incident and training data, and shared the results.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Safety goals and leading indicators for next year are set with owners, measurable targets and budget.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Supervisors have safety responsibilities in their job descriptions and performance reviews, and good safety performance is recognized.", "ref": ""}, {"text": "Each division has a designated safety representative with the time and training to do the role.", "ref": ""}]}]}], "pass_percent": 80, "app_name": "IntelliSafe", "company": "Tompkins Software Systems LLC", "brand_company": "IntelliSafe Systems LLC", "license_line": "Licensed software by IntelliSafe Systems LLC. Developed by Tompkins Software Systems LLC", "year": 2026, "repo": "https://github.com/Tompkins52/Safety-Training"};
